Munich 1856: Four maps thanging your view of the city
An insane institution, where young people are supposed to meet today.
A tomb chapel, which is now being dug for the S-Bahn tunnel.
Allotment gardeners cultivate the former land of farmers.
The oldest official map in Munich promotes exciting stories.
It's annoying when maps aren't up-to-date.
This is familiar to anyone who has ever had to get upset, because the Auto-Navi indicated a green meadow instead of a bypasse.
The historical maps of the digital Bavaria Atlas, an offer of the geoportal Bavaria of the state government, are anything but up-to-date - but this is precisely why very revealing.
Especially if you compare them to current online maps.
Then it becomes clear how cities and municipalities in the distribution area of the Munich Mercury have changed since the 19th century.
The Digital Bayern Atlas is a patchwork that you do not immediately recognize as such.
More detailed maps, so-called primordial position sheets, have been incorporated into the large overview map for cities and municipalities.
According to the Bavarian Surveying Administration, these originated between 1810 and 1864 and originated from Bavaria’s first survey.
Your client: Napoleon.
The French army leader occupied Bavaria since the spring of 1800 during the Second Coalition War with Austria.
Napoleon wanted an overview of the occupied country and commissioned a topographical recording of Bavaria - for military purposes, of course.
Even if the French troops finally withdrew with the peace of Lunéville of 9 February 1801: it was today's neighbors who created the idea of a nationwide Bavaria map.
Electorate Max IV Joseph continued to advance the project, founded the "Topographische Bureau" - and thus the Bavarian Surveying Administration.
The primordial position for Munich dates from 1856 and, like everyone else, can be found on the website of the Bayerische Landesbibliothek.
The comparison earlier/today is most impressive using the example of the state capital.
The city has expanded in all directions and has grown together.
An example of this is Untersendling, which still appears relatively isolated in 1856.
Sendling was incorporated 21 years after the creation of the map, i.e. 1877.
Today Untersendling is located in the middle of the city.
Untersendling’s houses concentrated around 1856 along today’s Plinganserstraße, which was only named in 1878 after Georg Sebastian Plinganser, a leader of the uprising of the Bavarian peasants against Austria in 1705.
Where the Westpark is today, one sees nothing but fields - at first glance: When zooming in, a smaller map with dozens of lines and numbers appears: handwritten numbered pieces of land from the so-called "primordial recording", from which the property tax was derived.
Today, small parcels no longer occupy farmers there - but allotment gardeners of the "Land in Sonne" plant.
A large green area stands out north of Untersendling.
The Theresienwiese, since 1810 the scene of the Oktoberfest, was actually still a meadow in the 19th century - and not a concrete desert.
On the other side of the Isar, the historical map gives an insight into the history of psychiatric medicine.
In the Urpositionsblatt you can read in the area of today's Au-Haidhausen not only the street name "am Gasteig" or from the hill "Auf der Lüften" - but also in large letters "Irren Anstalt".
Yes, here on Auerfeldstraße, people with delusions were once imprisoned.
In 1858/59, the "Auf der Auer Lüften" district was founded there in 1858/59.
From 1872, Dr. Bernhard von Gudden headed the institution - the psychiatrist who is brought into connection with the death of King Ludwig II.
“He was progressive for his time and, for example, ended the chaining of so-called lunatics.
In addition, he has forbidden the staff to use violence against the inmates," says Hermann Wilhelm, head of the Haidhausen Museum, who has dealt with the institution for an exhibition.
There was initially room for 280 patients and later expanded to 500 places.
At some point, this capacity was no longer sufficient - the reason for the construction of the district asylum in Haair 1905.
Since 2006, the Isar-Amper-Klinikum München-Ost, one of Germany’s largest psychiatries, has been located there.
On the site of the former Auer Anstalt, young people now meet in the Munich Salesianum.
The order acquired the building complex in 1920 and converted it into a youth home with training companies.
Western is the football field of the TSV München Ost, east residential houses from the 1980s.
In 2015, the children's house "Casa Don Bosco" was erected - architecturally inspired by the original building of the insane asylum, which had been destroyed in bomb attacks during World War II.
The Marienplatz was listed around 1856 as "main square", the Kaufingerstraße was called "Kaufinger Gasse".
The Second World War also brought a decisive change in the heart of Munich.
In December 1944, the bombs of the Allies tore many holes in the city centre.
A big one remained to this day - at the Marienhof.
The English Institute, small commercial buildings and a tomb chapel from the Medievals once stood at the Marienhof, where preparatory work will take place these days and on April 5, 2017 the ground-breaking ceremony for the second S-Bahn main line.
With the slider, the old Munich map can be compared with today's aerial photograph - and the hole at Marienhof can be replenished.
Civil rights group publishes travel warning for Missouri
The National Association for the promotion of people of color has issued a warning for people of color who travel to Missouri because of the discriminatory policies and racist attacks of the state.
"NAACP Travel Advisory for the State of Missouri, which is due to take effect until August 28, 2017, calls on African-American travelers, visitors and Missourians to pay special attention when traveling through the state and to exercise extreme caution as a number of questionable, rass-based incidents have recently occurred throughout the state and noted here," the group's statement said.
A recently passed Missouri law that makes it harder for people to win discrimination lawsuits, as well as the state's law enforcement agencies, disproportionately targeting minorities, prompted the group to issue the travel warning, the NAACP said.
“They have violations of civil rights that happen to people.
They are stopped because of their skin color, they are beaten or killed," Missouri NAACP's president, Rod Chapel, told the Kansas City Star.
"We are hearing complaints at such a frequency that we have not heard before."
It's the first such warning the organization has issued for a state in the U.S.
The group cited incidents such as racist offenses against black students at the University of Missouri and the death of Tory Sanders, 28, a black man from Tennessee.
Sanders died in questionable circumstances earlier this year after he no longer had gasoline while traveling through the state and was taken into custody by Missouri police without being accused of a crime.
The council also points to a recent report from the Missouri Attorney General's office, which shows that black motorists in the state are 75 percent more likely to be advertised.
"The recommendation is that people are aware aware and inform their families, friends and staff about what could happen in Missouri," Chapel said.
"People need to be ready, whether it's bringing bail money or letting relatives know they're travelling through the state."
Missouri recorded 100 hate crimes in 2015, according to the latest figures from the FBI's hate-crime reporting program, which ranks the state to the 16th place in the country in terms of number of such violations.
The travel warning is also a response to a new Missouri law that would make it harder to sue a company for housing or employment discrimination.
Earlier, the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) issued travel warnings for Texas and Arizona after states passed immigration laws requiring local law enforcement authorities to arrest people for immigration violations, which the ACLU believes will contribute to further racial profiling.
Travel warnings are usually issued by the State Department for other countries, but lately stakeholders have deployed the measure in response to certain laws and trends within the U.S.
Pasture fence project is elementary
The Fischerbach pasture fence project is a successful project and will continue next year.
CDU members of parliament Peter Weiß and Kordula Kovac have gained a picture on the spot.
Representatives of the Fischerbacher CDU local association met together with Mayor Thomas Schneider and the two deputies at the Rechtgrabenhof of the Schwendemann family.
Markus Schwendemann initially provided insights into his business, where he manages 55 hectares of meadows and 40 hectares of forest, supplies 40 dairy cows and just as many juveniles and accommodates holiday guests in five apartments.
The "Schwarzwaldmilch" is for him the alternative marketing route for the milk of his cows, according to Schwendemann.
On the table of the holiday guests comes exclusively regional.
Since he is also BLHV chairman of Fischerbach, he described the pasture fence project as "elementally important".
I can't manage profitably on such steep areas.
The grassland area provides for a lot of work, much had to be processed with the motor scythe.
"We are struggling here with the conditions and with the topography," Schwendemann emphasized.
In 2003, he took over the parental farm and managed it without chemical crop protection and fertilizer, even if it did not run an organic farm.
It was clear to Mayor Thomas Schneider: "The municipality has recognized the importance of the pasture fence project and is happy about the leader funding".
At a cost of 3000 to 4000 euros per hectare, the farmers would have to be supported.
It is hoped that the reforestation of the areas could be pulled out for quite a while.
"The greatest treasure we have is the people who work in agriculture and worry about how to move their operations further," the mayor stressed.
Farmers are often under criticism because of
In 2013, a total of 25 plots of eleven participants were fenced through pasture fence funding.
The open-housing area is thus 40 hectares, which may not be reforested until at least 2023.
Of the total cost of €71 500, half was funded by Leader, and the municipality of Fischerbach contributed a quarter.
Thus, 12 610 euros remained to the farmers, which they had to invest in 16.2 kilometers of pasture fence.
"The big advantage is the longevity of the fences," Schwendemann explained.
Kordula Kovac emphasized: "You are doing a really good job without farmers and rural women".
For Peter Weiß it was also clear: "The best food is produced here".
The Landscape Care Directive (LPR) Baden-Württemberg enables the state funding of pasture fences.
The aim is to keep the Black Forest open.
Prerequisite is the presence of a funding backdrop designated by the LPR.
In addition, the fenced area must not be trawl manageable (slopes above 35 percent) and must thus be threatened by reforestation.
Eight participants with 24 plots who would like to build almost 16 kilometres of fence are currently underway for the Fischerbach pasture fence project 2018.
The open-housing area corresponds to 35 hectares, the calculated costs are more than 112 000 euros.
Isn't there nothing more sacred?
Now Trump's White House takes care of the Statue of Liberty
The safest sign of a regime change is when they start attacking the statues.
The Americans appreciate these as much as anyone - hence the carefully staged overthrow of Saddam Hussein on Firdo's square in Baghdad in 2003.
Stephen Miller, one of the Trump regime's key ideologues, certainly knew what he was doing when he took a symbolic axe in a heated dispute with CNN's Jim Acosta about the president's proposals to drastically restrict legal immigration to the Statue of Liberty.
To stay fair, Miller didn't attack the statue itself.
A horde of boat owners and Liberty impersonators would have lynched him if he had.
But he attacked its importance, and in particular the meaning attributed to her when the famous poem by Emma Lazarus was added to his base in 1903, 17 years after the completion of the monument.
When Miller insulted Acosta: "I don't want to go down to a complete thing about history here, but the Statue of Liberty is ... a symbol of American freedom that illuminates the world.
The poem you are referring to, which was later added and is not part of the original Statue of Liberty."
Miller is factually correct, but as he put it himself, it's not really about history.
It's about the contemporary response to Lazaru's uplifting words, the onlys in which a state invites not only any old immigrants, but the poorest of the poor: "Your tired, your poor,/your afflicted masses who yearn to breathe freely,/the miserbrace of your rich shores."
There was never a time when the "miserabrish waste" on our screens was better seen.
Miller didn't engage in literary criticism - he made it clear that these people aren't welcome in Trump's U.S.
This conflict because of the significance of a statue is part of a broader political and cultural confrontation: it is indeed a conflict over the importance of America.
Lazarus - and her friends who campaigned to place their words at the grassroots after their deaths - knew very well that they were performing a highly political action.
The sculpture was intended to show the links between French and American republicanism by depicting the feminine embodiment of freedom in a worn-out classical pictorial depiction.
Lazarus changed this meaning: In her poem, the female figure is no longer abstract - she has received a voice.
And she gives herself a very different name: mother of exiles.
The poem's connection with the image is a brilliant feminist coup and a devastating attack on American nativism.
And just as Lazarus changed the meaning of the statue, people from the right wing have long wanted to change this back.
The great screamer Rush Limbaugh, a kind of John the Baptist for the coming of Trump, argued in 2010 that the Statue of Liberty did not invite anyone, but rather an early neoconservative that would bring the American flame into the nightly world: "Lady Liberty is moving forward.
It is said to carry the torch of freedom from the United States to the rest of the world.
The torch should not enlighten the path to the United States."
Quite uncommodating, Limbaugh has encountered something here.
The significance of the statue is entirely a matter of the point of view of perception.
Lazaru's great imaginative deed was to see them as she was seen by the exhausted, miserable, but hopeful people after long and often terrible journeys on the decks of ships.
It's literally about seeing the world from the perspective of people seeking refuge and an opportunity to improve their lives.
Their statue speaks to them the words of hope and welcome that they must hear.
And that's not, of course, the perspective Miller and Trump ever want to acknowledge.
Their torch should not illuminate the way, but ignite the hatred, which is their only source of power.
World Cup 2017: Neil Black praises Scottish members of UK team
Eilidh Doyle recalls a time when only two Scots belonged to Britain's team
The UK's athletics director Neil Black says the 16-strong Scottish contingent in the World Cup squad "will make a massive contribution to the team."
A record number of Scottish athletes have been selected for London 2017, which kicks off on Friday.
Black believes "something special is developing in Scotland" and the UK's athletics needs to learn something from it.
"We welcome it, and we're trying to understand it, and we're going to carry it on until we find out," Black said.
"I'm talking to people at the Scottish Institute of Sport and they think it has something to do with what they've done.
The Scottish athletes think it has something to do with what they've done.
British athletics: It has something to do with what they've done.
And also with the guy on the street corner.
Whatever the combination of circumstances, it's absolutely brilliant.'
Several of Scotland's athletes are medal contenders, including Laura Muir and Andrew Butchart - who will compete against Sir Mo Farah on the 5,000 m track, while Eilidh Doyle has been chosen by the squad as team captain.
“Eilidh is quite modest.
Their captain's speech was unbelievable," Black says.
“A number of athletes, whether they were Scottish or wherever they live or train, went out and felt very proud and very motivated.
The passion and real feeling that Eilidh earned quite naturally was great.”
The Commonwealth silver medalist, who will compete at her fifth world championships at the 400m hurdles, says it's "unbelievable" that 16 Scots are in the British squad.
Scotland's best toast number of athletes so far at the World Cup has been seven, achieved in 1983 and 2015.
The middle-distance runner Muir leads on this path and set five British and two European records last year.
It will take part in the 1,500 m and the 5,000 m runs.
Chris O'Hare, who runs 1,500 m, is another Scot to watch after a great season involving victory at the British Team Trials and the Anniversary Games.
Doyle remembers her first world championships when the only other Scot in the team was Lee McConnell.
The squad captain says she's "enormously proud" of the fact that many of her compatriots not only belong to the team, but in some cases will also be involved in the medals and finals.
"What's special about being team captain is because she's been chosen by the other members of the team, so it's been a great honour anyway, but knowing my teammates voted for me and voted for me makes it very special for me," she added.
“Obviously I’m very proud of all the Scots who belong to the team.
It just shows how far we've come.
And they're here as established athletes, athletes who want to step up on podiums.
As for my speech as captain?
Yes, I think she went well.
I've just pointed out we're very happy in this situation as we have a home World Cup.
So it's really important to rely on that home support and benefit from the fact that we're back at the Olympic Stadium.
“I’m getting a second chance to go out there and it’ll just be such a great atmosphere out there.”
Blocizing critics via Facebook: Don't apply for public office if you can't tolerate the heating-up of voters
Our country's father knew something about bad press.
Americans loved George Washington, but it didn't take long for the newspapers to beat him because of everything, from domestic politics to his political principles.
He scolded the criticism, for sure.
But he didn't silence his critics.
Because in 1783 Washington said: "Freedom of speech can be taken away - and, stupid and dumb, like the sheeps, are led to slaughter."
This takes me to Maryland to Gov. Larry Hogan, who has to work to be more like Washington.
Hogan's employees have blocked and deleted the posts of at least 450 people who voiced their opinion on his official Facebook page.
And the American Civil Liberties Union sued him earlier this week.
The governor's staff dismissed the lawsuit as frivolous, and the online comment was full of a backlash with "Well, and?"
"It's just Facebook," many people said.
But it's important.
And it's especially important if it's a guy like Hogan.
This is a republican governor in an overwhelmingly Democratic state that is astonishingly popular.
He has the second-highest endorsement of the nation's 50 governors.
Hogan isn't a reactionary hothead.
He has shown a firm hand in the leadership of his state and strict adherence to principles.
He was also very adept at using Facebook as the primary means to connect with his constituents and playfully debut his hairless head after chemotherapy on his side.
Therefore, the blocking of individuals who come to the side of the governor - which is a public forum that is described as official and by employees who are paided with public tax dollars - is unnecessary and ultimately dangerous.
In an interview with The Washington Post, Hogan's spokeswoman Amelia Chasse defended the governor's actions, arguing that blocking the comments was nothing more than a moderation.
But it's too easy to use the image of trolls or spammers or hateful people swinging the whiplash online.
The Post spoke to some of the real people blocked by Hogan.
And these are just real people talking to their chosen leaders: a teacher, an entrepreneur and a pastor, not trolls.
They all said their comments were respectful, thoughtful and not profane.
The pastor quoted the Bible in his post and appealed to Hogan's Catholic faith.
Lakshmi Sarma Ramani of Bowie, Md., wasn't hateful, but she asked for hate crimes.
"I politely commented that I was disappointed that there was no response to hate crimes and other current news," she wrote in the comments section of the "The Post" news program.
“I also don’t appreciate this idea, if a number of people take a stand on the same issue, then they are disregarded by some rather as a so-called collective effort and not recognized as a large group of affected citizens.”
What the governor's staff described as a "concentrated spam attack," others would likely call "advocacy."
The Facebook era makes it easy to crop a message by simply blocking a critic or deleting a negative comment.
It's much cleaner than in earlier times if you've done the same, that employees have been dispatched to collect and burn newspapers with critical editorials or arrest and silence protesters.
But that's what's happening digitally right now.
Hogan isn't the first public official to be criticized for insulting Facebook and other social media.
President Trump is being sued by Twitter users who have been blocked by his Twitter feed.
One of the first milestones on this issue fell last week in Virginia.
Loudoun County's board of board of supervisors violated the First Amendment, according to U.S. District Judge James C. Cacheris in Alexandria, when she banned a voter from her Facebook page.
And in Kentucky, Governor Matt Bevin (R) also received a visit from ACLU because of its use of Facebook and Twitter.
That shouldn't be that hard.
At the time of Washington, the period of affordable postage had a similar effect as the Internet.
The number of newspapers multiplied between 1776 and 1800, and anonymous letter writers slammed on his leadership.
And even then, Washington had anonymous trolls.
The people who used pseudonyms like "Juricola," "Valerius," "Belisarius" and "Portius," wrote all the letters to newspapers taking down Washington's decisions.
Petitions criticizing his position on Amity's treaty, commerce and shipping with Britain overwhelmed his office, according to the historical documents collected by the George Washington Project's online papers.
But he didn't silence them.
Freedom of speech, disagreement and discourses are part of our nation's basis.
And true leadership means accepting that.
Ronan Rafferty hopes for domestic comfort at Renaissance Club
Former European #1 Ronan Rafferty will hope for domestic comfort over the next three days as the Renaissance Club in East Lothian hosts the 25th edition of the Scottish Senior Open.
The Nordire, who lead next down at Archerfield links to the finals before losing to Paul Eales last year, is associated with the neighbouring venue and acting as tournament ambassador on behalf of the club's founder and CEO Jerry Sarvadi.
"I've seen how the Renaissance club was built, and we'll see it achieving its fame with this event," Rafferty said.
"The players will see this course at his best, he's in great condition.
Jerry did a great job when he fixed this place.
He's an educative champion of this game and is proud of his performance here.
This is a great showcase for this course.”
Englishman Eales is looking forward to defending a championship for the first time, although he has won in his 32-year career at both the European Tour and the European Challenge Tour.
"That feels really special," said the Southport-based player.
“I couldn’t defend the Extremadura Open as it was taken out of the programme in the year after I had won it.
Coming back to that part of the world is magical.
It's a wonderful place to play golf.
The memories from last year are coming back, and it's been something special for me and my wife Sharon to win here last year.
It's been a really special time and one that we'll always appreciate."
Gary Orr, who joined the European Senior Tour after his 50th birthday earlier this year, is returning to action on home soil.
This event will be his first Scottish appearance since the Aberdeen Asset Management Scottish Open in 2013.
"It always means a bit more when you're playing at home," said Orr, the man from Helensburgh.
“You want to do well, but you’re also feeling that extra pressure.
I really enjoyed playing again, and I played solid golf.
I'm happy with how it's gone so far.'
With the trio on the field are former Masters winner Ian Woosnam and senior major champions Paul Broadhurst, Roger Chapman, Mark James and Mark McNulty.
Orr and former Ryder Cup captain Sam Torrance are among the six Scots on the field, the others being Andrew Oldcorn, Stephen McAllister, Bill Longmuir and Ross Drummond.
Admission is free, the parking space costs £5 per car with one occupant and £10 per car with two or more occupants.
All proceeds will go to Prostate Cancer UK.
Fewer lung cancer deaths due to passive smoking
In the past two decades, fewer and fewer people have died of lung cancer because of passive smoking.
Scientists from the University Hospital Hamburg-Eppendorf (UKE) came to this conclusion in a study.
"According to our estimates, 167 lung cancer deaths per year are attributable to passive smoking," study leader Heiko Becher said.
As a comparison, he used his own 1994 study on this subject.
At that time, there were still 400 lung cancer deaths due to passive smoking per year in Germany.
The new investigation is published in the "International Journal of Public Health."
House connections turn out to be very complex
The construction work on Villinger Straße is in full swing and "lies reasonably in time," according to the head of the building yard Fred Heinze.
In the area of house connections, the situation is more complicated than assumed, since there is a lot hidden in the underground, which is not noted in the plans.
The traffic, which repeatedly hinders the construction work, poses a major problem for the construction squad of Stumpp.
"The ignorance of motorists from red traffic lights and barrier grids is already enormous," reports Heinze.
The road is closed and only the scheduled buses are entitled to drive through.
Angelo Caruso, Marvin Kostorz and Tim Kostorz from Stumpp have been working on the bridge for weeks.
“The hot days have been quite exhausting.
But fortunately we were supplied with cool mineral water by our friend Fritz Matt," says Marvin Kostorz.
Poverty and death in the Indonesian gold country
When Bardina Degei cookes dinner, she doesn't use a stove.
She rarely uses a pot.
In her wooden house in Enarotali, the capital of Paniai regency in the troubled Indonesian province of Papua, the housewife usually puts a sweet potato - locally referred to as "nota" - directly into the fireplace.
After half an hour, the charred tuber is removed with zealous, unwashed hands and engulfed.
Degei sits on the clay floor - she has no furniture - where she also carries out her daily work, such as washing clothes with murky water from the nearby swamp.
A bucket in a homeless room serves as a latrine.
As the youngest of her husband's four wives, no fields were allocated to her.
Polygamy is common here.
Of course, working late can be dangerous: most village men are unemployed, and many drink heavily, and there are the soldiers here.
"No one dares to walk around the village after 5pm," she says.
It's a rare glimpse into daily life in the highlands of Papua, a former Dutch colony taken up in Indonesia in 1969 after a disputed referendum, when only 1,026 elders were supposed to vote through a public hand survey in front of the occupation forces.
An existing movement stirring for independence against Dutch rule quickly turned against Jakarta's government, which maintains control of the region and excludes foreign journalists or legal observers.
In 2003, the province was officially divided into Papua and West Papua, with the independent part of Papua New Guinea comprising the eastern part of the island.
Enarotali is as secluded as it's desolate. The trip includes a 90-minute flight from the provincial capital Jayapura to Nabire and then a morning 5-hour drive by rental car.
There's no public transportation.
The city with about 19,000 inhabitants consists of wooden houses surrounded by bamboo fences, and corrugated metal roofs that have been converted by rust into various yellow-brown tones.
Very few Indonesians have made their trip here, let alone journalists and virtually no foreigners.
Before Christian missionaries arrived, Mee Pago Papuans revered a god named Uga Tamee.
There were other changes as well.
"We weren't used to wearing these clothes," Degei says, pointing to her vibrant, hand-woven turban, her dark shirt and light skirt.
“We used to only have leaves on our bodies.”
Papua is Indonesia's poorest province, where 28% of people live below the poverty line and have some of the worst infant mortality and literacy rates in Asia.
But it's also Indonesia's gold country.
The world's largest and most profitable gold mine, Grasberg, owned by Freeport McMoran from Phoenix, is located just 60 miles from Paniai, a highland province the size of New Jersey with 153,000 residents.
In 2015, Freeport grossed $3.1 billion worth of gold and copper.
In addition, Papua has an estimated €78 billion worth wood resources.
These riches, however, are a source of misery for the Papua, which ensure that Indonesia's mighty military preserves a suffocating presence.
A 2005 investigation into the New York Times reported that Freeport paid local military personnel and units nearly $20 million between 1998 and 2004, including up to $150,000 to a single officer.
Papuan's demands for greater autonomy threaten these golden goose and are treated mercilessly.
According to activists, more than 500,000 Papua have been killed and thousands have been raped, tortured and imprisoned by the Indonesian military since 1969.
Mass killings in Papua's tribal highlands in the 1970's became genocide, according to the Asia Human Rights Commission.
In the region alone, Indonesian police arrested more than 3,900 peaceful protesters last year.
"We Will Lose Everything," according to a 2016 report by the Archdiocese of Brisbane, which includes reports of atrocities of the previous year, such as unlawful executions and torture - rape and electroplation are particularly popular, according to another report - and the brutal crackdown on peaceful demonstrations.
"It's difficult to count the number of casualties as incidents happen every week," says Andreas Harsono, Indonesia researcher for Human Rights Watch.
The screws have intensified as Papua's resources bring an influx of settlers from other parts of Indonesia.
The population of 3.5 million inhabitants of the province is 83% Christian, but demographic development is changing as Muslim economic migrants come from the populous islands of Java, Borneo, Sumatra and Sulawesi.
Javanese warning canteens sell fried chicken and gado gado, a mixed vegetable served with peanut sauce.
Local people struggle to keep up here.
"The migrants have started selling chickens and vegetables at the traditional market cheaper than the local papua," explains Abeth You, a 24-year-old Paniai native who moved to the provincial capital Jayapura for work.
"It has tasted the native Papua - the mum-mama [the women] of Papua - their market."
Indonesian President Joko Widodo, popularly known as Jokowi, pledged to address the inequalities and rights violations in Papua during his 2014 election campaign.
The former carpenter secured 27 of Papua's 29 districts - including Paniai - on their way to the presidential palace in Jakarta.
But little has changed in Papua, and today locals feel betrayed.
"Our hearts are broken because we voted for Jokowi in 2014, with the expectation that he will fulfill our hopes that justice will be restored."
In fact, Paniai suffered a low point just two months after Jokowi's inauguration in October.
On December 7, 2014, a group of 11 children were outside singing Christmas carols outside a campfirefire in Enarotali when two Indonesian soldiers broke through the darkness on a motorcycle.
The terrified children told them they should turn on their headlights.
One of the soldiers took offense at their tone and later came back with four soldiers, according to local pastor Yavedt Tebai.
The soldiers who had been drinking hunted and beat the group with their rifle buttons, said victims and witnesses.
Then one of the soldiers fired into the group of children.
One child, 16-year-old Yulianus Yeimo, was so badly beaten that he fell into a coma.
A few hours later, the building was set on fire by the nearby government commission and things escalated the next day.
About 1,000 young Papua men, women and children gathered at a football field outside the local police station and military command center to demand justice.
They wore ceremonial hunting arches and performed the Waita dance - running in the circle and imitating a birdsong - of Papua's Mee Pago trunk.
Some protesters began hurling stones at police and military posts.
When the mood was heated, an order was sent to the soldiers via an internal funk: "When the masses resist more than three times, they shoot them dead," according to an official document from TIC, which has not been released to local media.
Yeremias Kayame, 56, the head of Enarotali's Kego Koto district, saw the looming danger and called for calm and urged the crowd to return home.
No one was listening in the mood.
"When I turned around, I was suddenly shot into my left wrist," he told TIME on the porch of his colorfully painted wooden house.
Kayame still doesn't know who fired, but says the bullet came from the ranks of the soldiers gathered here.
"It was crowded, a lot of shots were fired," he adds.
The local man Alfius Youw has been hit three times, according to his cousin, who witnessed the shootings.
"I ran towards him and examined his body to make sure it was him," Yohanes, who, like many Indonesians, bears only one name, told the TIME in a bleak mood.
"I saw that he was dead ... I kissed him."
Papua's chief inspector Yotje Mende told reporters that his officers only wanted to "secure" their station because it was being attacked.
"We have to defend ourselves when people threaten to kill us," Papua police spokesman Pudjo Sulistiyo said in 2015.
“It’s about life and death.”
According to Human Rights Watch, five young protesters were killed and many more injured.
The news of the killings didn't come 'filtered' to Jakarta until the following day.
Jokowi gave an impassioned speech in Jayapura for three weeks expressing sympathies for the families of the victims and vowing to address the historical grievances in Papua.
"I want this case to be resolved immediately, so it won't happen again in the future," he said.
Security Minister Wiranto said in October 2016 that he was setting up an out-of-court mechanism to resolve historic human rights violations.
But the excuses began almost immediately.
“Most injuries occurred a long time ago.
Some were in the 90's and early 2000s.
The point is that we're committed to addressing these violations, but there are processes that need to go through," he said.
Then Wiranto made a retreat when he spoke to TIME in Jakarta on June 5 and said he had no plans to set up a complaints mechanism in Papua.
Instead, "everything is regulated by law," he said.
Wiranto, whom the U.N. has indicted for "crimes against humanity," which claimed more than 1,000 fatalities in East Timor's 1999 bloody independence vote, said 11 counts of human rights violations had already been settled in Papua, including the Paniai incident.
Families of Paniai victims accepted such claims with grim incredulity.
"I've been interviewed four times in the last three years, but there hasn't been any progress," Yohanes says.
"I'm tired."
He says that years later, he still lives in fear.
"I'm scared," he says.
"I'm afraid of being arrested by the military, I'm afraid I'll be shot."
His brother Yacobus reiterated the view that people in Paniai are afraid to discuss the incident.
He says he was beaten by the military after helping to bury four of the casualties.
"After burying the corpses, the military searched for me," he says.
The shootings didn't stop.
On Tuesday, Indonesia's police shot at villagers in Paniai's Deiyai neighborhood.
During a confrontation between villagers and the manager of a construction company that refused to take an unconscious man to the hospital, one person died and 17 others were injured, including children.
The man, 24-year-old Ravianus Douw, who drowned while fishing in a nearby river, died on the way to the hospital.
Excited villagers protested outside the company's location office.
Police said the villagers threw stones at officers who responded by firing warning shots.
But locals say the mobile brigade (Indonesian paramilitary police) began shooting at the crowd and killed one.
"We were so panicked, we're afraid there's going to be revenge," says 29-year-old Dominggu Badii, who lives near the hospital and has witnessed how the injured have been admitted here quickly, according to TIME.
“I’ve been hiding in my house for two days.”
The Deiyai Parliament has called for the officers involved to be held accountable and to withdraw the mobile brigade of police from the area.
Paniai has always been a trouble spot for the Indonesian government.
The lack of meaningful development strengthens the dissatisfaction of the tribal members Mee, Moni, Dani and Damal, who live in the green highlands of Papua.
Many joined the "Free Papua Movement" (OPM), the rebel army that claims to defend Papua's rights by toasting sporadic attacks and kidnappings by Indonesian soldiers.
Some of the top OPM leaders are from Paniai, including Tadius Yogi and Daniel Yudas Kogoya.
In response, thousands of people have been arrested and arbitrarily taken into custody in recent years under the pretense of "ensuring national sovereignty."
Some never reappear.
Among the people of Papua, Paniai is known as "a tragic, forgotten place."
Poverty reinforces dissatisfaction.
The bit of rice that sells in Enarotali is too expensive for locals.
Bread is just as out of reach
The people here grow everything they eat: above all nota and fruit and leafy vegetables.
Agriculture is the task of the women, who can work four or five fields of the sweet potato.
They retain the bulk of the crop for the family, while the rest are sold at the local market.
Ten pieces of Nota cost only 10,000 Indonesian rupees (75 cents).
Over time, economic inequalities have grown between native Papua and the new migrants, which have arrived in greater numbers since the opening of new flight routes to Nabire Airport.
The few jobs there are in the region usually go to the better educated and more prosperous migrants.
Papua rarely have the capital or the necessary skills to run their own businesses competitively.
"The young people aren't interested in staying in the village ... because there's no jobs or money here," says John Gobai, the chairman of Paniai's tribal council.
Isolation keeps the eyes of the world away from Papua.
In addition, restrictions on reporting international mediaries remain tense.
Earlier this year, French journalists Franck Escudie and Babille Longchamp were deported from Papua because of a lack of coordination with related institutions, although they were given rare permission to film.
According to Phelim Kine, Human Rights Watch's deputy Asia director, Jokowi's election campaign's promises to lift restrictions on reporting to promote transparency and development have not been realized.
"There are new dangers for foreign journalists trying to report from Indonesia's troubled easternmost provinces of Papua and West Papua: visa denial and a blacklist," he said in a statement.
Lack of press control means that international pressure on the Indonesian government remains largely confined to Papua's immediate neighbours.
In March, six Pacific states - Tonga, Nauru, Palau, Tuvalu, the Marshall Islands and the Solomon Islands - urged the U.N. Human Rights Council to investigate the "various and widespread violations" in Papua, including the Paniai shooting.
The same countries have historically supported the OPM.
Indonesia's foreign ministry spokesman Arrmanatha Nasir dismissed the group's accusations and told journalists in Jakarta: "A democratic system still applies in Indonesia, and there is free media, so it's difficult to cover up the evidence of human rights cases."
The people on the ground want more foreign governments to take note of this.
When an official delegation from the Netherlands, led by human rights ambassador Kees Van Baar, Jayapura visited Jayapura on May 4, locals broke their silence and afflicted him "We want freedom," according to a source who also attended the meeting but wishes to remain anonymous.
Indonesia has another presidential election in 2019, but the Papuan say they probably won't vote for Jokowi again.
"Jokowi is a person who has good intentions, but he is surrounded by the people involved in the Paniai gunfires," says Gobai, the chairman of the tribal council.
He wants Jokowi to know that the Paniai people, apart from living under the constant threat of a predatory militiary, find themselves in poor education and health services.
Gobai says the Paniai people, like other Papuas, regard their voice for Jokowi as a "debt" he has to repay.
"They don't need money, they just want justice," he says.
Despite the threats and intimidations, families of Paniai's fire victims carried out one last symbolic act of defiance: the body of a victim was buried on a site exactly opposite the police and the military station.
They know that they may never receive justice, but at lease do not let those responsible forget their crimes.
"A member of our family has been killed," Yacobus says with his head lowered.
“What else can we do?”
Fires capture Dubai' 'Torch' skyscraper for second time
A fire founded its down-through book Friday in one of the world's tallest residential towers in Dubai, forcing hundreds of residents to flee the 337-metre-high, 79-storey building.
It's the second fire that sweeps in just two years through the high-rise, which happens to be called the Torch-Tower.
The building was evacuated, no injuries were reported, and there was no direct word about the cause of the blaze.
"We were asleep and we got up by the fire alarm and the people's screaming.
We ran down the stairs, and it took about 10 minutes to get down from the 50th floor," a resident who gave his name as George told Reuters.
Dubai's civil defence authorities said firefighting units extinguished the fire at around 4.00am local time (00.00 a.m. GMT) and cooled the building.
The government said it was working to provide accommodation for those affected.
The incident can revive questions about the safety of materials used in outdoor areas of high-rise buildings around the world.
An investigation by the management of the Torch building after its fire in 2015 revealed that most of the damage came from the cladding, the exterior cladding for decoration or insulation.
Speaking in the UK, police said they believe the system of insulation and disguise at London's Grenfell Tower in June may have contributed to the rapid spread of a fire in which about 80 people died.
No Davis Cup reform: Continue best-of-five
The president of the International Tennis Federation (ITF) has failed with his major Davis Cup coup at the General Assembly.
At the meeting in Ho-Chi-Minh City, the revolutionary proposal for this sport to play Davis-Cup individual only on two winning sets was not supported wafer-thin by the required two-thirds majority.
The five-set classics, which are often very long, but also very appreciated for this reason, are therefore preserved in the traditional competition.
63.54 percent of the member countries voted in favour of the reform, the goal of ITF President David Haggerty was therefore only narrowly missed.
“We respect this decision, but are disappointed that our member stations have not adopted the entire reform package for the Davis Cup and Fed Cup.
We need a change to secure the long-term future of these iconic and historical competitions," Haggerty said.
We will continue to work to improve the competitions.
After many objections, the proposal to host the final in a neutral place did not even come to the vote.
Only comparatively smaller details from 2018 are only comparatively smaller details: the finalists in the Davis Cup and Fed Cup will in the following year in the following year have the host right.
In order to reduce the costs for the organizers, the requirements in terms of training pitches, etc. will be facilitated and the players will have to complete fewer official media appointments before the Davis Cup.
More than 1 in 5 U.S. travelers put banned items on the plane, a survey finds
A survey of air passengers found that more than 20 percent deliberately or unconsciously smuggled prohibited items past the U.S. Transportation Security Administration checkpoints, including at least 6 percent who entered the plane while accidentally carrying prohibited knives or other items with blades.
Less than 1 percent claimed they had belatedly discovered that they had mistakenly traveled with firearms, ammunition or explosives.
The survey of more than 1,000 people - conducted by a jet charter service, the Stratos Jet Charters Inc. - also found that younger travelers were more likely to disregard the rules.
Of respondents admitting that they knowingly tried to fly with something banned by the TSA, 19.7 percent were millennials, compared with less than 15 percent who were members of Generation X.
The most common items that were intentionally smuggled past TSA agents were food and liquids.
More than 3 percent admitted knowingly carrying items with blades through security, while 2.2 percent of female respondents and 3.7 percent of male respondents also confirmed they intentionally put banned drugs on the plane.
The company said it had surveyed travelers across the country to find out how many had accidentally brought smuggling goods through airport security and how many would have done it intentionally.
It's no surprise that the people most likely to carry contraband - knowingly or unknowingly - through safety were the ones who fly most often.
However, the company points out that the TSA policies are somewhat complicated and confusing.
People are often unsure which liquids they are allowed to take with them or how much of it.
So it's no surprise that the largest amount of items seized by the TSA are prohibited liquids.
For a time, people thought the TSA might see books as contraband.
Liquids are allowed on domestic flights but not on international flights.
Elon Musk doesn't matter: Tens of Thousands of Tesla Customers Cancel Order
Elon Musk likes to give with the huge number of pre-orders for his new Tesla "Model 3".
In doing so, he has so fasted that a part of the customers has already withdrawn their non-binding reservation.
In addition to the always triumphant figures on sales increases and even gigantic sales targets, Tesla CEO Elon Musk for the first time revealed a different, less glamorous figure in the announcement of the most recent quarterly balance sheet.
Musk had frequently advertised in recent months with the more than 500,000 pre-orders for the new car, whose delivery is just beginning.
Now Musk admits: Of the 518,000 pre-orders, 63,000 have already been withdrawn.
Critics had repeatedly pointed out that these are only non-binding reservations.
In the event of a cancellation, Tesla will repay the €1000 ($1000 in the U.S. and £1000 in the U.K.) "reservation deposit" in full.
There are currently 45,000 such non-binding reservations available, Musk acknowledged in a conference call with analysts.
However, the Tesla boss does not believe that the cancellation of around twelve percent of all orders so far could become a problem.
His argument: The challenge with the ambitious growth targets is not in terms of demand, but on the production side.
Last year, Tesla delivered 84,000 vehicles.
In 2018, there are supposed to be 500,000 - taken all the models together, in 2020 a million.
The demand for this is obviously there.
According to Musk, 1800 reservations are currently added per day.
If you order a "Model 3" today, you can expect delivery in the second half of 2018 at the earliest - if Tesla manages to drive its brand-new, never-before-tested production lines without major interruptions with maximum capacity utilization.
Many experts doubt that this is possible.
However, if there are major delays, the non-commitment of the reservations could become a problem for Tesla.
Because then the company cannot prevent dissatisfied customers from running away in droves.
EPA chief Pruitt withdraws to reduce emissions because of pulling out Obama-Era's rules
A day after he was sued by 15 states, Scott Pruitt, the head of the Environmental Protection Agency, withdrew his earlier decision to delay the implementation of the Obama-era rules in order to reduce emissions of smog-causing aerial pollutants.
Pruitt presented the change when his agency turned out to the needs of the state environmental authorities more responsively than previous administrations.
He did not mention the lawsuit that had been brought against his previous post in a federal appeals court.
It is about a deadline that expired on October 1 for states to comply with 2015 standards for ground-level ozone.
Pruitt announced in June that he would delay compliance by a year in order to give his authority more time to study the plan and to avoid "interfering into local decisions or obstructing economic growth."
Pruitt, who was prosecutor of Oklahoma prior to his appointment by President Donald Trump, has long been a reliable adversary of stricter environmental regulations.
Since his arrival in Washington, Pruitt has repeatedly resorted to blocking or delaying regulations that the chemical industry and the fossil fuel industry are resisting.
Wednesday's sudden reversal is Wednesday's latest legal setback for Pruitt's regulatory rollback agenda.
Last month, a federal appointment court in Washington ruled that the EPA steward overstepped his authority when he tried to delay the implementation of an Obama administration rule requiring oil and gas companies to monitor and reduce methane leaks.
In a statement published on Wednesday evening, Pruitt suggested that his U-turn on ozone standards quite simply reinforce the EPA's readiness to cooperate with states through the complex process of timely fulfillment of the new standards.
"Among previous administrations, the EPA has often failed to meet the deadlines for implementation and then waited to be indicted by activist groups and others, and, in a settlement, set the date for implementation," said Pruitt, who sued the EPA more than a dozen times in his previous job.
“We don’t believe in regulation through legal proceedings, and we take deadlines seriously.
We also take the law and the authority it gives us seriously.”
Nevertheless, the EPA's opinion said Pruitt will at some point use his "power to delay and all other legally available powers of attorney" to ensure that the regulations are "based on sound policies and the best information available."
Republicans in Congress are pushing for a more comprehensive rewrapping of the ozone rules.
A bill approved last month is intended to delay the implementation of 2015 rules by at least eight years.
The measure has not yet been put to a vote in the Senate.
New York attorney general Eric Schneiderman, who chaired the coalition of states that sued the EPA this week, said the group intended to maintain legal pressure.
"The EPA's reversal - according to our lawsuits - is an important gain for the health and safety of these 6.7 million New Yorkers and the over 115 million Americans directly affected by the smog flowing into their communities," Schneiderman said.
New York joined California, Connecticut, Delaware, Illinois, Iowa, Maine, Massachusetts, Minnesota, New Mexico, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, Vermont and Washington and the District of Columbia.
Soil-Ozone emerges when pollutants from cars, power plants, oil refineries, chemical plants and other sources in the atmosphere respond to sunlight.
The resulting smog can cause serious respiratory problems in sensitive groups of people, and contribute to thousands of premature deaths every year.
Representatives of the public health and environmentalists cheered on Pruitt's surprising change of course.
"It's disturbing how much pressure it takes to get this sensible move from the person responsible for protecting the air we inhale," said Lori Ann Burd of the Center for Biological Diversity.
“We must continue to fight against the Trump administration’s ideological crusade to impolluters and their particular interests.”
Tumlingen celebrates 750 years of St. Hilarius Church
750 years of St. Hilarius Church: On the occasion of the anniversary of the mountain church, the old cemetery area in Tumlingen on Sunday, July 16, at 4:30 p.m. will be invited to a serenade.
Whether its idyllic location and beautiful views of the village, the historic St. Hilarius complex is a popular place to stay.
Visitors to the sixth benefit serenade can be enchanted by this powerful place.
The rescue and preservation of the oldest building in the village has made the friends and promoters of Tumlingen their concern.
Walter Martini emphasizes: "It's a unique village jewel".
In 1267, the St. Hilarius Chapel was mentioned for the first time in the Württemberg Book of Documents: Count Hermann von Sulz sold "the mountain with the parish church of Tumlingen on it, together with the vault" to Magister Eberhard von Horb and to his brother Dietrich for 20 marks of silver.
According to the wording of the document, in addition to the former parish church, there was also a stately court on the Tumlinger Berg, to which the church or the right of patronage belonged and not vice versa.
So people lived there, too.
As can be seen from the description of the Oberamt Freudenstadt from 1858, in addition to the Unterdorf and the Oberdorf, which can still be seen today in the townscape of Tumlingen, the "mountain" was the third, the stately settlement core of the village.
Old maps from the period around the year 1600 underpin these historical conditions.
The patronage law arose out of the early Middle-aged church law.
A gentleman who built a church, equipped it and occupied it with a pastor, remained the owner of this church and was able to bequeath this own church.
Associated with this was the right to appoint a pastor or to propose a new pastor to the bishop.
There were three altars in the church and two bells on the tower.
The greater of the two shattered on Christmas 1673.
The newly purchased belcome heard for the first time in 1692, but had to be handed over later in the Second World War in 1942.
In the 30-year war (1616 to 1648), the window panes were smashed and the bell ropes were stolen.
The roof was leaking.
In 1796, French troops camped near the church.
They broke out the pulpit and church chairs and used it as firewood.
Remains of the church threatened to collapse in 1809.
Until 1869, the Tumlinger Hilarius Cemetery also served as the final resting place for the dead from Hörschweiler.
In 1823, the cemetery was extended and the Church of Hilarius was demolished.
Only the church tower was left standing.
On New Year's night of 1834, a storm split the church tower and half collapsed.
To prevent an accident, the tower was demolished.
Only the tower dwarf, which still exists today, was preserved as a witness to the 750-year-old church history.
Today Horst Kallenberger and Rolf-Dieter Wasem from the "Schaffigen Pensioners" as the successor of Dieter Dockhorn maintain the Hilarius facility.
On Sunday, July 16, the friends and promoters of Tumlingen invite you to the sixth Serenade at the old cemetery to the St. Hilariuskapelle.
It is designed by the combo of the Kepler-Gymnasium Freudenstadt and with speeches by Kurt Kübler and Reinhold Matteis.
Local leader Hartmut Romann speaks the final word.
The program will begin at 4:30 p.m.
Admission is from 15.30 o'clock.
At the end of the Serenade, there will be a drink in front of the cemetery.
"Enjoy a summer evening under the shade of old trees and with the wide view over meadows towards the forest", the organizers highlight the fantastic view.
The serenade only takes place in dry weather.
The organizers hope for many supporters for the preservation of the historic St. Hilarius facility, which is important for Tumlingen and Hörschweiler.
Tumlingen's friends and supporters are particularly committed to home care, as Serenade organizer Walter Martini affirms.
Association wants approval of wind turbines checked
"Boisheim defends itself" sees no legal prerequisite for the construction and has therefore contacted the district council.
The association "Boisheim defends itself" has asked Landrat Andreas Coenen (CDU) to withdraw its approval for the construction of planned wind turbines in the Boisheimer Nette.
It was made illegal.
The association argues that at the time of the approval on December 30, 2016, no wind turbines could have been approved at all, since at that time in Viersen only on the city border with Schwalmtal a wind power concentration zone existed.
Within this concentration zone, construction and operation of wind turbines are fundamentally possible, but at the same time the concentration zone causes an exclusion of wind energy use for the rest of the urban area.
Such zones are intended to prevent an "asparagus" of the landscape.
The city of Viersen had decided a few days earlier by a change of the land use plan a second wind power concentration zone in the Boisheimer Nette - but this had not been approved by the district government of Düsseldorf, because of a formal error.
"Due to the lack of planning law requirements, the issued approval notice is still unlawful and therefore also to be withdrawn," said Christoph Erkens, chairman of "Boisheim defends himself."
Up-to-date state of affairs: Before the summer break, the city council has once again decided on the wind concentration zone in Boisheimer Nette; a permit from the district government is still pending.
Erkens: "At this point, the grant of a permit for the construction of wind turbines is excluded under planning law".
The circle confirms the receipt of the request.
"The corresponding letters have been received by the district of Viersen since last week," a spokesman explained.
The circle takes them as an opportunity to re-examine the matter.
However, they are not expected to cause the proceedings to be halted.
With a view to ongoing proceedings at the administrative court, the district did not want to comment on the content of the reasoning of the association "Boisheim defends itself".
The NEW Re, a subsidiary of NEW, is planning the construction of four 200-meter-high wind turbines on an area on Boisheimer Nette.
Last year, the company, in which the city of Viersen is indirectly involved, had already concluded leases with the owner of the approximately 65-hectare site.
With the construction of the four wind turbines, the electricity demand of around 11 400 households is to be met - this corresponds to around a fifth of the electricity demand of all Viersen households.
THE NEW Re intends to invest around 20 million euros.
Last summer, Viersen's city council gave the green light for the purchase of three wind turbines at the Linnich wind farm near Aachen as well as a stake in a wind turbine in Übach-Palenberg in Heinsberg district.
Car and tractor crash together: Woman dies in horrific accident
A horrific traffic accident occurred on Friday morning at around 10 a.m. on the L 333 between Niederwallmenach and Bogel (Rhein-Lahn-Kreis).
One woman was fatally injured.
Two other people were slightly injured.
The state road remained closed for hours.
According to the police, a 17-year-old young woman from Bornich drove her car with her 53-year-old mother as an accompanying person the L 333 in the direction of Bogel.
City and merchants plan 'home-shopping' weekend
Bad Langensalzaer and people from the region are to rediscover the shopping facilities in the city centre in September.
The trade association and the city administration want to use the weekend on 8 and 9 September to attract residents to the city center for shopping.
As the chairman of the business association, Christian König, announced, the "home-shopping" weekend should sensitize the customers to what offers the own city off the usual supermarket goods.
Therefore, a market with regional providers is planned, similar to the weekly market.
In addition, local companies should present themselves with their training places.
In addition, the shops of the city center are supposed to invite "midnight shopping."
The action is not only aimed at residents of the city and the surrounding region.
In preparation are commercials on the radio, an Internet video and printed bags that point to the action weekend, according to Christian König.
City administration and trade association cooperate with the Chamber of Commerce and Industry.
With this, the trade association and the city are going into another stage of the "Attractive downtown" project.
This includes new offerings such as the first "active day" at the Brunnenfest, including an open-sale Sunday, discussions with all traders and hosts in order to persuade them to participate actively.
Also attractive offers for Saturday and the waiver of parking fees on this day are part of the concept.
In addition, customer potentials are to be new or redeveloped.
This includes spa guests and tourists.
But many locals would also have lost sight of the city center while shopping.
In the end, everyone would have to help revitalize the city center - up to landlords, the establishments of the whole city and clubs.
It would be necessary to overcome with a network the lethargy, which had partly spread, King had said in the spring.
Birgit Streich, head of the IHK-Center in Mühlhausen, announced that for the first the third "Heimat-Shopping"-Day, five Thuringian cities will participate for the first time.
In addition to Bad Langensalza, these are Weimar, Sömmerda, Heiligenstadt and Bleicherode.
The day should "advocate the appreciation of local commerce and emphasize its importance for the common good of our cities more strongly," Streich said.
The action led by the Chamber of Commerce and with many partners also makes it clear that every citizen can influence their own direct environment with their purchasing behavior.
Two dead and several injured after explosion at U.S. school
