New other people were injured.
In a gas explosion in the US city of Minneapolis, two people have been killed and nine others injured, some of them seriously.
According to the fire department, an injured person was still in critical condition on Wednesday (local time).
One person died when part of a school building collapsed in the explosion.
An initially missing person was later also recovered dead.
The dead were employees of the school.
As a cause of the accident, the authorities suspected work on a gas pipeline.
During the holiday season, only a few people stayed in the Minnehaha Academy building.
Trump put pressure on Mexico after leaked transcripts of phone calls over payment of border wall
Transcripts of phone calls between U.S. President Donald Trump and leaders of Mexico and Australia have been released by The Washington Post.
On January 27, in a phone call with Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto urged to stop saying publicly that he would not pay for a planned border wall.
The U.S. president insulted him for publicly denouncing the wall. He reportedly told him, "You can't say that to the press."
Trump launched his presidential campaign with the promise to build a wall on the U.S.'s southern border, vowing to let Mexico pay for the project.
Transcripts of his first call with Australian Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull have also been released, revealing a tense exchange.
Mr. Trump told Mr. Turnbull that a trade for the U.S. to adopt refugees who emigrated to Australia was "ridiculous," "lousy" and "stupid," and that insistencing on the trade brokered by the Obama administration would make him look like a "depp" after proposing a ban on relocating refugees to the US.
Mr. Trump ended the call after telling Mr Turnbull "that's the most awkward call for the whole day."
Moore's Ford massacre: Activists recite racist lynching as a call for justice
A 1948-man Ford Mercury drove through a group of spectators in rural Monroe, Georgia, and rumbled towards Moore's Ford Bridge.
Before the car, which had four black passengers and a white driver, could reach the bridge, a group of white men with rifles stepped out to block his path.
The mob's leader - a middle-aged man in a pinstripe suit - took a long train at his cigar and looked scrutiny through the windshield.
Inside, the occupants hovered in fear.
"We want this n ---- r Roger!" the man yelled.
“Get him out of the car.”
Screams ripped the silence when a young black man was dragged from the passenger seat.
Luckily, this horrific scene, played on July 22, is just a re-enactment.
But then a sling is thrown around the neck of "Roger," almost everyone in the audience ejected very real sighs.
Since 2005, civil rights activists have been coming back to Moore's Ford Bridge to imitate the night in which two black couples - Roger and Dorothy Malcom as well as George and Mae Murray Dorsey - were lynched by the Ku Klux Klan in 1946.
No one has ever been charged with the murders.
"It's really unbelievable that in all these years, not a single person has been arrested - even though we're seeing them in our communities, even though we know who they are," said Tyrone Brooks, who is helping organizing the re-enactment.
“It’s a stain of dirt on a story, but it’s a burden to our souls.”
Brooks is a 71-year-old former congressman in the state of Georgia and a lifelong civil rights activist.
For him, the re-enactment serves as a dramatic call to action and represents an annual recollection of the Monroe community that an injustice has never been corrected.
"We want law enforcement, we want cessation, we want healing, we want reconciliation, but first we have to get justice," he said.
“We can’t come to healing and reconciliation until we receive justice.”
A coroner puts a bedcloth over the corpse of one of the victims of the Moore Ford lynchen on July 27, 1946.
According to FBI files obtained by the NBC News, more than 50 men from Monroe and the surrounding counties were suspected in the lynching process.
But no one has ever been charged.
Although the suspects have remained on the loose for decades, civil rights activists say the dual motivation for the lynching actions throughout the city has always been known: politics and sex.
In April 1946, for the first time, a Supreme Court decision allowed the black citizens in Georgia to cast their votes in the pre-election campaign in the summer.
Roughly at the same time as the election, according to the FBI, black farm tenant Roger Malcom stabbed a white landowner, Barnett Hester, during a fight - allegedly because of a woman.
Brooks said the rumor in the city had been that Hester slept with Malcom's wife Dorothy and that the baby she wore wasn't from her husband.
On July 25, 1946, Loy Harrison, a prominent white landowner, paid $600 to free Malcom from prison, according to the FBI.
He was escorted to prison by Dorothy and her cousins Mae Murray and George Dorsey.
But then when they moved away from the prison and approached Moore's Ford Bridge, a lynch mob surrounded the car.
"As it turns out, it was Harrison who prepared and ordered the murders," an FBI report concludes.
“It’s also evident that there was a plot between state and local law enforcement that wasn’t just involved in the killings, eliminating evidence and covering up the identities of witnesses.”
Brooks puts it even more concisely.
"It was an electoral law massacre," he said.
"You've all been killed to send a message to black people in this community: "If you're registering and if you vote, then this will happen to you."
For almost a decade, Cassandra Greene has led the annual re-enactment of the Moore Ford Bridge Lynch murders.
Their depiction is vivid, poignant, and based on a kind of deep racism that many would like to believe no longer exists in America.
It's hard to watch, and even harder to look away, but Greene said that's the point.
"We don't want to talk about lynching because then we're confronted with its ugliness in all of us," she said.
“But I can’t build a relationship with you if you don’t want to listen to me or don’t want to hear what I’ve been through.”
Rehearsals for this year's production began in June, and on the first day, nearly 30 actors - a dozen whites and the rest of blacks - crowded into a room and listened as Greene launched a well-rehearsed overview of the piece.
Wade Marbaugh is Greene's co-director here.
He's played the role of chief clan member for years, but he said the role will never get any easier.
"I'm feeling dirty because I'm not like that," Marbaugh said.
“But I think it’s very important to keep this story alive because we don’t want to go back to those times.”
On the other side of the room, actors Darrius Bradshaw and Nick Rascona, playing Roger Malcom and Barnett Hester, rehearsed their lines.
The play begins with Malcom screaming at Hester and afflicting him, angry that he slept with his wife, Dorothy.
“I said, n --- he, get away from my property!”
Rascona returns fire.
There's a pause.
And then everyone (at least all black actors) breaks out into laughter.
Greene laughs the loudest.
"What?!" Rascona asked.
He's alarmed and confused.
Were I too tough?
"No," Greene said, still giggling.
"I just don't think white people were saying 'n----- he'
"Yes," joked one of the other black performers.
“You only have to meet the hard ‘R.’.”
And that's exactly where the energy shifts in the room, a sense of camaraderie replaces unease.
Everyone is busy getting this right, not only because of the game, but also because of the memory of the four people killed.
The scene begins again.
This time, when Rascona says the N-word, he says it sharply and forcefully.
Absolutely none is laughing.
This repetition ends violently and quickly.
There are screams, gunshots and then silence.
A woman, dressed in a grief dress, stands above the actors' corpses and performs Billie Holiday's grieving eulogy "Strange Fruit."
Her voice breaks at every second word.
About a hundred yards behind, a group of about four dozen spectators wipes sweat and tears from their eyes.
The audience is divided almost evenly between black and white.
A couple has arrived from Ithaca, New York, to see the performance, many just coming down the road.
They've all experienced Georgia's oppressive moisture for nearly eight hours as Brooks takes them on a pilgrimage to the burials of the Malcoms and Dorseys.
Up to this point, the murders have remained an anecdote, but now, if you are on the same land where the lynching murders took place, the air feels ghostly.
When the song's last notices end, Greene invites the crowd to come closer and inspect the corpses.
Children squat at their heads, register the faked blood and the silent bodies and whisper to each other.
"They're not really dead," mutters a little boy, as if to remember.
After a few minutes Greene thanks the crowd - and suddenly it's over.
The actors throw away the lynching rope and wipe off their tears.
Bradshaw hugs Marbaugh, the man in the suit who played the clan member.
Nick Rascona hugs two of his fellow players and then breaks down crying in her arms, the emotional burden of the performance eventually breaks through with him.
"It's okay," one of the women whispers.
“We know that’s not in your heart.
You've done that for a reason."
Later, the audience sits down with the actors for dinner.
At a table, a white couple from Atlanta chats excitedly about the re-enactment with a black couple they've just met.
Denise Duplinski struggled to find words about what feeling the performance left behind.
"It's hard to hear these terrifying words and actions ... from people who look like yourself and who do that because they look like you," she said.
"What that's really doing with me makes me believe the lynching is still going on," she added, "today it's just called police shooting."
In the same room, Tyrone Brook's courtyard stops at his table, he eats and commemorates civil rights.
He said he had already started planning the performance next year.
"The re-enactment is inspiring in a way, because it describes the horror of what we have endured, but it's also a moment of learning," he said.
“It’s like, if we don’t learn anything from it, then it can happen again.
Whoever does not study their own history, who does not know their own history, is condemned to repeate it."
"Götterdämmerung" reconciled the Wagnerians in Bayreuth
Also in the fourth part of Richard Wagner's monumental work, director Frank Castorf relies on a colorful-frivolous staging against a gigantic, six-storey backdrop.
The revolving stage (designed by Aleksandar Denic) optionally shows a shoddy street corner with the remnant of the wall and kebabs in Berlin, a "Plaste und Elaste" logo from GDR times or the New York Stock Exchange.
In front of it parks an open Mercedes classic car in which the Rhine daughters pose as rocker girls, or alternatively an Isetta.
Stacked oil barrels are reminiscent of Castorf's leitmotif - money, power and greed - that runs through his "Ring" staging.
The three Rhine daughters - sung by Stephanie Houtzeel, Wiebke Lehmkuhl and Alexandra Steiner - harmonise wonderfully as in the "Rheingold".
Kess seduces Siegfried (Stefan Vinke), drag a corpse into the trunk in Hollywood manner and give the Krawallo clique.
Stephen Milling sings the gloriously nasty Hagen that surrounds the aura of a heavyweight boxer.
Brutally the scene when he slays Siegfried.
Later Gunther (Markus Eiche) has to believe in it.
The audience would like to thank the ensemble with strong applause.
Foster, Milling, Vinke and conductor Janowski, who is modest and bowing their hands to the Merkel rhombus.
There is great applause for the festival orchestra, which shows itself on stage for the first time - in view of the great heat again in casual leisure clothes.
The performance was eagerly awaited by Frank Castorf and his directing team.
If in 2013 he still had to endure a glaring whistling concert, there was great jubilation five years later, in which the isolated "Buh" calls went down.
Festival director Katharina Wagner may have been happy about it.
"One notices that the audience with the "Ring" has grown together in some way," she had said shortly before the performance of the dpa.
The audience not only made a form of peace with Castorf, but it had also grown an understanding of his point of view.
