Pearson cuts another 3,000 jobs and cuts dividend to revive business
Britain's Pearson education group said on Friday it would cut another 3,000 jobs and cut its interim dividend by 72 per cent as another restructuring programme would be launched to overcome the pressure the company is facing.
The job cuts, which Director-General John Fallon announced predominantly for the end of 2018 and the beginning of 2019, are coming in addition to his previous restructuring, in which 4,000 jobs have already been lost.
Pearson announced in a third cost-cutting program in May a further £300 million ($394 million) plan to revitalize a business hit by the quick move to digital learning.
The company, which last month sold its stake in Penguin Random House to partner Bertelsmann, said the outlook for the financial year remained unchanged after gaining 1 per cent to £2.05 billion in the first half.
Kabul's convoy attack: Nato soldier killed in suicide attack
A suicide bomber has attacked a military convoy in the Afghan capital Kabul, killed a soldier from Nato and injured several others, officials say.
Five soldiers and an interpreter were injured, a NATO statement said.
Unconfirmed local reports said three civilians also died in the incident in the Qarabagh district.
The bombing is the last act of violence hitting Afghanistan this monight - on Tuesday, more than 30 people were killed at a mosque in Herat.
On Monday, a suicide bomber killed two people near the Iraqi embassy in Kabul.
A statement from NATO said Thursday night's blast happened when a joint patrol with the Afghan army was attacked by an "impromprovised explosive device carried by persons."
The statement says all those injured are in a stable condition and are being treated at the U.S. military hospital at Bagram airfield.
Correspondents say that the growing strength of the Taliban and the group known as the so-called Islamic State (IS) in Qarabagh is a major concern for NATO forces in nearby Bagram.
According to the United Nations, there were at least 1,662 civilian casualties in Afghanistan in the first half of 2017.
In late July, a suicidal car bomb killed at least 30 people in a mainly Shiite district of Kabul.
The Taliban said they carried out this attack.
On May 31, a formidable bombling in the center of the city killed more than 150 people with the country's deadliest militant attack since U.S.-led troops ousted the Taliban from power in 2001.
The violence underscores the precarious security situation in Afghanistan as U.S. President Donald Trump weighs in on increasing the number of U.S. troops supporting the military and police in the country.
Correspondents say the suicide bombings in Kabul in recent weeks have also caused a crisis of public confidence in the government, its policies and, in particular, the reputation of President Ashraf Ghani.
EPA reverses decision to delay smog rule after lawsuits
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency has overturned a decision to delay Obama's administration's regulation requiring states to curb smog-causing emissions. This came a day after 15 states sued the agency over the move.
The EPA announced late on Wednesday the decision to push the so-called "Ozone provisions 2015," and said it showed the agency's commitment to cooperate with states.
"We believe in dialogue with and that we are responding to our state partners," EPA chief Scott Pruitt said in a statement.
Pruitt had announced in June's intention to delay ozone regulations - in which pollutant emissions are to be measured in a part of the country in order to determine where cuts must be made to adhere to stricter air quality standards - by a year until October 2018.
A group of 15 mostly democratic states, along with the district of Colombia, filed a lawsuit on Tuesday, saying the effort was illegal.
President Donald Trump's administration has sought to roll back a wide range of environmental regulations imposed by former President Barack Obama as part of a broader agenda designed to boost economic growth, according to Trump.
Efforts, however, have sparked the setback of Democrats and conservation groups who are worried about the impact of larger emissions on public health.
The EPA's website says that inhaling air containing ozone "can reduce lung function and intensify respiratory symptoms," such as asthma.
Local council wants community space
In the end, the decision was clear: With two votes against, the municipal council of Fluorn-Winzeln decides on the establishment of a common room in the future senior housing complex.
Before the discussion, there is an important information: The purchase contract for the still missing part of the property has been signed, reports Mayor Bernhard Tjaden.
Although it will still take until the area previously belonged to the parish is owned, there is no longer any obstacle to planning for the project to be managed by a private investor.
There is a discussion about a common room for this.
One of the two investors who are still in the boat has offered to meet the community.
On the ground floor, he would provide a living space - around 55 square meters - and an adjoining terrace with at least 70 square meters of space for a special price.
The administration and the majority of the municipal council envisages providing such a common space not as a meeting place only for the residents of senior-friendly housing, but openly into the municipality.
As an example, the word "knitting café" made the rounds.
The decision, which has already been pre-decided last autumn by the allocation of funds for the current budget, will be sweetened by funding opportunities.
The construction is located in the refurbishment area.
The establishment of a common room would be eligible, according to Tjaden, at 60 percent of 30 percent.
In order to signal to the investor, the proposed decision sees that it does not want to be thrown sticks between his legs in this respect, the observation that both the saddle - which is based on the surrounding development - and the pitch roof would be possible, which reduces the eaves height and makes the construction more economical.
Unnecessary, says not only Wilfried Sohmer, is this addition.
If it helps, unanimously, he'll pass the panel.
It's not so easy with the common room.
Daniela Ruf refers to the many own buildings, with which one does not really progress, but one now leans out of the window at the common room, she says: "I have a hard time with it".
Yvonne Staiger is absolutely in favour of the common room - but absolutely against municipal participation.
It sees behind it a covert subsidy to already wealthy or even foreign investors.
This is not the case, because with the acquisition of the land, you do not give a subsidy, but get a real, in case of doubt marketable value, is the counter speech.
Staiger's request to split the proposed decision accordingly finds three proponents.
So it remains with two votes against in the original program, which Stefan Haller summarizes: "If we take the decision this way, we take a giant step towards closing a huge gap in our infrastructure".
Collision with car: cyclist is hurled on road
While crossing the road, an 82-year-old cyclist was captured by a car and seriously injured in Garath on Wednesday.
As the police announced, the 82-year-old Langenfelder at 12.20 o'clock with his bicycle on the bike path of the road Schwarzer Weg coming from the direction of Hilden.
The senior wanted to cross the lane at the Am Buchholzer Busch road.
A passenger car driver driving in the same direction granted him priority.
When Langenfelder drove onto the road, he apparently did not pay attention to the oncoming traffic.
He collided with a car steering a 66-year-old Hilden woman.
The man was thrown into the street.
He suffered serious injuries and was taken to a hospital with an ambulance.
FC Bayern Munich have to do without David Alaba and Thiago in the Super Cup against Borussia Dortmund
The Super Cup takes place without him: David Alaba has to let the game go against Dortmund.
With personnel worries, FC Bayern travels to Dortmund for the first competitive match of the new season.
Seven professionals are missing the defending champion in the Super Cup against the cup winner.
Personnel worries at the German football record champions Bayern Munich are getting bigger and bigger ahead of Saturday's Supercup (20.30 pm/ZDF and Eurosport) at cup winner Borussia Dortmund.
Coach Carlo Ancelotti has to do without David Alaba in the duel with BVB.
This was confirmed by the Italian on Friday.
In addition, the Munich are missing Manuel Neuer, James, Thiago, Jerome Boateng, Arjen Robben and Juan Bernat.
For the five defeats in the past six Test matches, Ancelotti has blamed the physical condition and lack of balance in his team's game.
"We know we'ren't in the best physical condition," Ancelotti said, adding: "The most important point is balance in the team.
It wasn’t good lately.”
However, the 58-year-old believes in an increase in performance in the season's first official competitive match.
“Football is also tactics, strategy, personality and motivation.
I have great confidence that my team will show these qualities.
The squad is ready.
We want to play intelligently," said Ancelotti, who expects a "difficult, intense game."
The duel of the two German football heavyweights captivates the fans.
The game is sold out with 81,360 spectators, the German Football League (DFL) announced on Friday.
Hiking in the resin from star to star
In the Harz, the first "star path" is to be created nationwide.
This is planned by the association Sternwarte Sankt Andreasberg.
There is a planetary path in Göttingen - since 2003.
A "star path" meets the information needs of many Harz visitors, it said.
It combines nature, science, movement and athletic performance.
The members want to set plaques along the planned route and inform them about stars and constellations.
In a first phase until 2018, the association is planning "easy-to-reach installations at five to six locations" in the Oberharz.
St. Andreasberg, Braunlage, Hohegeiß and Sieber would be eligible.
The information on the boards is to be supplemented via the Internet.
Therefore, a good mobile network reception plays a role in the selection of locations.
Accessibility is also a criterion.
Later, the star path will be extended by train.
"On the star path, viewers will learn a lot of interesting facts, for example about the physics of the individual stars," the association explained.
In addition, they learned why stars had different colors or why one star shines more strongly than another, although it is much further away.
It will also be conveyed which constellation a selected star is located in and when it is best seen.
The association has been operating the highest observatory in northern Germany since 2014 in the mountain town of St. Andreasberg.
According to the Federal Office for Nature Conservation, the location in the middle of the Harz National Park is one of the six darkest regions in Germany.
Often from there the Milky Way and even the Andromeda galaxy are clearly visible to the naked eye.
In the university city since 2003 on the initiative of the funding association Planetarium Göttingen, there is something similar to the planned Sternenwerg - the Planetenweg.
Beginning in front of Gebhard's hotel in Goetheallee, which forms the interior of our solar system, the planetary path with its steles leads up to the Hainberg and the observatory - as well as an "exterrestrial" location in Diemarden (community of Gleichen) with the planet Sedna.
The steles with the individual planets - including Pluto degraded to the dwarf planet at the end point - are positioned exactly at a distance and scale of one to two billion.
The earth thus shrinks to a sphere of 6.5 millimeters in diameter, while the sun, which is exactly 75 meters away, measures 70 centimeters.
The total distance of the planetary path is about four kilometers.
While the light takes about eight hours from the sun to Pluto, pedestrians need a good hour to stele with Pluto.
Pedestrians are therefore faster than the light in space.
However, the march to the next fixed star would take another half a year.
Mikey Garcia and Vasyl Lomachenko meet for the first time discuss a possible showdown
Vasyl Lomachenko and Mikey Garcia, who were supposed to be in a collision course to stage the most exciting boxing match in the near future below £147, met for the first time on Thursday.
They were in separate rooms at ESPN offices in Los Angeles until Garcia, the undefeated World Boxing Council lightweight champion, walked down the hallway to greet Lomachenko, the World Boxing Organisation super-featherweight champion.
After Garcia's victory by unanimous resolution over former four-division world champion Adrien Broner on Saturday at Showtime, both fighters are members of the top-five pound-for-pound list.
Garcia stretched out her right hand and said, "How are you, champion?
I wish you the best of luck.”
Lomachenko defends his belt against Miguel Marriaga on Saturday night at 7pm on ESPN.
As the pair posed for photos, Lomachenko manager Egis Klimas noted that a possible showdown between the fighters would be the "perfect order of magnitude."
"I'm a former featherweight," Garcia recalled.
"I've risen to 140 pounds for Broner, but I'm not that big.
I'm small for 140.
I'd like to come down to light weight.
And he has to move on lightweight [135 pounds] and then we struggle.
That's what fans demand.
Let's give them that."
Lomachenko agreed.
“Boxing needs this fight.”
Klimas asked if it should be on pay-per-view, part of a strategy Lomachenko will have to face a 135-pound debut this year to line with Garcia a date (37-0, 30 Knockouts) who is also interested in the fight against the winner of Jorge Linares-Luke Campbell after their light-weight title fight at the Forum on September 23.
"This fight could take place in pay-per-view mode because all the fans asked about it," Garcia said, later openly expressing himself fighting at the network that offers the best financial package.
We're the best.
No other names can generate this kind of attention.
Whenever they are ready ... "
Garcia said he considers Lomachenko to be "an enormous fighter," and he will possibly take part in his Saturday fight.
But Lomachenko stopped at the generous praise for Garcia and said that he thought the three-fight champion from Riverside looked "very slow, I'm sorry Mikey ... you wanted the co-off".
Garcia said the focus was to show impact strength, then boxing.
Both said they could feel the momentum built up for their own struggle.
“This is the struggle that must be carried out.
No other struggle creates so much attention and enthusiasm.
I'm more than happy to work on it," Garcia said.
Lomachenko said: "I've been waiting for the top fighters, and that's Mikey.
It's going to be a great fight.'
Four injured after tyre pitcher
In an accident on the A7, four people were partially seriously injured on Thursday afternoon.
According to initial information, the driver of an Opel Zafira probably lost control of his vehicle at around 16.30 shortly before the Marktbreit junction.
The car hurled right into the embankment, where it rolled over and then remained on the roof in the green strip.
The four occupants - two adults and two children - were partially seriously injured in the accident.
During the initial supply of the injured by the rescue service, the A7 towards the north was closed for about an hour, then the traffic on the left lane could be routed past the accident site.
