New results of Swedish asylum age assessment tests published
The most recent results of tests for asylum seekers in Sweden, who have undergone a new method to medically assess their age, show that more than 80 percent of people tested are older than 18. But not everyone believes in the accuracy of these outcomes.
The Swedish Forensic Medicine Authority (Rättsmedicinalverket) has begun carrying out the tests last year.
They are supposed to make the assessment of the age more precise during the asylum procedure, after the Swedish migration authority (Migrationsverket) was criticized for mistakes in assessing the correct age of some refugees who were minors.
To date, Migrationsverket has sent 6,880 cases to be tested and the Forensic Medicine Agency has now published the results of a total of 2,481 tests from mid-March to July 31, 2017.
Eighty percent (2002) were judged to be 18 or older, while in another 25 cases the Forensic Medicine Agency assessed that the person was "possibly 18 years of age or older."
In 432 cases, the conclusion was that the person "may be under the age of 18."
The tests will only be carried out in cases where the person's age is questioned.
According to Johan Göransson of the Forensic Medicine Agency, the results are consistent over the past few months.
"We don't see much difference in the statistics of the past few months, the numbers are quite constant," he told The Local magazine.
The high proportion of cases in which the person has been judged to be over the age of 18 does not necessarily mean that the asylum seeker lied about his age.
In May, the agency released the results of 581 tests and said there were 442 cases "suggesting that the person examined is 18 years of age or older."
When Svenska Dagbladet examined the 581 published results, it found that in 243 of these cases, the person had openly stated that she was turning 18 this year.
The method of medical age assessment, in which X-rays of wisdom teeth and MRI scans of knee joints are taken, which are then analyzed by dentists and radiologists, has also been criticized in some areas.
One sceptic is endocrinologist Claude Marcus of the Karolinska Institute, whose work involves assessinging people's biological maturity to see if they went through puberty sooner or later than usual.
“I had reason to think about it, and I don’t think the type of measurement should be used.
Assessinging biological age in this way is very uncertain.
If we can't say it for sure, we shouldn't be pretending we could," he told the Metro newspaper.
However, Göransson of the Forensic Medicine Agency defended the accuracy of the tests: "In general, you can say that the risk of misjudging a child as an adult is low.
The biggest risk is when the person is just under 18, in which case it’s about 10 percent.”
Fully in the plan
The work on the three bridges of Landstraße 108 between Lauterbach and Schramberg is progressing well.
On Wednesday, a seven-ton concrete part was installeded at the bridge at Lauterbach's eastern entrance.
"We are very well in time, it's going great," says Reinhold Leicht, construction manager at the Department of Roads and Transport at the Regional Presidium Freiburg.
In April, work beganded simultaneously on all three bridges between Schramberg and Lauterbach.
The end of construction is scheduled for October and it is easy to ensure that the schedule is adhered to.
The first bridge coming from Schramberg leads over the Trombach.
It is a vaulted arch bridge made of concrete, which is clad with red coloured sandstone and has a total length of 4.80 meters.
It was built in 1900.
Like the other two bridges, it is now getting old.
In order to ensure stability and road safety, the three structures are now thoroughly renovated.
The other two bridges lead over the Lauterbach.
They're made of red sandstone.
Both were erected in 1939 and are twelve and 10.60 meters long respectively.
The bridge at the entrance to Lauterbach has now received a 1.30-meter-wide and seven-tonne so-called bridge cap on Wednesday morning on the north side.
This is an edge fastening, which is also used as an operating route.
A 2.20-metre-wide bridge cap is to be deployed on the southern part of the bridge on Friday.
Then the pedestrian path is to run.
The special thing is that these are prefabricated parts, a patent of the Schramberg company Dizwo.
The other two bridges are already equipped with the patented bridge caps.
The installation of cables through the building in Lauterbach has required special sensitivity.
Because there also runs a fiber optic cable, to which many companies are connected.
The residents were informed about the project in advance of the work by handouts.
In addition, fish and crabs had to be fished first.
For the bridges between the building and the collecting pond in the direction of Schramberg, the traffic is single-lane regulated by a traffic light.
For the construction work on the bridge at Lauterbach's entrance, the road in the area of the bridge at the height of the hotels "Tannenhof" and "Sieben Linden" is fully closed.
The cost of the three bridge renovations is a total of 700 000 euros.
The whole thing is financed by the country.
AfD in Gerolstein: Colorful protest at German potato salad
It does not happen so often that parties pull together, but when it comes to the Alternative for Germany (AfD), what is usually difficult to reconcile.
The parties represented in the Vulkaneifel district (CDU, SPD, Greens, FDP, FWG, Linke) belong to a broad alliance, which on this day peacefully shows flag, because the AfD in the Gerolsteiner Stadthalle Rondell invites according to the poster "great election campaign kick-off".
And well-known names to the start - like Alice Weidel, who together with Alexander Gauland forms the top duo for the federal election on September 24, and Deputy Federal Chairman Beatrix von Storch.
But those who are already on site hours before the start of the event do not want to listen to the AfD learners, but to demonstrate.
The motto "The Eifel remains colorful".
Originally, the city picnick was planned under the motto "Everyone at a table" on the Brunnenplatz, but now takes place in the parking lot below the rondell - and thus directly before the eyes of those who come to the AfD event in the Stadthalle.
A colourful picture that presents itself there.
And what Christa Karoli, chairwoman of the Forums Eine Welt and one of the authoritative organizers, is really looking forward to: "It's great how many people have come together here".
There's music and plenty of food, including a "primordial German potato salad that even the AfD could easily eat," says the woman who brought him along.
The police have ordered many officers to Gerolstein, it shows presence especially at the station, which is only a few meters away from the venues.
How did the AfD actually come up with Gerolstein?
Mario Hompes of the AfD Vulkaneifel reports that he tried for such an event and got the contract.
The 45-year-old from Gönnersdorf had initially asked in Daun, but got a rejection there, because there was not enough parking due to the Laurentiuskirmes.
So now the rondell, Hompes is happy that "we can have such a beautiful event here".
With regard to the event at the Rondell forecourt, he says: "That's the good right of the people, the main thing, it remains peaceful".
Around 8 p.m., a band provides entertainment on the Rondell forecourt, various speakers in the Stadthalle, including the Rhineland-Palatinate AfD chairman Uwe Junge.
In there are about 200 listeners who are waiting for the top candidate for the federal election, Alice Weidel, and the Deputy Federal Chairman Beatrix von Storch to give their speeches.
The police presence remains unchanged, but so far the officers had a quiet evening.
The AfD event lasts a little more than hour, the guests on the forecourt have a longer breath, but there is also shortly after 21 o'clock cleaning up.
The police can take off - no special occurrences.
Trombone meets trumpet
There will be a rehearing with the Wolfach trombonist Frederic Belli in the Haslach monastery concerts series tomorrow, Sunday, at 8 p.m. in the monastery church.
Together with the Bulgarian trumpeter Miroslav Petkov, he gives a concert under the motto "Black Sea meets Black Forest", or "Balkanfolklore" meets a classical German education with roots in brass music.
Both soloists are winners and winners of numerous international competitions and have solo positions in top European orchestras.
Petkov is a solo trumpeter at the renowned Concertgebouw Orchestra Amsterdam and Frederic Belli is solo trombonist of the SWR Symphonieorchester.
Both unite the desire not only to cross the boundaries on their instruments, but also to merge the boundaries of different genres.
Johann Sebastian Bach resounds like in a new guise, Daniel Schnyder's "Weltmusik" unites with Bulgarian folklore, and in the piece "Exchange Suite" by the jazz echo award winner Nils Wogram, classical music, jazz and free playing merge into a fascinating overall work of art.
This centrepiece of the program was only premiered this spring with huge success in Freiburg.
The renowned composer Nils Wogram has written it especially for the two musicians and even more: the Haslach monastery church with its special acoustics is consciously included in the concert.
Kristen Stewart talks about her bi-sexuality
“Some people love grilled cheese and will eat it until the end of their lives.
But I want to try everything," Kristen Stewart said openly about her bisexuality.
“When I bite into grilled cheese once, I think, ‘Oh that was cool, what’s next?’.”
Arguably the Hollywood star's best-known relationship was that of her "Twilight" colleague Robert Pattinson, which ultimately divergented bitterly.
Despite all this, the brunette performer assures Harper's Bazaar magazine: "I was seriously in love with everyone I've dated.
Or do you think I played it?”
After her break-up, Kristen admitted she was bisexual: "I've always lived dualism and seriously believed in it.
So it's never puzzled or hindered me.
“I’ve just never likened not being taken seriously.”
According to "Bang Showbiz", however, she suffered particularly from it during school, when she preferred to dress like a boy: "Not all girls are typical girls.
This really hurt me a lot.
When I was eleven, the kids told me I'd look like a boy.
I felt really attacked, frightened and embarrassed.
Now I look back and think to myself, 'girl, be proud of yourself!'."
She doesn't like to explain her love life to the public in particular: "People expect you to behave in a certain way and that annoys me.
And perhaps some describe me as ungrateful, but I don't like having to justify myself in front of the whole public."
West Virginia's governor announces he's changing party
West Virginia's Democratic governor, Jim Justice, announced Thursday night at a rally with President Trump that he would change sides.
Whether you like it or not, the Democrats have distanced themselves from me," Governor Justice said.
“Today I’m going to tell you with a lot of prayers and a lot of thought ... today I’m going to tell you as a resident of West Virginia, I can’t help you anymore if I’m a Democratic governor.”
The rally erupted in jubilation.
"Tomorrow I'm going to change my registration with Republicans," Justice added.
“As a coach, I’d say it’s time to play another game.”
Mr. Trump previously pledged a major announcement at the West Virginia Rally.
Mr. Trump won West Virginia by 42 points, and Justice didn't approve Democratic nominee Hillary Clinton.
Justice, which is the only billionaire in the state, according to Forbes, was elected in 2016.
Forbes notes that he owns coal mines in five states and inherited a coal business from his father.
It's worth about $1.59 billion.
In the meantime, the Republican National Committee issued a statement on Thursday night saying: "The party change from Governor Justice is another blow to a Democratic Party that would prevent us rather than work on making our country great again."
"With only 15 governorates and the scrutiny of few legislative bodies in history, Governor Justice's announcement is only the last rejection of a party that is without leadership from top to bottom and unable to find a positive, unifying message," RNC Chairman Ronna McDaniel told CBS News in a statement.
Coe praises 'honest' Russian apology for doping scandal
Worldwide athletics chief Sebastian Coe praised on Thursday what he described as an outspoken apology from Russia to a doping scandal, but he reiterated that it was not the moment for the country to be welcomed back into the sport.
Russia's athletics chief Dmitry Shylakhtin said during the IAAF congress on the eve of the World Athletics Championships that his country's banishment from the sport was accurate and that he was determined to combat doping.
He said he felt sorry for "all the athletes who have been snatched gold and silver medals in competitions."
Despite its comments, the IAAF Congress voted in favour of maintaining the ban imposed in November 2015 after a World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) report found evidence of state-sponsored doping in Russia.
"I thought it was a very open response today, a very honest presentation," IAAF President Coe told reporters.
“The entire council and the entire Congress were pleased that the Russian Federation has realized in itself that it has experienced some pretty hot times and will do everything possible to ensure that the federation is redesigned.”
Coe said he was also "pleasured" that Russia was accepting the criteria for its reintroduction.
"I think it's been a very constructive day, and I think that progress has been made, but Congress supports the task force's recommendations that this isn't the moment to reinstate Russia," Coe said.
“We have to do everything we can over the next few months to normalize this situation.
The guiding principle has always been that we wanted to separate the clean athletes from the depraved system," he added
Nineteen Russian athletes will compete at the World Athletics Championships, which begin in London on Friday, as neutral actors following the strict IAAF criteria.
The head of IAAF-Task Force, Rune Andersen, said on Monday that Russia still had to meet some of the criteria for reinstatement.
Drug tests are still inadequate and banned coaches are still working freely, he told reporters in London following a lecture at the IAAF Council.
Mourning act for soldiers crashed in Mali
At a funeral service in Fritzlar in northern Hesse, around 1000 people of the two soldiers who died in a helicopter crash in Mali commemorated.
The ceremony took place on Thursday in the cathedral city under high security precautions.
Defense Minister Ursula von der Leyen (CDU) said nothing about the still unexplained cause of the crash.
However, she described the two perished pilots as "an expert in their field".
"We bow to this highest sacrifice they have made on behalf of the Federal Republic of Germany," she explained.
The 33-year-old major and 47-year-old staff captain from northern Hesse had died a week ago on Wednesday in West African Mali.
Their coffins were transferred to Germany and on Thursday accompanied by a Bundeswehr wake to Fritzlar Cathedral Sankt Peter.
At noon, only the relatives in the church said goodbye.
Afterwards, the funeral service began in the circle of the family, closest friends, representatives of the Bundeswehr as well as politics.
Among them were Hesse's Prime Minister Volker Bouffier (CDU) and the inspector general of the Bundeswehr Volker Wieker.
Mourners laid down roses, the relatives stood arm in arm on the coffins.
In front of the church, Fritzlarer and numerous soldiers followed the ceremony on a canvas.
Defence Minister von der Leyen described the perished soldiers as "highly decorated" and operational experience.
The chief of staff had helped set up the flight operations of the Tiger attack helicopter in Fritzlar and was one of his regiment's first six-trained tiger pilots.
Volker Bauersachs, commander of the regiment to which the soldiers belong, also declared: "If this occupation could not avert the crash, then no one would have been able".
Minister von der Leyen did not show any doubts about the UN mission in Mali that the operation was dangerous.
But he is also of high importance "for a country from which we are separated only by two states," she said.
After the funeral service, the coffins were taken out of the cathedral.
When and where they will be buried, the Bundeswehr did not specify.
Prince Henrik doesn't want a tomb next to his wife
The man of Denmark's Queen Margrethe (77), Prince Henrik (83), does not want to be buried alongside his wife after his death.
This was announced by the royal house on Thursday.
Traditionally, the Danish royal couple is buried in Roskilde's cathedral church.
The sculptor Bjørn Nørgaard already made a sarcophagus for the two.
The queen agreed with her husband's decision, the notice said.
Where Prince Henrik will be buried is still unclear.
However, he had no intention of being buried in his French homeland.
Princt Henrik had never made a secret of the fact that he was not happy with his title.
After ascending the throne of his wife Margrethe in 1972, he would have liked to become king.
He retired in 2016 and also drops the title "Prinzgemahl".
Since Henrik did not get the title and the function he wanted in Denmark, he did not consider himself an equivalent to his wife - even after death, explained Lene Balleby, the chief spokeswoman of the Danish royal family.
Where the 83-year-old's final resting place will ultimately be, is still unclear.
However, he did not intend to be buried in his French homeland, but in Denmark.
He likes the country he's worked for over the past 50 years.
Henri de Laborde de Monpezat, son of a French noble family, had met Margrethe in London in 1965.
He worked on the Thames at the French Embassy.
During his later wife, the sympathetic expressions of the people were always flowing, Prince Henrik had a difficult stand in his nearly 50-year reign with the Danes.
Speaking an autobiography, Henrik said of the first years in Denmark: "A few months after my arrival, everything I did was criticised.
My Danish was fluctuating, I preferred wine instead of beer, silk stockings instead of knitting socks, Citroën instead of Volvo, tennis instead of football.
Even for the Gauloises, whom I smoked instead of Virginia tobacco and who had a reputation in this country for being the brand of socio-critical intellectuals, I couldn't hope for leniency.
I've been different.
I seemed to be happy with that position and not to be embarrassed.
These were two mistakes!”
Prime Minister Weil wants swift new election
CDU and FDP facing possible majority in Lower Saxony
Lower Saxony's Prime Minister Stephan Weil (SPD) is after the loss of the red-green government majority for a quick new election of the state parliament, but he does not want to resign.
Weil spoke out in favour of the dissolution of parliament on Friday.
Previously, the surprise exit of a Green MP from their state parliamentary group in Lower Saxony had plunged the government of Prime Minister Stephan Weil (SPD) into a serious crisis.
Five months before the official date of the state election, the red-green coalition in Hanover lost its narrow one-vote majority in parliament to the opposition from the CDU and FDP.
Former Green MP Elke Twesten justified her move with the non-nomination for the 2018 election in her constituency in Rotenburg (Wümme).
"I see my political future in the CDU," she declared in Hanover on Friday.
CDU group leader Björn Thümler wants to recommend his group to take them in.
CDU and FDP would have a total of 69 seats in the Lower Saxony state parliament, the SPD and the Greens 68 seats.
So far the ratio has been reversed.
The new state parliament is regularly elected on 14 January next year.
Thümler said the red-green state government must now decide whether it could continue to govern in this situation without a majority.
Stephan Weil, however, considers it essential that the Landtag decides its self-dissolution as quickly as possible.
The CDU group is expected to decide on its further action on Tuesday.
The state constitution also provides for the possibility that the state parliament deprives the prime minister of confidence and elects a successor.
“Our Constitution offers several options.
These options need to be checked legally cleanly," Thümler said.
“One will calmly discuss all questions if they are legally sensibly audited.”
Thümler called Twesten's move "yet somewhat curious."
The politician herself insisted: "I'm not a traitor.
"I feel very good."
She described herself as a supporter of black-and-green.
The Greens urged the renegade MP for the return of their state parliament mandate.
"Of course, we assume that that she will return her state parliament mandate, which she has received via the green state list, with immediate effect," state chairmen Meta Janssen-Kucz and Stefan Körner said.
The leader of the Green Party Anja Piel said: "We regret the decision of Elke Twesten extraordinarily".
She had consciously decided not to have a debate in the group.
“Even against the background that there were no substantive differences, we cannot understand this step.”
SPD chancellor candidate Martin Schulz denounces the conversion of Lower Saxony's Green MP Elke Twesten to the CDU as betrayal.
"The behavior of the former Green MPs in Lower Saxony is not only betrayal of the voters, but also betrayal of red-green," wrote the head of the Social Democrats on Facebook on Friday.
He supports the decision of Prime Minister Stephan Weil (SPD) to quickly re-elect the state parliament.
The departure of Twesten costs red-green in Lower Saxony the majority.
"In her injured vanity and frustration, the politician places her personal interests before the good of the country," criticized SPD Secretary-General Hubertus Heil.
“It’s a scandal that the CDU in Lower Saxony participates in this dirty intrigue game and tries to make political capital out of it.”
Lower Saxony's CDU parliamentary leader Björn Thümler wants to recommend his deputies to include Elke Twesten in the CDU.
Thomas Oppermann, the SPD’s parliamentary group leader in the Bundestag, accused Twesten of distorting the result of the 2013 state election with personal career thinking.
The CDU was at that time the strongest force before the SPD, but the SPD and the Greens had a wafer-thin majority of one vote in the state parliament.
Minister of Agriculture in an interview - How do we protect ourselves from poison eggs, Mr Schmidt?
Germany is discussing contaminated eggs.
How did the scandal happen?
How can consumers protect themselves?
Agriculture Minister Christian Schmidt (CSU) is in an interview with the editorial network Germany (RND).
Mr. Schmidt, how can consumers protect themselves from poisoned eggs?
I can only encourage all consumers to take a close look at the eggs in your refrigerator and check the stamp imprint.
On the consumer protection portal www.Lebensmittelwarning.de, the federal states provide the latest information on the affected egg batches.
Affected eggs should be destroyed or returned.
Even if, according to everything we currently know, a health hazard is practically excluded.
Is Aldi now taking over the best possible crisis response - all eggs out of the offer - because the central role of the reconnaissance officer isn't really perceived by the Federal Minister of Agriculture?
Food monitoring is the responsibility of the federal states.
Nevertheless, my house turned up immediately and has been in close contact with the authorities of the countries since Germany became aware.
All information and risk assessment are available to consumers.
The trade, like the manufacturers, has to ensure that the products sold are marketable and harmless to health.
If the maximum residue level of 0.005mg/kg is exceeded for eggs, they are not marketable.
However, unencumbered eggs can be sold and consumed.
This time it's not the businesses to blame.
Are manufacturing companies and consumers in the same boat?
According to the current state of knowledge, the production companies are not directly to blame.
Rather, they have to expect economic damage due to presumably illegal practices by service companies.
As always, there are too few inspectors in an emergency.
Is there only a lack of staff or conclusive responsibility?
After all, it was the official controls that uncovered the process.
However, the truth is that food fraud and criminal energy can never be 100 percent excluded.
Precisely for this reason, a consistent clarification of such events and the handover to the public prosecutor’s offices are of decisive importance in the cases in which criminal offences may exist.
I have already made it clear on several occasions that I am expecting from the responsible authorities for a complete investigation.
Is regular alarmism in the case of food scandals really necessary?
Consumers have the right to obtain only safe and marketable food.
When an unapproved insecticide is used in the food industry, consumers are rightly outraged.
Thanks to the means, manufacturers, dealers and consumers have fallen into a situation that is absolutely not necessary.
In principle, it must be clear: the unlawful use of the insecticide Fipronil in the husbandry of food-supplied animals is not acceptable and must be terminated immediately.
This must be carried out at all levels consistently and with high pressure.
I expect all competent authorities to clarify the facts swiftly and completely.
How football's financial rules are affecting Neymar's move to Paris
The rules aimed at preventing unhindered spending by European football clubs won't bother Neymar's world-record transfer from Spain's Barcelona to France's Paris-Saint Germain.
Any consequences are yet to come for Paris Saint-Germain from UEFA, because the governing organisation that overseen the European game has the power to ban teams from accessing the prestigious Champions League tournament.
Financial fair-play rules launched by UEFA in 2011 mean PSG must at some point demonstrate that the transfer of Neymar was funded without major losses.
Barcelona has paid €222 million (€262 million) to buy the Brazilian out of his contract, and Neymar will receive a salary that will amount to several tens of millions of dollars a year.
Here are some of the financial aspects of Neymar's move to France.
PSG can almost certainly loosen up the money for Neymar to sign and take him to a galaxy of other stars, in light of the team's energy-ridden ruling family of Qatar.
Clubs are allowed to spend a lot of money on the acquisition of players, but have to counteract legitimate sources of revenue, which allow them the break-even point in their football-related business.
FFP was conceived by Michel Platini after his election as UEFA president in 2007.
The former French player believed clubs that had taken on enormous debts in their pursuit of success effectively defrauded and risked ruining themselves if banks or owners withdrewed their support.
In 2011, FFP shifted from focusing on the club's debt to monitoring their losses.
The changing meant the business model that Roman Abramovich originally used at Chelsea in 2003 - buying a club with potential and then invested vigorously to improve the squad and build a global brand - should become a tougher affair.
During the current three-year FFP assessment period, which runs until 2018, clubs can accept losses of 30 million euros.
And even though Platini was disgraced from the presidency in 2015, his UEFA successor Aleksander Ceferin remains committed to deterring excessive spending.
"Certainly FFP is not dead and we will certainly strengthen the rules for the future," Andrea Traverso, who is responsible for FFP at UEFA, told The Associated Press last week.
Although PSG has paid Neymar's buy-out clause in a lump sum, it can distribute the amount for accounting over the duration of his contract.
Neymar has signed a five-year contract, so that the transfer fee for the next five years can be reported in the annual financial statements of €44.4 million.
The first wave of FFP sanctions in 2014 has hit PSG along with Abu Dhabi, which is owned by Manchester City with the heaviest sanctions.
PSG received a €60 million fine (then €82 million), which was later lowered to €20 million - and it was ordered that the team members for the 2014/15 Champions League would remain limited to €21 instead of the otherwise normal €25 players.
UEFA is looking for clubs that could try to outsmart its financial oversight by inflating its earnings through companies associated with a club's owners.
While a Qatari company could sponsor PSG if the value of sponsorship were to exceed standard market rates, UEFA would not consider this a legitimate income.
That's exactly what happened in 2014, when UEFA decided the value of PSA's sponsorship had been bloated by the Qatar Tourism Authority.
It has been reported that part of PSG payments to Neymar could come via payments from the organizing committee of the 2022 World Cup in Qatar.
But PSG and Qatar 2022 have denied that Neymar will become World Cup ambassador and such a manoeuvre for UEFA regulators could easily be figuring out.
The previous sanctions against PSG mean UEFA will be even more attentive to observing any attempt to circumvent the rules.
UEFA had no preparatory talks with PSG on Neymar's transfer, the people familiar with the situation said on Thursday.
They spoke to The Associated Press under condition of anonymity as they were unauthorised to discuss the PSG situation.
The latest published reports show that PSG achieved more than €10 million in 2015/€16 with sales of €542.4 million.
The accounts for the last financial year have yet to be released.
PSG expect Neymar's arrival is a boon for sponsorship deals, merchandising and jersey sales.
The association expects turnover to increase by at least 30 per cent after signing.
The Spanish league was clearly irritated to see one of the best football players go and it tried to prevent Neymar's departure by claiming that PSG would hurt the FFP.
"It's a clear case of "financial doping" with the club and the state," La Liga president Javier Tebas told Spanish daily AS in reference to Qatar's ultimate ownership of PSG.
Arsene Wenger, whose club is owned by an American sports tycoon, shares La Liga's reservations against a state that finances a club.
"It's the consequence of ownership and that's completely changed the entire landscape of football over the last 15 years," Wenger said Thursday.
“As soon as a country owns a club, anything is possible.
It's going to be very difficult to respect financial fairplay because you can go different ways or have different interests when a country has such a big player to represent a country."
Swissh is looking for a new chocolate supplier
In recent years, Chocolat Frey has produced on-board chocolate for Swiss.
Now the sweet cooperation is facing a possible end: the airline rewrites the order.
Swiss spokeswoman Meike Fuhlrott confirmed at the request of the news agency sda a corresponding report of the newspaper "Nordwestschweiz" on Friday.
According to current planning, the airline will conclude a new contract in the first half of 2018.
The products would be inspected every two to three years and subjected to quality control.
"We are very satisfied with the current supplier Chocolat Frey," the spokeswoman said.
Around 17 million small red-and-white packaged Schoggi plaques at 14 grams distribute the flight attendants per year to the passengers before landing.
Swiss did not want to provide any information on the costs.
Industry insiders estimate the order volume according to "Northwestern Switzerland" at 1.5 to 2 million Swiss francs - the Migros subsidiary generates an annual turnover of around one billion Swiss francs.
But the advertising effect is always more important: For a Schoggi manufacturer, it is a prestigious order.
Swiss does not want to reveal how many applicants there are in the new tender.
However, only Swiss manufacturers are eligible.
According to the newspaper report, Chocolat Frey wants to strive for a renewal of the contract beyond the spring of 2018.
For the sda, the largest Swiss chocolate manufacturer was not reachable for comment.
Since 2006, Swiss has been distributing chocolate panels aboard its flights.
Before Swiss concluded a contract with Migro's subsidiary, Barry Callebaut had delivered the chocolate tablets in collaboration with Hug.
Swiss introduced two and a half years agove the opportunity to place the logo with Chocolat Frey with the start of its collaboration.
The airline collaborates with various Swiss chocolate producers.
On long-haul flights, First Class customers are pampered with treats from Confiserie Sprüngli, in business class passengers snack on Schoggi from Lindt and in the Economy Class Schoggi by Chocolat Frey.
Swiss Schoggi from Chocolat Frey also distributes on the short-haul route, both in business and economy class.
Athletics - Discus Olympic champion Robert Harting reaches World Cup final
The three-time world champion will fight for a medal in Saturday's final.
His brother Christoph, Olympic champion from 2016, had failed in the national World Cup elimination and could only watch the discus competition from the stands.
Also with the first attempt, Polish defending champion Piotr Malachowski, who kicked 65.13 meters wide, qualified for the final fight.
Nostalgia goes into the niche "Wet Hot American Summer" and "Comrade Detective"
This repetition of the story is simultaneously nostalgic because of the glorious days of the characters of the 80's, for the '90's grunge era (when thought leaders David Wain and Michael Showalter shot the sketch comedy "The State" for MTV), and for themselves - that's, for 2001, before their stars, such as Elizabeth Banks, Amy Poehler and Paul Rudd went to bigger things.
As a cultural artifact, it's equally a parody and an embodiment of our Facebook-enabled era of permanent memories.
But as a franchise, it feels like time's becoming tight.
"First Day of Camp" worked surprisingly well, not just because of its absurd humour (it contained an original story for a talking tin of vegetables spoken by H. Jon Benjamin).
By stiffing at the core of the gag - middle-aged actors play horny children growing up - they nevertheless managed the impossibility of winning back the last part of the joke.
"Ten Years Later," with its adults now playing adults, is less emotionally rooted and its parody goes more according to the watering can principle, including a reef on the stalker movies of the 90's like "The Hand That Rocks the Cradle."
At one point, a character browses through an Apple powerbook, some inline skates and "a number of other things from 1991."
Many of the central stories are the same.
The gracious co-op (Mr. Showalter), now novelist, still hasn't forgotten Katie (Marguerite Moreau).
Victor (Ken Marino) still hasn't lost his virginity.
And there's another crazy-violar plot involving Ronald Reagan (including Mr. Showalter), who is now backed by George H. W. Bush (a good-happy impression of Michael Ian Black).
As usual, the excursions are the best part of the franchise.
It's a lot of fun to see the eternally young Mr Rudd, who shows Matt Dillon's lower lip beard from "Singles," whose former big-man-on-camp Andy now feels like an old cracker.
It's the moments of random madness that could fuel this franchise like a kind of repetitive "Seven Up!" series, until it achieves what I'm adopting as its logical conclusion: "Wet Hot American Summer: Twenty Years Later," whose final scene will show the gang will attend the film premiere of "Wet Hot American Summer."
"Comrade Detective" on Amazon, meanwhile, is reminiscent of another kind of nostalgia: the revival of the Cold War of "The Americans," the film "Atomic Blonde," and even "GLOW," with its east-to-West wrestling iconography.
Created by Brian Gatewood and Alessandro Tanaka with Rhys Thomas as the director, claims to be a found TV series: a Romanian drama of the eighties with the two policemen as friends, two officers from Bucharest who fight for truth, atheism, and the path of Marxism-Leninism.
The starring roles - a hard-boiled Gregory and an idealistic Iosef - are played by Florin Piersic Jr. and Corneliu Ulici, whose dialogues are dubbed by Channing Tatum (also an executive producer) and Joseph Gordon-Levitt.
It's a brilliant idea.
But it's not much more than an idea.
There are some nice gags to watch (the police spend time watching chess on TV, as Americans might do with football).
And the list of famous speakers - including Jenny Slate, Nick Offerman, Mahershala Ali and Chloë Sevigny - makes for a decent game 'Find out the voice'.
But "Comrade Detective" is so committed to the veracity of a desperate propaganda drama - a murderer wearing a Reagan mask is included - that it often plays like a single one.
With six long episodes, it drags on, and the comedy isn't fast or frequent enough.
"Comrade Detective," which has been redacted to a smaller length, could better deliver its agitprop satire, as Gregor and Iosef repeatedly visit the American embassy, whose lobby is always staffed by two fat men devouring a bunch of hamburgers.
The message for the imagined communist audience is that Western culture can make everything the food for a feast.
The fictional Romanian creators were not wrong: we even do it with our own recent history and with its own.
Breach against drug mafia in the country
The police have managed a blow against the Italian mafia in Baden-Württemberg.
On Wednesday morning, numerous objects were searched in the Schwarzwald-Baar-Kreis, in Rottweil, Konstanz, Esslingen and Stuttgart, as the prosecutor’s officence announced on Friday.
15 defendants between the ages of 25 years and 77 were arrested and arrest warrants have already been issued.
In addition, the officials ensured larger quantities of marijuana and cocaine, firearms and several hundred thousand euros.
Two people were arresteded during simultaneous raids in Italy.
There were German arrest warrants against the two suspicious Italians.
Therefore, the two will probably be extradited to Germany.
Italian and Bavarian investigators were involved in the action.
In total, more than 300 police officers were deployed.
The members of the mafia are accused of organized drug crime, serious acts of violence, attempted murder and robbery and extortion.
Since the summer of 2016, the Prosecutor’s officent in Konstanz has been investigating organised drug trafficking in southern Germany.
Between the public prosecutor’s offices in Constance and Palermo there was a lively exchange and the investigators of Kripo Rottweil had almost daily contact with their colleagues in Sicily.
Therefore, the large-scale operation on Wednesday morning was also accompanied by four officials from Palermo.
The investigations were mainly targeted by Italian nationals with a close connection to the Italian mafia.
An incident on May 27 in Hüfingen, where several shots were fired into a restaurant early in the morning, is also likely to go to the gang's account.
Wise laments big gaps in foreigner's register
"Data from citizens who shouldn't show up": According to a report, the foreigners' register in Germany has considerable deficiencies.
This leads to sometimes serious wrong decisions in asylum procedures.
According to a media report, Frank-Jürgen Weise, the Federal Government’s Refugee Management Commissioner has drawn attention to large gaps in the central register of foreigners.
Deficiencies in the data registered there could lead "to some serious wrong decisions" in asylum procedures and deportations, Weise warned in an analysis available to the Süddeutsche Zeitung.
According to the data, ten million people with foreign nationality are recorded, including about 5.7 million from non-EU countries.
The data is therefore managed by about 600 immigration authorities in Germany.
Because the data had not been adequately maintained, the data quality in parts had not been good so far.
In individual cases, data from 1921 had been found "from people who are no longer alive, or from citizens who have become German citizens and should actually no longer appear in the register".
"Faulty data inputs can significantly slow return processes," the report continued.
In addition, EU citizens who do not have to leave are included in the statistics on people subject to departure.
However, such false assignments "led to a distorted debate about the handling of those obliged to leave," according to the analysis.
Meanwhile, the federal government has apparently begun sending refugee families back to Italy with children under the age of 16 if they have entered from there.
This is according to an internal paper of the Federal Office for Migration and Refugees (BAMF), reports the “Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung”.
The paper states that the BAMF carries out such procedures “concerning families with children – with the exception of infants and small children – to Italy since 1 June 2017.
Since the end of 2014, this was practically no longer possible, according to the newspaper with reference to the difficult situation on the ground.
Under the Dublin agreement, the EU state is responsible for refugees, which they reach first.
If a migrant travels on to another EU country, that state can send it back to the country of entry.
"In the meantime, the capacity for families with minor children in Italy has increased significantly".
Italy has issued guarantees for the admission and accommodation of families.
Even the European Court of Human Rights (ECtHR) considers these commitments sufficient.
Other EU states would also send refugees back there under the Dublin Agreement.
Ulla Jelpke, spokeswoman for the left-wing political group in the Bundestag, criticized this decision: "Families with children threaten because of deficiencies in the Italian asylum system illegal reception conditions and a threat to the best interests of children".
Second best result achieved
A total of 220 liters of people living in Oberreichenbach were already provided by the blood donation service for other people.
At least of the twelve citizens who were honoureed on Friday.
Especially Jürgen Greule, who now shared 50 liters of blood with his 100 donations.
Traditionally, the municipality pays tribute in the meeting of the municipal council to the multiple readiness to donate blood.
"It's ingenious how our body is designed for it and we pay tribute to the donors who provide their blood to strangers," underlined Mayor Karlheinz Kistner.
"A single blood donation helps several people," explained Holger Maisenbacher, head of readiness of the German Red Cross (DRC), because the preserves are decomposed into their components.
For example, three people could benefit from a blood donation.
At the same time, Maisenbacher reported on the actions that will be carried out in Oberreichenbach in
"This year, we achieved the second-best result with 114 donors," the DRC district readiness manager said.
Obviously, the move to the Theaterhaus a few years ago led to higher numbers of participants.
Even before that, Thomas Bockstadt, Susanne Reichle, Sandra Schöck, Thomas Reichle and Meta Umbeer were among the multiple donors and were awarded for 50 blood donations each.
Herbert Mair and Dorothee Riexinger recorded half as many, while Tamara Pfrommer, Petra Schleeh, Stefanie Stoll and Uwe Vetter each gave 500 milliliters of their life juice ten times.
The Bank of England will keep interest rates at 0.25%
The Bank of England is expected to announce later that interest rates will remain unchanged after weake economic growth and weakening of inflation dampened speculation about a rise.
The Bank of England has warned that economic growth will remain "sluggish" as it maintained interest rates on family incomes in the midst of intensifying pressure.
Monetary Committee (MPC) policy-makers of the bank's (MPC) voted by 6 to 2 to keep interest rates at 0.25%, with fewer members aiming for a rise this month as weakened support for an increase.
In its quarterly inflation report, the bank lowered its growth forecasts for 2017 to 1.7% and to 1.6% for 2018, warning that the pressure on household incomes would continue, with inflation expected to rise to just under 3% in the autumn.
But she signalled that interest rate hikes would become necessary over the next few years to curb inflation fueled by Brexit and she said that borrowing costs might have to rise more than expected in financial markets.
Members also voted to withdraw part of the mammoth stimulus package issued a year ago after Brexit.
She will demand time for the funding plan to provide cheaper funding for banks from next February, although it has been said that it is now expected to provide £15bn more under this system - with £115bn.
Minutes after deciding on the interest rate, the bank said: "In MPC's central forecast, gross domestic product (GDP) remains weak in the short term, as pressure on household real incomes continues to weigh on consumption."
On interest rates, she reiterated that "a certain tightening of monetary policy" would be needed to cool inflation and a "slightly larger" scale than markets expect.
Markets are currently forecasting the first increase in the third quarter of next year and another in 2020.
However, the bank stressed that increases would take place "gradually" and "limited."
The bank's downward-corrected growth forecasts for this year and next are compared to the 1.9% and 1.7% projected in May.
It has maintained its growth forecast to 1.8% in 2018.
The pound fell against the dollar and the euro after this news.
The pound fell 0.5% to $1.31 and fell 0.4% to €1.11.
The decision to make no change comes after the latest disappointing growth figures dampened rising expectations for a rate hike and GDP in the second quarter has risen from 0.2% in the previous three months to 0.3%.
Growth is expected to be 0.3% in the third quarter, although it is expected to recover slightly after the bank towards the end of the year.
The latest inflation report offered little enthusiasm for households affected by rising inflation and minor pay rises, as tightening becomes even worse at first before it will then improve.
She added that monetary policy over the next few years cannot "prevent" the income slump, but she added that wages will "significantly" recover at the end of its three-year forecast.
The economy is also boosted by rising demand for British goods, due to the weak pound, which will compensate for part of lower consumer spending.
The decision comes a year after cutting interest rates to 0.25% last August after the shocking EU vote that caused the pound's fall.
The bank estimates it is likely to take four years for this to fully reach prices and the economy.
Ahead of the bank's last verdict on interest rates, the call for a rate hike had risen as inflation boosted pressure on hard-harassed households, with three policymakers demanding a rise to 0.5% in July.
Inflation has dropped to 2.6% last month from 2.9% in June, although the bank said this was expecteded and it is expected to rise again in the coming months.
The bank said the overreach relative to its 2% target was attributable "quite" to the weak pound.
Andrew Montlake, director at Coreco Mortgage Brokers, said: "Given the recent decline in swap rates, the mortgage market is likely to become more competitive again as lenders loudly look for businesses and end the year in a positive way.
The market looks especially good for those where the existing deals expire and take out the new mortgages, and many with toll-free options and low rates are becoming increasingly popular."
NHS (National Health Service) to prescribe controversial HIV drug 10,000 people
Patients will be given access to "transformative" HIV prevention treatment from September following a fight in the High Court against funding the drug at the NHS.
NHS England announced that pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) taken before intercourse, which can reduce the risk of infection in high-risk patients by around 86 percent, is now being delivered by the health service over an initial three-year study for an estimated 10,000 people.
He agreed to fund the trial after the Court's appeal last Year upheld a Supreme Court ruling that the NHS had the authority to pay for the PrEP despite claiming local authorities were supposed to fund it.
The move announced on Thursday was greeted by NHS England boss Simon Stevens as an "important new intervention," while the National Aids Trust (NAT) described this as a "crucial moment."
Sexual health clinics in London, Brighton, Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield will be among the first to allow people to register, and more clinics will follow in October before it will be launched across England in April 2018.
Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) is a consequence of HIV preparations taken before sex to reduce the risk of HIV infection.
Studies have shown that PrEP significantly reduces HIV transmission in people at risk of infection.
Taking PrEP before intercourse means that there are just enough medication in a person's body to prevent HIV infection.
Such medications can be taken every day or so two hours before intercourse.
Those eligible for treatment include gay or bisexual men, transgenders, and individuals with HIV-positive partners who have not been successfully treated.
In clinics, appropriate participants are identified who agree to the study, including men, women, transgenders and individuals who have a partner whose HIV status is not known to be controlled by antiretroviral treatment.
People who live in England and are registered with a family doctor can also sign up for possible participation at their local participating sex clinic.
The programme will become £10 million and NHS England signed a contract to deliver the drugs this week following an international competitive procurement.
The study aims to assess the full potential of PrEP by collecting clinical evidence of how it is used, recorded and implemented on a large scale.
Stevens said: "This great new intervention should complement and overhaule the far-reaching and increasingly successful efforts to prevent HIV.
It's another milestone in the more than three-decade progress in addressing one of humanity's greatest health challenges."
Deborah Gold, chief executive at NAT (National Aids Trust), said: "This is a pivotal moment in the fight against HIV.
PrEP, if it is specifically targeted to needy and vulnerable people, offers the opportunity to change the English HIV epidemic.
From September, people at high HIV risk will have access to this NHS-funded attempt in England to develop a new instrument that is truly individually controlled and is not subject to a negotiation with a partner, resulting in the improvement of many, many lives.
We sincerely welcome this announcement.”
Ian Green, chief executive of Terrence Higgins Trust, said: "The priority must now be that the study is carried out quickly across the country and that no one who is at risk of HIV will be left behind.
Now that PrEP test medicine has been procured, we are on track to protect more than 10,000 HIV-infected people.”
Sharon Hodgson, shadow health minister, said: "The beginning of the PrEP process is welcome and overdue after months of delays and hesitant implementation by the government.
The evidence shows how transformative this drug can be as part of our approach to HIV-prevention and end the transmission of this life-changing infection.
This attempt brings us one step closer to understanding the benefits of PrEP.
Now it is important that this study is carried out as soon as possible throughout the country to protect individuals who are HIV-infected, and that it will help us get a step closer to ending the spread of HIV in society.
Living the big life: Mezzanine and clever designer tricks have turned this little Earls Court apartment into a generous home
Joanne Leigh, a former banker with a passion for real estate, shrinked from a large house in Knightsbridge to an Earls Court apartment in 2014.
It's located on the first floor of an sleek Queen Anne terrace - and it's a testament to the new design that Joanne hasn't packed and sold her bags.
"Before that, I lived with my ex at Knightsbridge in a much larger room," Joanne says.
“I was looking for something comparable to my price.
I've been shown this apartment in Earl's Court, and she had the same high ceilings.
I fell in love with them and made an offer that day.”
Since the first visit, the 1,000-square-foot apartment has been completely overhauled.
Originally a one-bedroom property with a nested layout - you had to walk through the kitchen to get into the bedroom - Joanne wanted to add storage space and a mezzanine to make optimal use of the generous ceiling height.
"I wanted high ceilings, big windows, a lot of light.
That's my requirement," she says.
To this end, she commissioned Erfan Azadi from Notting Hill’s architecture and design studio Duck & Shed to use the 12.5-foot-high ceilings and create a home that was not only a development but also a place to live for Joanne.
The ceiling wasn't large enough to place two rooms on top of each other, so the architectural challenge was to create a mezzanine in which one could stand.
Erfan had to think creatively in order to solve the stipulation.
"The steel mill is quite complicated," he explains.
"We had to arrange the rooms in such a way that they were stacked on top of each other without having to bend down.
It wasn’t just about building a simple platform, it’s about putting on the steel in such a way that it had the right height for the function above or below.”
This arrangement enabled Erfan to place two rooms on top of each other in full - with a lot of extra drama.
For the mezzanine, a classic copper light window from Retrouvius overlooks the glamorous living area.
Mirrored gold furniture, silver accents and vintage Serge Mouille lighting set the style for the program.
The use of mirrors plays a role in adding theater, space and light.
At both ends, a short corridor is mirrored to make it look longer.
In the kitchen, the reflection feels like a window into another room.
At the entrance, mirrored units offer space for all cutlery and jars from Joanne.
By perceiving a more glamorous space, the beauty of the living room can seal the kitchen when it is not used.
Collapsible kitchens aren’t new.
What's special about this is that it's a garage door.
"A bit of engineering went in there," explains Erfan of the steel-reinforced, veneered MDF door.
"It's counterweight, so when you push up the door, it slides very easily."
Thanks to the clever use of space and storage space, Joanne was able to gain an additional 16 m2, an extra bedroom and an improved layout.
"At the end of the day, you're going to sell the floor space - or how your property is valued," Erfan says.
“In a rudimentary form, a real estate agent will enter and perform a calculation based on soil size.”
Here they have managed to enlarge the floor area and at the same time use high-quality materials that give the room its luxurious advantage.
"The materials are the things that touch you and that therefore help to create an emotional bond in a home," says Erfan.
“You don’t fall in love into the electrical outlets in the walls.
These are important, but they don't play with your heart."
It shows that an apartment can turn into something quite astonishing through good design and increased square footage from something standard.
In fact, like Joanne, you might decide not to leave these.
Brethren fight over a £500 million real estate empire
Two brothers find themselves four decades after their Italian family founded the dynasty, in an altercation over one of Victoria's largest real estate empires worth over $500 million.
Former director of 'Carlton-Football-Club' and developer Colin De Lutis lives in a villa in Toorak, just three doors away from his younger brother Paul, who has taken him to the Supreme Court to clarify how their property and business assets are to be divided.
Paul had wanted to withdraw from the family business since 2014, but a sharp dispute over the break-up takes place in the Supreme Court.
Together with their mother Anna, the brothers own 38 properties worth more than half a billion dollars, accumulated since the family bought a shop on Smith Street, Collingwood, in 1973.
There, the Westco family started jeans and grew into a chain of 160 retail stores.
It sold for $85 million in 1999.
This money has been invested in real estate, De Luti's portfolio now includes offices in the city centre, Brunswick and Sorrento's development areas, the apartments in Gold Coast and Mt Buller, as well as shopping malls in suburbs.
According to an agreement with her deceased father, Colin - described by Paul as "main operator and the dominant personality" in the De Lutis family - owns about two-thirds of the company's assets and Paul about a third.
A court record states that this means Colin owns $316 million worth of property and $176 million worth Paul.
The division of real estate and cash has proven to be a problem for Paul.
"The divisions proposed by Colin have left him with what I thought was the best objects," he wrote in a first affidavit in 2015.
Last Friday, after several failed mediations, the first hearing of the Supreme Court took place.
The brothers are now also fighting for control over the existing business - of which Colin, as Paul claims, excluded him.
Colin argues this is not true, but accuses Paul of incompetently managing some possessions.
In an early affidavit filed by Colin, he says he hopes to settle the dispute.
"My Italian heritage stipulates that the family is the most important priority," he wrote.
Cost rises in the case, with hundreds of pages involving affidavits, emails and reports from companies, including Deloitte, Pitcher Partners and Charter Keck Cramer, and with leading lawyers, including Allan Myers, QC, and law firms held by both sides.
Ms. De Lutis and her late husband Luigi were Italian immigrants who ran milk bars and delicacy businesses from the late 1950's and bought a shop in Brunswick in the early 1970s.
They used security to buy the Collingwood property where Colin opened the family's first denim store.
Colin says he started Westco there when he was "in my final years of high school, when Paul was still a young child (about 10 years old)."
Anna De Lutis says she and Luigi, who died in 2012, hoped to pass on their property amicably to their sons.
"Luigi often said to me that he never wanted the brothers to end up in court," she wrote.
Two other brothers, Mark and Luke, are no longer part of the De Lutis group of companies, with Colin claiming Mark was paid $10 million and Luke £26 million.
Both Lukas and Mark, Paul says, had become "embittered about the terms of their financial separation."
Colin has held back a lot despite being a former AFL board member and, according to a 2015 biography of Eddie McGuire's best friend of the television star.
Fairfax Media contacted Colin and Paul De Lutis and their lawyers.
Only Colin replied via text message to say "I really can't say much" because the case was before the courts.
"It's very, very sad that my brother Paul decided to sue me and move our family name through the courts," he wrote.
In mid-2016, Colin wrote in a letter to the court that he had attempted to meet his brother's demands.
"Our father wouldn't have wanted Paul and I to end up in our current dispute," he said, adding that he was "still hoping for a solution."
On Friday, that hope seemed to be over.
"Our matter will be resolved by the courts in the near future," Colin wrote.
Trump's new chief of staff plans to curtail media abstinence.
Others tried and failed.
Maybe John F. Kelly can actually do it.
If so, he'll be first.
Politico reports that the White House's new chief of staff plans to curtail the flow of information on President Trump - including news media reports - in hopes of keeping the boss on a more even course.
Here's a piece by reporter Josh Dawsey:
“When John F. Kelly, the new White House chief of staff, met with senior executives on his first day, he posed a major problem in President Donald Trump’s White House that he wanted to find into the president’s hands.
Kelly told employees that information should flow about him - whether on paper or in briefings - because the president would make better decisions if given good information."
Kelly's diagnosis makes sense, but others have tried to tame Trump by monitoring his media diet.
President Trump's relationship with television goes back for decades - and now that he's in the White House, his television advertising is still strong.
"If Candidate Trump was upset because of unfair reporting, it was profusive to show him that he received fair coverage from positions to be persuaded," former campaign adviser Sam Nunberg told Politico in February.
Politico's Tara Palmeri wrote at the time that "the key to keeping Trump's Twitter habit under scrutiny, according to six former campaign officials, is to ensure that his personal media consumption includes steady praise."
All right.
But the idea that Trump's Twitter habit was ever "under scrutiny" is ridiculous.
Maybe these campaign officials know something we others don't know - that Trump's tweets would have contained even more fuel without their interventions.
We're probably never going to know anything about the tweets Trump didn't send.
If his collaborators managed to keep trouble away from him a few times, then their efforts were worth it.
But none has consistently been able to prevent Trump from evoking controversy.
Part of the problem is that people in a White House made up of competing parties keep trying to advance their own agenda by presenting Trump material that potentially promotes their views of the world.
Politico - wholly engaged in this story - reported in May on the advisers' preference to provide the president with dubious information for strategic reasons.
This was an example described by reporter Shane Goldmacher:
“Currential and former Trump officials say that Trump, like a volcano, can respond to negative press clippings, especially those with damaging leaks, and that he’s stiffing to figure out where these came from.
That's exactly what happened in late February when someone maliciously gave the president a printed copy of an article from GotNews.com, the website of internet provocateur Charles C. Johnson, accusing Deputy Chief of Staff Katie Walsh of being "the source behind a heap of Leaks" at the White House.
Although Johnson has been permanently banned from Twitter for harassment or that he has offered no concrete evidence or that he has made false accusations in the past and revoked them.
Trump read the article and started questioning employees about Walsh.”
Goldmacher added that the then chief of staff Reince Priebus and White House Secretary-General Rob Porter have "trieded to implement a system to manage and document the paperwork Trump receives."
How'd that work out?
Kelly tries to do the same three months later.
Maybe he's going to prove himself as a more effective manager than Priebus, but Trump is still his impulsive self, and his advisers are still striving for influence.
These immutable factors make Kelly's mission very difficult.
Most Wanted Criminals Get Mail from Europol
Since the address is unknown, the help of the population is requested and hoped.
Polices across Europe are sending (virtual) postcards to dangerous criminals on the run this summer.
Since the addresses of the wanted criminals are naturally not known, clues from the population are hoped and requested.
Europol reported on Friday of the action, which follows the "EU Most Wanted" advent calendar from winter.
The experience has shown that many fugitives are hiding at holiday destinations, Europol said.
21 postcards have been put together for wanted criminals from 21 EU countries.
Since 2016 there is the website www.eumostwanted, more than 2.5 million times it has been viewed.
So far, 36 of the online-seeked people could be arrested, at least eleven of them on the basis of hints after publication on the website.
Travellers negotiate a trench and return to the 'millionaire series'
Some of West Midlands' wealthiest homeowners are once again on a war footing with some determined travellers who have negotiated a trench pulled by the community to reclaim a field in front of their mansions.
Travellers have parked their 20 caravans in a field outside a prosperous street of Solihull called the "millionaire series."
They have brought horses, babies and an army of vans, having only been kicked out in May without being deterred by a ditch unearthed by the council to prevent their incursions.
The tactful group found a gap in the defence facilities and pushed their vehicles through a section that went unhindered to find a way.
Angry residents have afflicted Solihull's council, who said it was "useless to deal with the issue."
An anonymous resident said: "They moved there on Monday night after being forwarded by Bentley Heath, where they threw stones at passers-by.
The Council must be forced by shame to put it into practice as it was useless when dealing with the problem.
Travellers were there in May, were moved on and are now back.
More arrived last night, probably even more today.”
She added: "The Birmingham council knows how to deal with it by holding long instructions to protect their parks.
“I live on Streetsbrook Road and I’m not happy to continue to pay my community tax if the council can’t even stop that.”
One resident who didn't want to be named said: "It's ridiculous, we've only just seen the last round of these travellers, just to be looted by another round.
The numbers have increased this time and you have even seen a horse in the park area."
A spokeswoman for Solihull's council said: "We are aware of an unauthorised camp in the countryside beyond Streetsbrook Road and are working with Solihull's police to move on as soon as possible.
To support our collection of evidence, it is very important that as many residents as possible report anti-social or criminal behavior to the police by calling 101.
This contributes to creating a picture of the situation in which the court must examine when we present our case."
Birkenhof becomes Waldhotel Luise
From the former Hotel Birkenhof becomes the Waldhotel Luise.
The house at the gates of Freudenstadts is currently undergoing renovated and is scheduled to reopen on 1 March next year.
This is good news for the city.
With the closure of the Hotel Birkenhof by Ambiente Betriebsgesellschaft mbH on 13 April 2014, around 30 000 overnight stays per year were lost at that time and also revenues from the tourist tax.
In the autumn of 2014, in view of dramatically increasing numbers of refugees who came to the district, the hotel building was offered to the district office as a collective accommodation, which caused controversial discussions.
The city prevented the accommodation of asylum seekers through the establishment of a development plan to secure the area as a hotel location.
The decision was not uncontroversiable in the local council at the time.
But especially Freudenstadt’s mayor Julian Osswald defended the hotel location and spoke already at that time of serious interested parties in the house.
He was supposed to be right.
The Luisenhotels group with managing director Birgit Scherer based in Bad Bergzabern has acquired the former Hotel Birkenhof at a voluntary auction in Berlin and wants to make it a three-star-plus-category house under the name Waldhotel Luise.
The group has four houses with the Freudenstadt object.
Two of them in Bad Bergzabern and one in Bad Herrenalb.
They are called Hotel Luise, Luisenpark, Parkhotel Luise and now Waldhotel Luise.
At the former Hotel Birkenhof there have been rubble containers in front of the entrance for a long time, and when you enter the house, it becomes clear what extensive renovation work is being carried out.
The floors were torn out, ceilings partly dismantled, old bathtubs removed from the rooms.
The entire hotel building is a single construction site.
There's still a lot of work to be done by March.
"It's actually a nice hotel," says Thomas Scherer in conversation with our newspaper.
Everything is to be renewed in the Black Forest style.
The rooms are equipped with large walk-in showers, the entrance area will be redesigned, new windows will be installed.
Everything should later become "beautifully close to the citizen", as Thomas Scherer expresses himself.
“We want to make a piece of jewelry out of it.”
Swimming pool, wellness area and a meeting room are included.
You don’t want to position yourself at the high level of the hotels in Lauterbad, but already at an upscale downtown level, Scherer emphasizes.
The Waldhotel Luise with its approximately 60 rooms wants to be broad, explains Scherer further.
Both individual holidaymakers, groups or business people should feel comfortable in the hotel.
He had already held talks with OB Julian Osswald and tourism director Michael Krause, according to Scherer.
The ideas of the city are consistent with those of the hotel group.
"We're on the same line."
Asked about the amount of the investments, Thomas Scherer does not want to determine exactly.
Just under a million euros, he reckons.
The outdoor facilities are also brought to the fore, which is already clearly visible.
In a courtyard, a large terrace is created, which will be accessible from the restaurant, which is public.
Thomas Scherer is optimistic: "Freudenstadt is a good market".
U.S. special investigator deploys grand jury
In the Russia affair around U.S. President Donald Trump, special counsel Robert Mueller is said to have set up a so-called grand jury, according to a report.
The panel has begun work in the past few weeks, the Wall Street Journal, citing people familiar with the matter.
The newspaper assessed the move as a sign that Mueller had expanded and intensified his investigation.
CNN reported that there were already initial subpoenas from the panel in connection with a meeting between Trump's eldest son and a Russian lawyer.
U.S. intelligence agencies have long accused the Kremlin of deliberately interfering in the 2016 presidential election campaign.
Mueller is investigating whether there was collusion between Trump's campaign team and Moscow.
The focus of the investigation will be several men who acted as advisers to the Republican in the election campaign.
Mueller also reportedly scrutinized Trump's private and business environment.
The “Washington Post” wrote a few weeks agove that the special counsel had personally put Trump at the center and examined the allegation whether the president may have obstructed the judiciary in the wake of the affair.
The main task of grand judgies is to examine available evidence in possible crime cases and to decide whether to file charges.
Grand juries are also named indictment chambers.
In most cases, they are switched on when it comes to larger and controversial cases.
The procedure is always carried out behind closed doors.
The panel can also be called in by the public prosecutor's office to subpoena people for interrogation under oath.
According to CNN, summons have already been issued that revolve around the encounter between Donald Trump Jr. and a Russian lawyer.
Individuals involved in the meeting had been asked to make a statement, the report said.
In addition, they should provide documents.
In addition to Trump Jr., Trump's adviser and son-in-law Jared Kushner as well as his then campaign chief Paul Manafort were also present at the conversation.
Legal experts said on U.S. television on Thursday evening that the establishment showed that Mueller would not be intimidated in his work and that, if necessary, he wanted to act quickly.
However, it does not necessarily mean that the grand jury will be involved at the end to decide on the filing of an indictment.
Nevertheless, the establishment of the body is the beginning of a new phase in the investigations and a new cause for concern for President Trump.
Amid the new revelations, the president used a speech in West Virginia on Thursday night to portray the Russia affair as a campaign by his political opponents.
"Russia's story is a total invention," Trump said.
“The issue gives Democrats a way to feel good because they don’t have anything else they can talk about.”
His special adviser Ty Cobb stated in a statement that he knew nothing of the appointment of a grand jury.
However, it also usually works in secret, he emphasized.
The White House endorses any action that accelerated the special prosecutor's work "fair."
Former FBI Director James Comey has said three times that the president has not been investigated.
There is no reason to assume that anything has changed, the statement said.
Mueller, however, had only been deployed after Trump fired Comey.
Because of this expulsion, accusations against Trump had become loud that he had wanted to obstruct the judiciary.
Comey testified before a Senate committee in June that Trump asked him for a cessation of investigations against then-national security adviser Michael Flynn.
The president is under great pressure because of the investigation.
According to U.S. media, the White House collected material against the special counsel and his associates.
That sparked speculation as to whether Trump wanted to fire Mueller.
Unlike an independential special counsel to Congress, such as in the Watergate Affairation surrounding President Richard Nixon's later resigned, Mueller has a position in which he could theoretically be fired by the Justice Department at urging the president.
However, several senators from both parties want to prevent such a move.
Two groups of Republican and Democratic senators on Thursday unveiled bills under which the special counsel could no longer be dismissed so easily.
Anctartic: Gigantic iceberg drifts in Antarctica
In West Antarctica, a gigantic iceberg has detached itself from the ice shelf of almost seven times the size of Berlin.
This was announced by the Bremerhaven Alfred Wegener Institute for Polar and Marine Research on Wednesday.
The colossus, which weighs more than a trillion tonnes and weighs 5800 square kilometers, is now drifting north and will probably take two or three years to melt.
With 175 kilometers in length and a width of up to 50 kilometers, it is one of the largest ice giants that researchers have registered in the past three decades.
There is no danger to people from him.
According to researchers, it is unclear to the extent to which climate change played a role in the demolition of the iceberg.
In total, however, there has been a series of unusual break-ups of ice problems in the region for about 30 years, writes the U.S. research center for snow and ice (NSIDC) in a fact background.
According to many researchers, this could be related to global warming.
So far, therefore, the colossus has been part of the Larsen-C shelves.
Shelf ice floats on the sea and is feded by large glaciers.
It therefore grows further and further into the sea and breaks off regularly.
The Larsen-C shelves is located south of South of South America almost at the top of the Antarctic Peninsula.
North of this shelf ice, the balance of waxes and break-off was disturbed a few years ago.
A complete ice shelf has disintegrated there, writes AWI researcher Daniela Jansen.
There is some evidence that the Larsen-C shelves are also in jeopardy, because the front edge has never retreated as far as at present.
In addition, the ice sheet is under tension and can disintegrate if at a key location a bit breaks off.
The melting of ice shelves and iceberg does not raise sea levels as they float on the water and displace ice as much water as it yields after melting itself.
However, the loss of the ice shelf causes the glaciers to flow directly into the sea and can drain much faster than before.
This could possibly increase the sea level, but only to a small extent, writes the British research project for the observation of Antarctica (Midas).
Larsen-C ice shelves is the fourth-largest ice shelves in Antarctica with almost 50 000 square kilometres.
At the transition from the mainland it is up to 700 meters thick, at the leading edge to the free ocean about 200 meters.
There it breaks down regularly.
"Although it's a natural occurrence and we're not aware of any direct connection to climate change, the ice sheet is now coming into a more vulnerable position," said Martin O'Leary of Britain's Swansea University.
"This is the furthest withdrawal since records began," he also confirmed.
Scientists are debating whether the Larsen-C ice shelf will now grow again or decline in the long term and possibly coincide, said his colleague Adrian Luckman, adding: "Our models say it will be less stable."
But this development will take decades.
Even if these researchers do not trace the demolition of this gigantic iceberg to climate change, in the past decades many ice shelves of the Antarctic Peninsula have crumbled.
This long tip of Antarctica stretches towards South America.
According to data from the British Antarctic Survey research programme, the Antarctic Peninsula's ice area has declined by 28 000 square kilometres since the 1950s.
The rise in temperature contributed to the decline.
The Antarctic Peninsula has warmed by around three degrees over the past 50 years and is thus one of the areas in which the temperature rises fastest.
CDU wants house tariff on staff
Because it is becoming increasingly difficult to fill vacancies, the CDU calls for leaving the employers' association in order to become more attractive.
Shortly before the summer break, Kreistag waved through 41 new positions in the administration.
More than 1100 employees and civil servants work for the district administration in Mettmann.
Now another 17 trainees have completed their first working day.
The apprenticeships with the circle are apparently highly sought-after.
Around 620 applications were registered for the places to be awarded this year.
Nevertheless, the district still lacks personnel in order to be able to cope with the increased requirements and tasks.
There are currently still 50 unoccupied positions at the district of Mettmann.
Among other things, doctors and engineers as well as forces of the general administration are sought.
The CDU now wants to take the initiative together with the FDP and the UWG and oppose the shortage of skilled workers in the administration.
According to the wishes of the parties, the administration is to "develop a concept for personnel recruitment and personnel development".
The aim is not only to meet short-term needs for skilled workers, but also to counteract the shortage of applicants in a future-oriented manner.
The overall concept should orient itself internally and externally, show concrete measures for lateral entrants and include a training offensive, demand CDU, FDP and UWG.
It sounds like a good approach.
Because the circle is dealing with personnel problems that you didn’t know before.
Other cities advertise good officials and employees and offer more attractive conditions.
There is talk of a veritable "competition of public employers".
In addition, there is a high number of employees who retire from the service due to illness or age.
It’s high time to act so that there are no more vacancies.
But Klaus-Dieter Völker, leader of the CDU's parliamentary group, goes with his party beyond the joint motion.
He recently caused a big surprise in the Kreistag, when he publicly questioned the personnel cost capping and astounded the other parties.
Peoples want all the possibilities that make it possible to leave the municipal employers' association to be examined.
"If we do not come to a satisfactory solution here in the short term, the district administration with the trade unions - perhaps also in cooperation with the circular cities - should strive for a house tariff," said Völker.
Thus, the CDU tries to pay the employees and civil servants better than it actually envisages the tariff regulations for the public service.
Salaries are currently exactly prescribed, there are increases or more money only if you switch to a higher level, which is based on professional years.
However, the tasks of the circle have grown more and more in recent years.
Only a few weeks ago, new rooms had to be set up, in which employees take care of the provisions of the new prostitute protection law.
Not least due to the increasing number of asylum seekers, District Administrator Thomas Hendele and district director Martin Richter have a problem: they have too little staff.
Italy intensifies the tone of NGO migrant rescue boats
Italy has warned NGOs using lifeboats for migrants in the Mediterranean that they will not be allowed to continue if they don't abide by new rules on their operations.
"If NGOs don't sign a new code of conduct, it's difficult to see how they can continue to work," Interior Minister Marco Minniti said in an interview with Turin daily La Stampa.
Minniti's warning came a day after Italian authorities seized a boat from the German aid organisation 'Jugend Rettet' because their crew had effectively cooperated with traffickers in a way that facilitated illegal immigration.
The charity, which has only been in operation for a year, declined to comment on extensive allegations by Italian prosecutor Ambrogio Cartosio.
"For us, saving human life is and remains our top priority. So we are very sorry that we can't operate in the search and rescue zone at the moment," the organization said in a statement on social media.
“We cannot judge any allegations that are currently being made only after we have collected all the information and can assess the situation.”
The Italian authorities had been overseeing the boat of 'Jugend Rettet', the Iuventa, since October.
It is thought that its crew inflatator migrants will take on board, which will be delivered directly to them by human traffickers, and that the smugglers can depart so that the ships can be reused.
At least such a meeting allegedly took place just 1.3 miles off the Libyan coast, according to the prosecutor's file, the contents of which have been published by Italian media.
The crew are suspected of overseeing the authority of the Italian coast guard, which oversees rescue operations in the zone for humanitarian reasons rather than any other motives.
Under the code of conduct, boats such as Iuventa in particular must have an Italian police officer on board who oversees their activities.
Only three of the nine NGOs involved in search and rescue operations have so far agreed to abide by the code: Save the Children, MOAS from Malta and Pro-Activa Open Arms from Spain.
The latter said on Thursday that the new rules would be considered unnecessary but acceptable, as they would not entail "substantial changes or obstructions" of their bailouts.
Among those who refuse to sign is the Nobel laureate organization Doctors Without Borders (MSF).
Minniti, meanwhile, said the number of migrants arriving in Italy following the rescue efforts had declined sharply in recent weeks, suggestinging that efforts to strengthen the Libyan coastguard and cooperation with local mayors had borne fruit.
"Over the past few days, we've started to see light at the end of the tunnel," the minister said.
He announced plans for further talks with Libyan mayors this month on economic development initiatives and with Chad, Niger and Mali on measures to reduce the number of migrants leaving these countries in hopes of being able to reach Europe.
Rises and Trials
Nine Weilermer bikers, the "Berlin outpost" and two guest riders met in Wachenheim in the Nordpfalz to enjoy trials and sights for four days.
There were three friendly guides available for this purpose.
The accommodation in the Wachenheim valley proved to be a good starting point for the tours.
Wachenheim's castle and wine fest was a welcome extra.
The first tour leaded at hot summer temperatures along the vineyards to the Michaelskapelle above Deidesheim.
This was followed by viewpoints, partly on towers, from which you could look far over the Palatinate Forest and the upstream plains.
In between, there were always longer climbs, for which long trials rewarded.
On the second day we went to Bad Dürkheim to the Saline and the Volksfest Wurstmarkt.
Again, viewpoint lined up at viewpoint, connected by "flovy" trials and moderate climbs.
On the last two days there were again many meters of altitude.
Caves, rocks, towers, castles, ruins and refreshment huts brought welcome breaks.
In Bad Dürkheim, the bikers breathed healing brine in the open-air inhalatorium.
Neustadt was also targeted.
After 173 kilometers and almost 4500 meters of altitude, the group had got to know the Palatinate Forest at its best - sporty and cultural.
Console instead of turf - Bundesliga prepares for e-sport
In the future, will the sports stars be sitting in front of computer consoles, instead of fighting their way across the stadium turf in wind and weather?
Fact: E-sports has long since become a serious industry, a lucrative business - big stars, millions of fans and huge tournaments with high fees included.
This is also recognized by the first Bundesliga clubs, which are increasingly involved in this area.
VfL Wolfsburg started in 2015, followed by FC Schalke 04 and most recently VfB Stuttgart, which founded an esports department in mid-Jululy and signed two professionals for the computer game FIFA 17.
"We hope to inspire completely new young target groups for the VfB," says CEO Jochen Röttgermann.
He is sure "that a number of clubs arise on this train".
Next weekend, the soccer world association FIFA organizes the first "Interactive Club World Cup" in London, in which the gamers of different clubs compete against each other.
In addition to Schalke and Wolfsburg, Manchester City, Paris Saint-Germain or PSV Eindhoven have qualified, for example.
Robin Dutt also sees great potential in e-sports.
Bayer Leverkusen and Werder Bremen's ex-coach now advises an agency for virtual football players.
"Real football, in particular, has to deal with the 14 to 30-year-olds that the young people are still going to the stadium in the future," says the 52-year-old.
And: The clubs tried to conquer all sorts of markets, especially the Asian ones.
With prominent e-sportsmen under contract, you could be present in these markets much more often online than just once a year sending the Bundesliga team to Asia.
Exactly what is it about?
In e-sports, computer games such as "League Of Legends", "Dota 2", "Counterstrike" or the football simulation FIFA are challenged at the competition level.
Leagues in which the pros compete against each other have long established themselves, as in normal sports.
And at the 2022 Asian Games, e-sporting is already part of the official programme.
But is it even a real sport?
For many, the notion is sure to take some getting used to.
Ingo Froböse, too, has dealt with the question for a long time.
For the professor at the Deutsche Sport in Cologne, e-sport is a sport because it is competitive and shows many biological reactions that can also be found in classic sports - for example, increased heart rates, stress responses, hormonal changes.
In addition, it needs cognitive skills, technical and tactical understanding and rapid responsiveness.
Professional gamers should not only train on the console, but also their body and pay attention to the diet.
“The fit player is a good player.”
Froböse, however, also sees problems: "Doping is becoming more and more an issue".
Controls are difficult to carry out in online games, and in the scene the structures of classic sport were missing.
The scientist positively emphasizes the high integrative effect of e-sports.
The players are traveling internationally, that's like a big global family, there are no intercultural obstacles.
A study by the management consultancy Deloitte and the Federal Association of Interactive Entertainment Software (BIU) predicts e-sporting growth for e-sports.
In 2016, €50 million had been implemented in Germany, mainly through advertising, sponsoring and major tournaments.
In three years, sales are expected to be 130 million.
Against the almost 2.4 billion euros of the football Bundesliga, this is little.
However, the e-sport "is no longer far from the other established sports such as handball, basketball or ice hockey," write the Deloitte advisers.
Internationally, many countries are already further along.
Major markets are Asia and the USA, where ex-basketball player Shaquille O`Neal and baseball star Alex Rodriguez participate in the U.S. team NRG eSports.
Hollywood actor Ashton Kutcher invests in eSports betting provider Unikrn.
There is also a lot going on in Europe.
E-sports have been introduced as a teaching subject in a Norwegian school, says Froböse.
In the Netherlands and France, football first division clubs are now obliged to set up an e-sports department, according to Dutt.
"In a European comparison, Germany is lagging behind," explains VfB board member Röttgermann.
To fall only into nostalgia, and transfiguring the old days, is no longer enough today.
It is about preserving the tradition of the association, but at the same time aligning the company with the modern market.
"We live in a digitized world," says Dutt.
“You can’t say that everything stays the way it’s always with football.”
Poison-eggs ade - The egg alternatives for cooking and baking
In the meantime, almost all federal states have been affected by the scandal over poison-loaded eggs.
Aldi Süd and Aldi Nord have reacted and taken all eggs out of sale throughout Germany.
Only eggs are to be taken into the shelves, which demonstrably do not contain fipronil.
Michelle Carter convicted of lyrics pushing Conrad Roy to suicide
A Massachusetts woman has been sentenced to two-and-a-half years for encouraging her boyfriend to commit suicide through texts and calls.
Michelle Carter, now 20, will spend 15 months of her conviction behind bars and five years have been suspended on probation.
Carter was convicted in June of negligent homicidation by Conrad Roy, who took his own life on July 13, 2014.
Roy's father said Thursday at the hearing: "My family is heartbroken.
My son was my best friend.”
Carter was allowed to remain vacancy untilled.
She had been confronted with a maximum sentence of 20 years, but her legal representatives argued that both she and Mr Roy were suffering from a mental illness.
Dressed in red pants and a cream-coloured blouse, Carter stood there with tears in her eyes, folded hands and down-facing eyes when she was convicted.
"Her actions have killed Conrad Roy," the prosecutor told the court.
“She ended his life to make her own better.
She didn't take responsibility," she said.
"She didn't show any remorses."
But Carter's lawyer said it was a "terrible circumstance she utterly regrets."
However, Judge Lawrence Moniz said he didn't find that her "age or degree of maturity, or even her mental illness, have a significant impact on her actions."
One prosecutor reased a statement from Roy's mother Lynn Roy saying there is "not a day when I don't mourn the loss of my beloved son.
I want him to be proud of me and how I deal with everything. I'm trying to be there for his sisters despite all the pain we're going to carry with us for eternity," it says.
Roy's sister, Camdyn Roy, testified that she would be "persecuted" by the realisation that she would never attend her brother's wedding or be an aunt for his children.
"Not having that only person I've been with every day since I was giving is a pain I'm always going to keep with me for the rest of my lives," she said.
The case seems to set a legal precedent as there is no law in Massachusetts that makes it a crime to urge another to commit suicide.
Carter was 17 years old when Roy was founded dead from carbon monoxide poisoning in a vehicle in 2014.
The case attracted national attention after Carter's text messages revealed that she had encouraged him to end his life.
"Hang up, jump from a building, stab yourself, I don't know, there are many ways," she said in several messages she had sent in the two weeks before his death while he was on vacation with his family.
In the moments before his suicide, she wrote, "You have to do it, Conrad" and "All you have to do is turn on the generator and you'll be free and happy."
Judge Moniz said Carter was seeking attention through Roy's death and ordered her to benefit from the case through any press.
The case has shared legal opinion.
Among the few who defended Carter was Amanda Knox, who was convicted in Italy in 2007 for the murder of her British roommate before being acquitted two years ago.
Ms Knox, aged 30, posted a commentation in the Los Angeles Times: "Michelle Carter deserves sympathy and help, not prison."
Joshua Kimmich: Learning from FC Bayern's new mini-lame
It is no coincidence that Joshua Kimmich was allowed to captain at the Audi Cup.
FC Bayern trust the Confed Cup winner to have a big season - extra motivation doesn't hurt there.
There was something there otherwise in his place in the cabin, Joshua Kimmich saw that immediately, even if the extra garment was very, very small.
But since on the second day of the Audi Cup, of course, there was no Philipp Lahm and no Manuel Neuer to be seen, of course, he stooped the tie assigned to him.
"The coach didn't announce it," the Confed-Cup winner recounted afterwards, after his first appearance as Bayern captain.
Well, the debut could have gone better than the 0:2 against SSC Naples.
But Kimmich was able to interpret the sign from the coach as well.
It was not the eight-year-older Arturo Vidal that had been chosen as a game leader in the C-Elf consisting of youth players, debutants and professionals who had just gone out of vacation, but he.
Zuckerl is best distributed to those whom one trusts a lot, whom you want to keep in a mood.
So if you have been chosen to take the legacy of Lahm, you can only use a little extra motivation.
Kimmich confirmed: "That's what you want as a player: take responsibility."
On Wednesday, the 22-year-old raced across the field as a mini-lame.
To the right back, that's now his position, the white bandage on the arm did the rest.
And in general, Kimmich seemed in this starting formation like the one who can take the rudder best in his hands.
He gave instructions, tried to steer the unrehearsed squad.
He had the eye for the whole, while many others were busy with himself.
Vidal by overplaying his lack of fitness (barely worked); Renato Sanche's trying to build on devil's performances (worked only to a limited extent); and Sebastian Rudy and Niklas Süle not to stand out negative in their first appearance in the Bayern jersey.
After all, that worked quite well.
Süle ran alongside Kimmich, Rudy in front of the defense, both acted confidently.
The well-named Napoli storm often struggled, in addition, Süle also tried himself successfully a few times in the game setup.
His colleague Rudy admitted that the result "wasn't as we hopeded."
But he, too, showed a decent performance on debut.
Game relocation, change of pace: "For this style I was also brought".
How unfamiliar this Bayern world still is for the newcomers, you could see after the game.
Rudy, for example, did not give one or two interviews in the catacombs, but certainly six or seven interviews.
Kimmich said goodbye after a few crisp statements in the back of his new teammates.
There he heard an interesting sentence from Süle: "What's going on here, I've already noticed.
At Bayern, you just have to win every game.”
After all, he doesn't have to teach the new one anymore.
Alaba motivates ÖFB ladies
David Alaba believes in the success of our ÖFB ladies
Austria's best-known footballer contacted Twitter shortly before the game and wished the ÖFB ladies all the best.
The Bayern legionnaire is a club-mate of ÖFB captain Schnaderbeck and cheers with our ladies.
The Austrian Footballer of the Year's tweet and Champions League winner should be a special injection of motivation.
Our ladies might also need the famous Bayern-Dusel.
Company satisfied with Rhineland-Palatinate minister: Economy praises Wissing's pace
It often turned out irreconciliable, the relationship between the country and the regional economy, when Eveline Lemke (Greens) was still economy minister of Rhineland-Palatinate.
This can be seen after more than a year in which the new Economy Minister Volker Wissing (FDP) is in office.
Chambers and companies from the region speak on T V-requestion of a change in economic policy - and at the same time blame the old minister.
Frank Natus, chairman of the Association of Trier Companies (VTU), says: "Wissing takes up the wishes of the economy, which Lemke has all blocked".
Natus sees the biggest U-turn in infrastructure policy: The gap closure of Autobahn 1 between Kelberg and Adenau is now at the top of the country's list of priorities.
Later this year, the ministry hopes for a planning approval procedure, by 2021 the construction law is to stand.
A role in the country's plans is also played by the western bypass Trier, which stands in the urgent need of the federal transport route plan.
The aim is to put the route into operation in December 2020, says ministry spokeswoman Cornelia Holtmann.
Natus sees this as a signpost to support regional but internationally competing companies.
The Chamber of Commerce and Industry (IHK) Trier strives, as suggested by Wissing, a Rhineland-Palatinate label for wine in order to increase sales in supermarkets and to stop the trend of German wine export, which has been slumping for ten years.
In addition, Wissing has an open ear for people who want to start their own company.
The Trier Chamber of Crafts sees an additional incentive for young trainees in the new Meister bonus, says Managing Director Matthias Schwalbach.
A €1000 premium is planned for the passed master’s examination and €2500 if graduates become self-employed within ten years after master’s training.
However, the homework list for the country remains long.
With the planned road construction, facts can be quickly created, at the same time lack the necessary personnel, says Jan Glockauer, Managing Director of the IHK Trier.
Out of 56 new engineers that Wissing intends to hire mobility, only 29 have been filled so far.
The IHK calls for more contracts to be awarded to external offices.
CDU parliamentary vice-president Christian Baldauf refers to calculations by the Court of Auditors that the country is investing more than €20 million less in the preservation of state roads in 2017 and 2018.
Frank Natus also expects a concept from the country on how it intends to remedy the shortage of skilled workers in the region.
The previous broadband expansion in the country in the country is also sobering.
In 2016, regional companies had rated the network supply in an IHK survey only with a rating of 3.4.
They complained about stalling internet connections and demolitions.
Former Economy Minister Eveline Lemke, on the other hand, is currently out of politics and still sits on the supervisory board of the wind power company Pro Wind.
Lemke left the Greens faction in April to become president of a private university in Karlsruhe - where, after four months due to discrepancies, she already cleared the field.
Audi to change 850,000 diesel vehicles - automakers face a backlash
A day prior to Audi's announcement, BMW said it would offer to upgrade the software in more than 350,000 vehicles.
The BMW chairman said the company felt "there are smarter options than driving restrictions," referring to European cities - including Munich, where BMW is based - to ban or restrict diesel vehicles.
Daimler announced on Tuesday that it would modify three million Mercedes vehicles in Europe to reduce its diesel emissions.
None of the companies described the ventures as a recall.
European automakers have strongly encouraged the use of diesel vehicles in Europe and the United States to support compliance with rules on carbon dioxide emissions.
But the nitrogen oxides emitted by diesel engines are considered carcinogens and can cause asthma.
The cost for automakers to install facilities to neutralize the exhaust gases ejected by diesel vehicles are also increasing, making it difficult to keep the price of the cars competitive.
German Chancellor Angela Merkel is also accused of coddling the powerful car companies and ignoring signs of the problem.
The companies are trying to avoid Volkswagen's mistakes, that the use of so-called shut-off devices that could regulate emissions to comply with regulations when a car has been tested but driving back in normal driving conditions.
In the United States, several employees of Volkswagen have been indicted, others are being investigated on both sides of the Atlantic.
Last month, the former head of thermodynamics was arrested in Audi's engine development in Germany.
Former manager Zaccheo Giovanni Pamio is Italian and is therefore not protected from extradition and could be brought to justice in the United States.
Earlier this year, Audi was involved in German criminal investigations at Volkswagen after authorities accused Audi of installing a system to circumvent emission regulations in Europe, and a request focused on the United States was extended.
The inclusion of Audi in the investigation could weigh heavily on Volkswagen: the luxury automobile manufacturer is disproportionately contributing to Volkswagen's profit.
This message has the raft on the Isar on Saturday
The city wants to protect the Isar.
So that the Isar visitors also participate, a raft drives through Munich tomorrow Saturday, which has a special message.
If it attracts again to the Isar on Saturday, you will see a large raft between Flaucher and Reichenbachbrücke.
And that's not a usual raft, with Isar tourists.
Instead, the construction department wants to clarify.
On the raft you can see a large information transparent.
It shows a mountain of rubbish.
The prelude to a publicity campaign to appeal to Isar visitors.
With the motto "True love is ...", the understanding of the Isar is intended to sharpen as a wild river worth protecting.
Last summer, the raft crossed the Isars and again this year there are further actions and measures to achieve considerate and environmentally conscious behavior.
Murderillo's tuber noses and the laughter against suffering
Mordillo was at times one of the most-published cartoon artists, the tuber noses of his characters are his trademark.
Even at 85, the Argentine is still drawing and working on a film.
His message: Laughter "because there is too much suffering in the world".
Guillermo Mordillo was born on August 4, 1932, the son of Spanish immigrants in Buenos Aires.
In the first years as an artist, he worked his way through, among other things, as a draughtsman for greeting cards - which frustrated him.
"The happiest day in my professional life's life was the day when into my job lost," he once said.
After stops in Lima, New York and Paris, he made his breakthrough at the end of the 60s: with the publication of his cartoons in "Paris Match" and then also in the "Stern" and other leading magazines around the world.
Even in China, Mordillo found a lot of fans.
Again and again, the artist was inspired by cities.
Large, roundy Mordillo figures tumble into drawings with the Leaning Tower of Pisa, Big Ben in London and the Red Square in Moscow.
In the early morning, he still sits at the drawing table today, working almost ten hours every day.
Through calendars, puzzles, books for young and old, T-shirts, plush toys and watches, his droll figures continue to find a lot of distribution worldwide.
For six years he has also been working on an animated film and a TV series with his characters.
The main character in the film "Crazy Island" is a giraffe - Mordillo's most popular animal in his drawings - that has a centipede as an opponent.
For the first time, his characters speak in the 3D film, which is scheduled to hit the cinema screen in 2019.
"I don't know exactly what they're going to say, it's weird for me," Mordillo says.
The script isn't written by him.
That's why he's more likely to rely on the TV series, which, like its original drawings, is produced wordlessly.
Lavender from Provence threatened by climate change
He has become a symbol of Provence in southern France, but there is a warning that he is at risk of climate change.
Lavender is declining because of conditions reminiscent of drought and insects transmitting the disease, causing major headaches for scientists and farmers trying to find solutions.
Eric Chaisse, an expert at the agricultural research institute CRIEPPAM in Provence, said: "The lavender in Provence is effectively endangered because the plant has been exposed to the effects of climate change in recent years, which brought much less rain in the spring and much more in autumn.
This has an impact on the lifetime of lavender.
There's also a small cicade, a microbacterium that transmits dwarf cicades, and in very dry weather there are very many of them."
And there's a lot at stake.
Lavender oil attracts not only for holidaymakers, it is also in high demand for perfume and cosmetics.
Eric Chaisse said: "Instead of having very long plant ears with a large number of flowers, we have smaller, underdeveloped and either missing or weak.
So we have fewer parts with essential oil.”
The solutions considered include a better distribution of water and the slowing of evaporation from the soil, but also spraying the plants with a protective mixture of clay and water and the development of varieties that are more resistant to drought.
The town of Geisa in the Thuringian Rhön celebrates its 1200th anniversary this year.
The highlight is this Saturday and Sunday with a medieval festival.
Grocers, guilds, traders, vagants or a Landsknecht camp are to be experienced on these days in the city center.
Murdering a journalist underscores the growing threat in Mexico
Employees of the weekly Riodoce usually meet on Wednesdays to review their plans for coverage of recent chaos in the state of Sinaloa through organized crime, corrupt officials and incessant drug wars.
But on that day, in the shadow of their own tragedy, they came together to talk about security.
They have been toldered that it is important to change their routines.
Two high-ranking journalists are discussinging what feels safer: to take their children to the office, who were the target of an attack with grenades in 2009, or leaving them at home.
Security experts have written three words on a plaque at the front of the room: opponents, neutrals, allies.
They ask reporters to suggest names for each column - no proof is needed, perceptions and gut feelings are enough.
There are drug smugglers, politicians, businessmen, journalists suspected of being on the government's payroll or cartels, a catalogue of criminals who make the chaos of Mexico dangerous.
There is no rest from the violence, and while the bodies are piling up across the country, more and more journalists are involved: at least 25 since President Enrique Pena Nieto took office in December 2012. 589 are under federal protection after attacks and threats and so far there have been at least seven deaths in seven states this year.
Among the latter is her editor and her inspirator of Riodoce, the co-founder Javier Valdez Cardenas.
"The biggest mistake is to live in Mexico and be a journalist," Valdez wrote in one of his numerous books on violence related to drugs.
His absence is deeply felt, even though his presence is everywhere - a large photo of Valdez showing his middle finger with the word "Justice," hangs on the facade of the Riodoce building. Two reporters in the Thirties, Aaron Ibarra and Miriam Ramirez, wearing T-shirts showing his smiling face with glasses or his typical Panama hat.
The workshop takes place less than two months after his death, and reporters are discussing their shared topics, trauma, their nightmares, insomnia, paranoia.
Although a special federal prosecutor's office was established in 2010 to deal with the cases of journalists, according to the committee for the protection of journalists, it has initiated only two criminal cases.
As with most thousands of murders associated with drug trafficking and organised crime every year, the killers of journalists are rarely brought to justice.
At Riodoce, they still insist on treating Sinaloa's violence, even though they are heartbroken and although the terrain is now even more treacherous.
Without information about the killers, without justice, the meeting has had little use for discussing security, Ibarra says.
"It's very dumb to waste my time in this workshop," he says.
“As long as we don’t know why, we distrust everyone.”
On the morning of May 15, Valdez left the Riodoce newspaper's office in Culiacan's national capital.
He only managed to drive a few blocks wide before his red Toyota Corolla was stopped by two men. He was forced out of his car and 12 times he was shooted, presumably because of the newspaper's name - which translates "Twelfth River."
The shooter drove away in his car and damaged it nearby.
Valdez was 50 years old.
He left behind a wife and two adult children.
Rumours tend to fly freely around Culiacan.
But on Valdez, there's virtually nothing but silence.
Since Guzman's arrest last year and extradition to the United States in January, Sinaloa has become one of the country's bloodiest battlefields as rival factions fight here to fill the vacuum.
It doesn't matter that someone or several people are shooned on the street every day in Sinaloa or that the cemetery is full of decorated, two-storey mausoleums for drug kings that are bigger than many homes for the living.
Forget for a minute that there is a state of "quiet" if only a cartel has control over the coastal state with its valuable ports and drug trafficking routes to the United States.
Nevertheless, and despite Valdez being aware of the dangers of his work, Ismail Bojorquez (60), a co-founder and director of Riodoce, is broken with the guilt that he couldn't protect his friend.
He believes two misunderstandings contributed to the murder.
Initially, there were the publication of an interview with Damaso Lopez, a leader of one of the rival antitrust factions in the war with Guzman's sons.
This publication may have annoyed the sons, suspected gait members bought up each copy of the issue as soon as it was extradited to newspaper kiosks.
The second mistake was not to force Valdez to leave the country for his own security after the seizure of another newspaper that had published the same story.
Valdez has been a legend in Mexico and abroad, and his killing is seen as a milestone in Mexican violence against journalists.
He'd survived so long that his friends and colleagues assumed he'd always be there.
He worked as an experienced reporter for Noroeste in 2003 when he partnered with five colleagues to establish Riodoce and sold €50 stakes.
In Sinaloa, "it was impossible to engage in journalism without encountering the drug problem," Bojorquez said.
Over time, the newspaper earned a reputation because of the bold and honest reporting, and sales and advertising increased.
Reporters loved to be able to publish hard-hit research without fear of censorship, and readers were fascinated by a publication in which they could read stories that no one else dared to handle.
Eight years after Riodoce's founding, she received the prestigious "Maria Moors-Cabot-Prize" for her reporting in Latin America.
In the same year, Valdez won the Committee's International Press Freedom Award for the Protection of Journalists for his Courage in the pursuit of Mexican drug history, wherever it led.
He voluntarily admitted that he was scared.
"I'd like to go on living," he said at the time of the CPJ award.
Drug trafficking in Sinaloa "is a way of life," he said last October in an interview with Rompeviento-TV.
“You have to take on the task that happens to you as a journalist – either that or you’re playing the dumb.
I don't want to be asked, "What have you done in the face of so many dead ... why didn't you say what was going on?"
The Riodoce team misses Valdez, the fun bird who could curse like a dock worker, the friend who was generous with hugs and advice, a teacher who knew how to survive.
They relied on his routine.
He'd always wear his hat.
He would always go to El Guayabo, the bar across from the office, and always sit there at the same table.
Now they ask: Was his love of routine his perdition?
His death also forced them to question their own assumptions about how they can best do their job and stay alive.
"You don't like dealing with their wives, their children, their clean businesses, their clandestine runways" to move drugs.
"These things weren't allowed," Bojorquez said.
The result, even in the best times, is a high degree of self-censoring and self-preservation.
Trusting one's own instincts.
If it smells wrong, stay away from it.
The annoyance is, the editor of the riodoce Andres Villarreal said, that "smell a sense that can be deceived ... and then the matter happened with Javier."
The old rules, as he and others say, no longer apply in Sinaloa - just as in Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Guerrero and other states where a poisonous mixture of lucrative smuggling routes, weak institutions and corrupt government officials is at home.
The landscape is constantly changing.
In the room where Riodoce's staff met for security training, suddenly no cellphones were allowed. Days earlier, it was revealed that spyware, sold exclusively to governments, was used to monitor journalists and activists in Mexico.
Outside, two police officers sought protection from the heat of 104 degrees (40 ° C) in the shade of a tree.
They were mandated by the state government to protect Riodoce's offices in a four-storey building in a mid-range district of Culiacan.
Half in jest, some of the reporters wondered if these officers were among the 50 percent of police officers whom the governor himself described as untrustworthy.
It's been months since the reporters went into the mountain landscape, where the drug gands are de facto under scrutiny.
For this week's edition, Riodoce has looked at three main stories.
There was the assassination of the brother of former well-known boxer Julio Cesar Chavez in Sinaloa.
They also had an exposé on government spending focused on the governor's hometown.
And there was a group who performed a kidnapping at one of Culiacan's most expensive restaurants, a block from the prosecutor's office.
There wasn’t an official word about who was kidnappeded or how it happened, so caution was when it was time to write what everyone in the city knows: that the restaurant is a favorite place of drug traffickers and authorities.
A reporter learned from public records that the restaurant was registered under the name of a politician who belongs to the ruling Institutional Revolution Party (PRI), which dominated all political levels for almost the entire century.
Several PRI governors have recently been accused of high-profile cases of corruption.
Villarreal asked the reporter to write about previous incidents at this establishment, including one in which a son of "El Chapo" Guzman escaped a military robbery.
Just a few months earlier, readers would have viewed Valdez's column as the best-informed source about the kidnapping.
"Previously, we would have already known what happened," Villarreal, 46, nicknamed "El Flaco" because of its slender body stature.
“Now all channels of communication with our sources are broken.”
Valdez's office has been rededicated to the storage space for signs and stickers protesting against the murders of journalists, because employees have become something they themselves would never have expected: activists on behalf of the press.
Reporter Miriam Ramirez grabbed a few signs and went to a demonstration the next morning for another journalist to the prosecutor's office.
Salvador Adame disappeared three days after Valdez was killed in the western state of Michoacan.
A burned body has been discovered and officials, based on DNA tests, say it's his.
Across the country, journalists have become more vociferous by writing "SOS Press" on sidewalks and buildings in organized protests.
On this day, director Bojorquez is away in Washington, trying to rally international support for the judiciary in the Valdez case.
At a meeting with Sinaloa's governor the day after Valdez's assassination, Ramirez accused the authorities of spying on journalists and killing them for telling the truth.
She has since asked for her to be redittled by the government because she's been concerned that her anger has violated her objectivity.
Valdez had repeatedly said that journalists in Mexico were "surrounded" by organized crime, government officials involved and an indifferent society.
In his last book "Narco-Journalism," he wrote that reporters are killed not only by drug gangs, but also on the orders of politicians and security forces stuck under a blanket with organized crime.
The media watchdog "Articulo 19" last year assigned more than half of the attacks on journalists to the police and officials.
"In Mexico, you're dying because they want you to keep your mouth shut," Ramirez said.
Clearly the killings have a chilling effect.
No one forgets the death of blogger Maria Elizabeth Macias in the northern border state of Tamaulipas six years ago.
Her body was found along with a note allegedly signed by the Zetas cartel: "Here I am because of my reports."
A computer keyboard and headphones lay next to her severed head.
Some stores have chosen to close, such as the El Norte newspaper in the northern border state of Chihuahua after the murder of correspondent Miroslava Breach in March.
Others carry on, as El Manana of Nuevo Laredo did after the assassination of their director in 2004.
In 2010, the Diario de Ciudad Juarez publicly addressed the drug cartels with an editorial on the front page with the headline "What do you want from us?"
Some journalists have fleded from their home states, or even the country.
It's a painful choice.
It's hard to find work in exile, and they're stilling the streets looking for dangers.
And sometimes they are hunteded, as it is apparently the photographer Ruben Espinosa, who was murdered together with four women in an apartment in Mexico City three months after fleeing Vercruz.
For those who stay behind and continue the work, it's a daily dance with high-risk choices.
Ibarra, who once wanted to be a poet, admits that the drug trade terrifies him.
"Mexico is going to hell, and that's why I became a reporter," he said.
At midnight last Friday, when the last edition was already over, Riodoce's editors sat on the sidewalk in front of the office and drank beer as suddenly all their phones began to hum.
A series of shootings involving gang rivals and security forces near the seaside resort of Mazatlan had left 19 confirmed deaths.
The war escalated further, as promised by a number of cartel embossies in the region.
From the curb, they have posted the news on Riodoce's website via mobile phone.
The front page had to be changed the next day.
Sirens howled nearby - another shooting in the area.
Bojorquez glanced at the policers, who stood guard to see if they were on standby.
If they were scared at all, they didn't show it.
Under the enormous portrait of the fallen founder of the newspaper, whose middle finger was visible to the whole of Sinaloa, Riodoce's associates followed in his footsteps.
"How can you even think of closing," Bojorquez said, "when on the same day Javier was murdered, the intern asked me to send her to the streets for reporting."
Dinosaur used camouflage to dodge his enemies
At first glance, it looks like a statue, albeit a terrifying one.
Now researchers have discovered that the first findings of the incredible rhino of a Canadian miner have been so well preserved that experts have described it as "Mona Lisa of dinosaurs."
It was so well preserved that they could even find that his scaldy skin were of reddish brown - and they said she had had something of a "troubled past."
The report in Current Biology magazine described him as "the best-preserved armored dinosaur that has ever been founded and one of the world's best dinosaur specimens."
The fossil is a newfound species of nodosaur that lived in the middle of the Cretaceous period between 110 million and 112 million years ago.
Its incredibly well-preserved condition has baffled explorers describing it as 'really remarkable'
According to reports in National Geographic, the armored herbivore is the best-preserved fossil of its kind.
It was found by Shawn Funk when he dug at the Suncor Millenium Mine near Fort McMurray in northern Alberta, Canada, on March 21, 2011.
He encountered something that seemed out of place by the surrounding rock and decided to take a closer look.
The fossil he discovered was sent to the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Paleontology.
They spent the next six years revealing the wild animal within the 2,500 pound (1,100 kg) lump of the earth.
After all of this hard work, the finished result is now ready to be unveiled.
"If you're just blinking your eyes a little, you could almost believe it's been asleep," said lead author Caleb Brown, a scientist at the Royal Tyrrell Museum, where the creature can be seen.
"It will go down in the history of science as one of the most beautiful and best-preserved dinosaur specimens - the Mona Lisa of dinosaurs."
Researchers have found that this herbivore, although it is covered with armor and resembles a walking tank, is likely to have been threatened by carnivorous dinosaurs.
The heavily armored dinosaur used a red and white camouflage to hide from predators, and used a shielding technique known as counter-shading, which is also used by many modern animals.
This would have allowed to blend better into its surroundings and dodge its enemies, experts say, suggesting it has been threatened by larger dinosaurs.
The 110-million-year-old creature from the Nodosaur family made headlines for the first time in May and is now on display at the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Palaeontology.
Researchers have now studied the wild animal and called it Borealopelta markmitchelli after museum technician Mark Mitchell, one of a team that has spent more than 7,000 hours carefully exposing it.
The astonishing preservation of the specimen allowed Dr. Mitchell and an international team of scientists to document the pattern and shape of scales and armor over his body.
They discovered the countershading, a common method of defense in the animal kingdom, which means the tip of the creature was darker than its underside.
Although the countershading is common, the results are surprising, since the size of Borealopelta far exceeds that of animals living today.
It suggests that the dinosaur was chased by enough pressure from carnivorous dinosaurs, that evolution favored the obfuscation of confrontation as a means of survival.
But most contemporary animals deploying the counter-shading - think of deer, zebras or armadillos - are much smaller and more vulnerable than prey, signaling that this nodosaur has been subjected to a real struggle for survival.
"Strong predator behavior on a massive, heavily armored dinosaur shows how dangerous the dinosaur predators of the Cretaceous period must have been," Brown said.
The chemical analysis of organic compounds in their scales also shows that the skin of the dinosaur was red-brown.
Scientists continue to study the animal for clues about its life, including its conserved intestinal contents, to find out what it ate as its last meal.
They believe the dinosaur, when he died, got into a river and driven out into the sea where he sank on his back to the bottom of the sea.
At that time Alberta was as warm as Southern Florida today, and rivers and oceans probably spread much further inland than they are today.
"This nodosaur is really remarkable because it is completely covered with conserved scaly skin, but is also preserved in three dimensions and retains the animal's original shape," Brown said.
“The result is that the animal looks almost the same today as it did in the early Cretaceous period.
You don't need to use much imagination to reconstruct it. If you're just squinting a little with your eyes, you might almost believe he's asleep."
Cretaceous was a time when giant theropods, carnivorous dinosaurs that stood on two legs crossed the earth.
Although the king of them, the Tyrannosaurus Rex, lived millions of years after Borealopelta, the armored dinosaur was hunted by some of his fearsome ancestors.
This includes the Acrocanthosaurus, a 38-foot (11.5 m) long, six-tonne monster.
The scientists, whose latest findings appear in the journal Current Biology, believe Borealopelta sank into the sea after death and mummified in the mud.
The creature was found by Shawn Funk when he dug at the Suncor Millenium Mine near Fort McMurray in northern Alberta, Canada, on March 21, 2011.
According to the museum, it's the world's best-preserved armored dinosaur, including skin and armor, and is complete from the snout to the hips.
Creatures were on average about 18 feet (5 meters) long and weighed up to 3,000 pounds (1,300 kg).
It contained two 20-inch-length tips protruding from its shoulders.
The researchers believe the armored herbivore was swung through western Canada until a flooded river flooded it into the open sea.
But the underwater burial of the dinosaur preserved his armor in exquisite detail.
The petrified remains of this particular specimen are so well preserved that the skin remains still cover bumpy armored plates along the dinosaur
As Michael Greshko has written for National Geographic, such a level of conservation is as 'rare as a win in the lottery.
The more I look at it, the more confusing it becomes.
Petrified remnants of the skin still cover the bumpy armored plates that carry the animal's skull.
His right forefoot is at his side, his five fingers are spread upwards.
I can count the sheds on his sole.'
In thunderstorms: Mass carambolage on A8: Many injured
Violent accident on Thursday evening on the A8: Nearly 30 vehicles are involved in a mass carambolage.
There are numerous injured, mile-long traffic jams - and gaffers.
About 20 people were injured on the Stuttgart-Munich motorway (A8) late on Thursday afternoon in a mass-carambolage.
According to the first data of the police, almost 30 vehicles crashed into each other between the junctions Adelzhausen and Odelzhausen (Aichach-Friedberg district) during a thundrester.
Initially, the exact number of injured did not provide any information.
These were minor to moderate injuries, said a police spokesman.
A large number of rescue vehicles were at the scene of the accident.
The A8 was completely closed towards Munich.
In the direction of Munich, a backlog of about ten kilometers long was formed.
As an AZ reporter reports, there were also massive obstructions on the oncoming carriageway - through numerous gaffers.
The firefighte noted the license plates of the vehicle drivers, who now face sensitive penalties.
Shortly after 18:45, a second accident occurred on the A8, this time between Augsburg and Dasing.
There was a collision between a car transporter and a passenger car.
A rescue helicopter was on its way to the scene of the accident.
Deutsche Bahn: Group tests 'comfort check-in'
It’s a moment that almost everyone has experienced before: unsuspecting nothing you sit on the train and sleep.
Or you're engrossed in your work and you're having an ingenious thought
And then: pufff.
Comes and wakes you rudely or tears the passenger out of the middle of the workflow.
Ticket control.
But the days of interruptions are soon over.
Deutsche Bahn is currently testing the managerless ticket inspection.
You simply confirm by mobile phone that you take up your place and thus devalue the ticket.
A test operation is currently underway on two routes.
“If the supply arrives, let’s expand it.
It is then expected to be available early next year on many more long-distance routes in Germany," a railway spokesman said.
"Comfort check-in," the railway calls the devaluation of tickets without a conductor.
However, in order to use them, some prerequisites must be met: You need a smartphone or a tablet.
And you have to have booked an online ticket - which is what most passengers do anyway.
You also have to have bought it via the app of the train, the DB Navigator, which is anyway the most convenient type of ticket booking.
And finally you need a seat reservation.
Because the "comfort check-in" works like this: You get on the train, call up your ticket under "My tickets".
In the case of the test customers, a button for electronic devaluation will appear.
Train attendants receive a message on their mobile devices that passenger X is on the train and has taken the booked seat Y.
If the train attendant arrives at place Y during the ticket inspection, he knows that a passenger is sitting there with an already validated online ticket.
So you can continue to sleep, continue to work, keep silent.
The "check-in" has two advantages: one for the customer, one for the train - which in turn is supposed to benefit passengers.
"If you want to travel through our train attendant staff in a relaxed manner without a ticket check-in, simply check-in yourself in the DB Navigator.
This allows passengers to make their travel time on the train more personal," says Michael Peterson, DB Fernverkehr's marketing board member.
Second plus: "And our employees have more time than today to respond to individual customer needs".
Those who often travel by train know that in addition to delays and missed trains, ticket checks are among the biggest annoyances.
Because often you yourself, but in any case one of the fellow travelers do not have all the documents that you need to validate online tickets.
Either the credit card or the identity card is missing or otherwise a document that you have to present.
The result is endless discussions between train attendant and passenger, which leads to a bad mood in the compartment or large area and delays in the control of one's own ticket and in the end takes the time to take care of other concerns.
Only a small number of test customers can still use the new service.
And only on the connections ICE 915 Dortmund-Stuttgart and ICE 912 Stuttgart-Essen.
Further train numbers on the same route have not yet been unlocked.
How one activist came to learn the answer to the question: "Whose Streets?"
Brittany Ferrell was shaken to her core on August 9, 2014.
Just a day after she arrived in New York City, the St. Louis-born sat on the bed of an Airbnb apartment she rented and scrolled through Facebook on her phone.
She stumbled across a mail from someone from high school.
"The police have just killed an 18-year-old child, and it's still on the road," it said.
Confused, since no one else had posted the information on her feed, she closed Facebook and opened Twitter.
A user with Handle @TheePharoah was constantly retweeted on their timeline.
The rapper from the St. Louis area was in the process of tweeting live the death of Michael Brown, an unarmed black 18-year-old killed by a white police officer in Ferguson, Mo.
He posted a picture of Brown's lifeless body, which was stretched out in the street and would stay there for four hours.
"It's wild," Ferrell thought to himself.
But death was nothing new for them and their community.
She dumped her phone to return to tweets about people on the Canfield-Drive later in the evening.
There were photos of the police tape and people screaming, and of a man who claimed to be Brown's father, who was wearing a sign with the inscription: "Ferguson's police just killed my unarmed son!"
She watched a live feed in which a policeman stood in front of a group of protesters with a barking dog.
In another video, she said, another police officer unlocked his rifle and shouted, "I'm going to kill you... animals all."
"That's not common," Ferrell recalled of her statement.
"That's not the kind of murder we've got used to that we're used to and that we can typically name."
At that moment I felt chills.
“I was furious and crying.”
She booked a return flight and went home the next day.
After landing at 2 p.m., she picked up her 6-year-old daughter from her father's house and decided the two would go to Ferguson the next morning.
What happened to Ferrell and Ferguson in the following three years is the subject of a new documentary, "Whose Streets?," in theaters from 11.
It's a story of survival and protest, love and loss, strength and resilience from the point of view of the people who live in the community and have filled the streets to demand answers.
When Ferrell went to Ferguson with her daughter, who wore a floral dress and a matching crown, the two discussed a conversation they had "about the black experience and black condition" countless times before that morning.
"You remember how I've taught you, when black people have to fight for what they believe in?" she recalled what she said.
“We’re going to Ferguson now because the police killed an 18-year-old boy and that wasn’t right.
I couldn't take them with me," Ferrell said later.
“What happened today is a climax of what happened in the past.
She needs to know it and see it and be raised in it.
It must be well developed, not only activism and organisation, but also know the different layers of black experience and black condition and what we need to do to become free to liberate ourselves.
Even if I'm lucky enough to give her an experience where she personally doesn't experience blatant racism, she's no different from the next black women's child.
I feel like I'm doning her a disservice to keep her away from it.'
The first stop when they arrived in Ferguson was at South Florissant Road, a location opposite the police department that became a camp area when the activists were waiting for Officer Darren Wilson's possible non-indictment.
There Ferrell and her daughter connected with a local business owner making lunch.
They prepared bags and helped pass them on to protesters.
Slowly, a new kind of activism for Ferrell began to take shape.
"My activism totally changed after August 9," she said, noting that as a student at the University of Missouri-St. Louis she was president of the Minority Student Nurses Association, focusing on food justice and health inequalities in black communities.
“I had no experience in organizing.
It's all come."
Weeks later Ferrell met Sabaah Folayan, one of the film's co-directors during an evening demonstration.
Folayan said she and her cinematographer Lucas Alvarado-Farrar only wanted to "document" what happened.
One question came to Ferrell's mind: "Do you want to document or are you trying to find a story that you can take advantage of?"
This scepticism has been nurtured by countless instances of people, often by whites - in a community that benefited from their pain and resilience.
As Folayan recently explained in an interview: "We had heard that people who were born and raised in Ferguson didn't center their voices and we wanted to do it differently."
Folayan had already collaborated with then-co-director Damon Davis, an artist known for his activism surrounding death row inmates.
Ferrell's engagement brought Ferrell closer to the project, along with six other locals following the team of "Whose Streets?" in the years after Brown's shooting.
"This documentary isn't someone who speaks for us or talks to us. We're talking," Davis said.
“That was the main thing for me how these people are portrayed, because that’s how I will be portrayed.”
The goal wasn't to focus on Ferrell's experience - to show that the movement is "not a Messiah leader, but a community coming together," Folayan said - she proved to be the film's most open participant.
As a result, documents from "Whose Streets?" show surprisingly personal aspects of Ferrell's life as she falls in love and her wife marries.
"I felt like naked," Ferrell said, laughing as she first saw the finished product.
"But I understood that there must be a certain vulnerability in this work and in the humanization of black people."
Moreover, Ferrell's story repeats the role of black gay women in the broader Black Lives-Matter movement.
Two of the movement's three founders, who coined "BlacklivesMatter" on social media following the killing of black teenager Trayvon Martin in 2012, identify as gay.
"Black, gay women in leadership positions have carried the movement overall," Ferrell said, "and that's why we're constantly fighting on different fronts.
Black, gay women must bear the brunt of everything.”
Folayan agreed to note that "the movement is really maintained by black women and many black women."
However, she insists that "this isn't an affirmative type of action," where Ferrell was chosen as the film's heart based on her identity.
"She was the person who stirred up that energy," she said.
"It's not a coincidence, even though she's been at the forefront because there's so much at stake at a lifetime at these intersections as a black, gay woman.
You can feel their energy through the screen.”
In the meantime, news cameras in Ferguson have long since disappeared, but the racial links between the community and the police continue.
Ferrell is on probation for 21⁄2 years for blocking a highway at a demonstration.
It describes the socio-political atmosphere of the area, which she calls her home: "A chaos."
Still, she clings to a song and she feels committed to that memory since she took to the streets in Ferguson three years ago.
“It’s our duty to fight for our freedom.
It's our duty to win.
We need to love and support each other.
There's nothing to lose except our chains."
From "dream" to "megalomania": TV readers discuss bit gallery
The bit gallery moves people, even though it is far from being built.
But the idea of a shopping centre at the southern end of the pedestrian zone has been dividing the population into proponents and opponents since the first presentation in 2009.
Cold leaves the subject hardly anyone.
The topic is also discussed on our Facebook page "Volksfreund Bitburg".
While some say to the Bit gallery: "That would be a dream" (Anja Becker) or "That would be really great" (Nicole Schausten), comes from the other side: "Don't need a person" (Siggi Betzen) or quite simply: "Megalomania!" (Lars Scholz).
While the proponents rely on the fact that a gallery attracts more customers to the city, from which the other shops also benefit, the opponents fear that then the pedestrian zone will bleed out.
