Eva Kilic: "Whate shopping palaces in Bitburg?
Whether clothing, shoes, drugstores or supermarkets: I find this superfluous.
There’s already enough of that in our city.
You can see how many shops are empty here - some for months and years.
However, since there is not even a decent weekly market here, it is all the more important that at least the market hall is preserved.
After all, the goods offered there are fresh, are mostly offered directly by the producer and is also inexpensive.
As you can see, shops in a shopping center are only rented by larger companies, because the rents there are often unsustainable for a retailer.
It’s a shame, there’s almost no more special deals, and what’s left will probably disappear.”
Wolf Fandel: "A shopping center with a market hall doesn't fit together for me.
And the gallery doesn't fit Bitburg.
Above all: Where should the Bauernmarkthalle go during the construction period?
The old Edeka would be the solution - even in the long term, even if the bit gallery actually comes.
Or does the ghost Bit-Galerie manage to distribute all business from Bitburg beforehand?”
George Marquet: "That's just not going to work.
And nobody needs a shopping center that's empty either.
In my opinion, it would have been better to promote a real farmers market, instead of this gallery.
But for a farmer's market hall there is no specifyingly, thanks to the insanely many - I think - there is no longer
If the farmers market hall is destroyed, a piece of culture will disappear again."
A fitting resemblance: Artist creates portraits with Chelsea Manning's DNA
In the middle of the room at the Fridman Gallery in New York, many faces can be seen - white, black, and brown that are almost imperceptible - hanging from the ceiling on cables.
The 30 portraits were created by artist Heather Dewey-Hagborg of cheek swabs and haircuts sent to her by Chelsea Manning.
Manning sent the excerpts from Fort Leavenworth prison, where the former intelligence analyst served a 35-year prison sentence after she made classified diplomatic reports on the WikiLeaks website.
The new exhibition, which opened on August 2, is called A Becoming Resemblance.
She's the product of two-year correspondence between Dewey-Hagborg, whose discipline combined her expertise in technology, computer science and art, and Manning, a transwoman and groundbreaking dissident whose sentencing was converted by Barack Obama when he had only three days left.
Dewey-Hagborg, through the algorithmic analysis of DNA extracted by Manning and from which 30 portraits of what someone might look like with these genomic datas, created a sharp, if somewhat intellectual, commentary not only on the malleability of DNA data - the many ways they can be interpreted and the inherent determinism of these interpretations - but also on identity.
Manning, who was born as Bradley Edward but has already spoken openly about identification as a woman since her puberty, was convicted of 20 charges in 2013, including six violations of espionage law, computer fraud and theft.
Through the prison, her image was suppressed, so much so that there was only one photo - a grainy, black-and-and-white selfie in which Manning visibly unwell sits in a driver's seat and wears a platinum blonde wig - with which she was associated.
In 2015, Dewey-Hagborg just came away from the huge success of her 2012 project 'Stranger Visions'.
In it, the artist produced portraits of strangers from forensic artifacts such as cigarette butts and chewing gum by extractinging the DNA from the waste to create a picture of what these people might look like.
This was when she received an e-mail from a newspaper magazine.
"They conducted an interview with Chelsea Manning while she was in prison, and they wanted a kind of portrait to accompany this article," Dewey-Hagborg explained at a press preview of the new exhibition.
And she couldn't visit at that time and she couldn't be photographed, that's why you made contact with Chelsea and asked her if she wanted to make a DNA portrait.
The artist and her imprisoned muse became unequal piles who exchanged several letters over the course of two years.
They even created a "Suppressed Images" comic illustrated by Shoili Kanungo, which foresaw a future in which the president would transform Chelsea's verdict and she would be able to see the exhibition in person.
That Obama, in fact, just days after the book was published, would demand Manning's release after seven brutal years in a military prison in Kansas was a welcome kind of happy gathering.
There is also now at the opening of the exhibition something deep and strong, as Donald Trump wages a war against informants from the government camp and against transgender troups to find new, tweetable ways to further inflame the rigid divisions he was supposed to fix.
When Dewey-Hagborg was asked about the strange timing of all of these things, she said that "things are happening for a certain reason."
In an artistic statement written down on the wall of the gallery, Manning, with her oversized signature underneath (the "i" in her family name is provided with a heart) called for an end to "automatical fragmentation, the basis of which have been gender, race, sexuality and culture."
On the opposite wall, their mitochondrial DNA sequence - a centipede of Cs and Gs and As, is written in pencil.
Dewey-Hagborg hoped to show how astonishingly similar, at least in biological terms, we are, by including the 200 letters (or nucleotides according to the artist's sophisticated scientific lexicon).
"What people will hopefully take away is that our genome doesn't care about who we are and how open genetic data for an interpretation and how subjective it is," said Dewey-Hagborg, whose 2007 video work, Spurious Memories, can also be seen.
“DNA data can tell so many different stories, so that’s 30 of these stories.”
The last piece in the exhibition, which is kept in its own room, is a page from the comic novella mentioned above.
She shows Manning as she emerges with a hands-free phone in her hand from the disciplinatorial arcasers of the United States like King-Kong.
"If they cancel your speech, then they've won," it says.
“Never shut up.”
"It came straight from a letter she wrote to me," said the artist, who greets from Philadelphia.
“I still get goosebumps when I talk about it.”
Manning hasn't seen the exhibition in person yet - as anticipated in the comic book - but if it should, then it will be greeted by the masks in the middle of the room, by its own genomic simulacs, which gather like hordes of protesters.
This was also targeted, like everything in the exhibition, up to size (5 " 2, Manning's stature), in which their genetic haplogroups were written with pencil.
"In the past I've had them on the wall, but I wanted them to feel like a crowd," Dewey-Hagborg said of the portraits.
"I wanted it to feel like a mass movement that formed with Chelsea, like the movement that was also behind its liberation."
Nortwestern University professor and an Oxford University employee wanted for homicide
A nationwide manhunt is underway for a professor at Northwestern University and an Oxford University employee who are wanted as suspects after a man was stabbeded with fatal injuries in a luxury apartment in Chicago.
On Monday, Wyndham Lathem (42) and Andrew Warren (56) issued arrest warrants for murder for their alleged involvement in Trenton H. James Cornell-Duranleau's custody court.
The documents say Cornell-Duranleau, 26, died after several stitches.
A community warning system released by Chicago's Chicago Police Department says the body was discovered on July 27.
Officers received a call, arrived at the Grand Plaza Apartments and found the victim with multiple injuries to his body.
Cornell-Duranleau has still been pronounced dead at the scene of what happened, and detectives are investigating the stings as murder while police are actively searching for the two suspects, officials said.
Lathem has been an associate professor of microbiology and immunology at Northwestern University since 2007.
Alan K. Cubbage, the vice president for university relations, said in an email statement that Lathem is now on leave of absence and has been barrelled from entering Northwestern University's campus.
"This is now a criminal matter being investigated by the relevant authorities and Northwestern University is cooperating on this investigation," Cubbage said.
Warren is a senior treasury assistant at Oxford University.
A spokesperson for the university and Somerville College said in an email statement that "we are in contact with the police in the UK and ready to help U.S. investigative authorities in every possible way."
The statement added that Warren's colleagues were shocked and want him to turn himself in.
Chief Communications Officer of the Chicago Police Department, Anthony Guglielmi, said he urged the suspects to turn himself in to the authorities.
"The police have an idea of their whereabouts, and our efforts to locate them are intensifying," Guglielmi said.
Guglielmi said that local, regional and national authorities are helping to find the suspects.
Guglielmi said the police received a call from the building manager of the Grand Plaza building, who had just received an enigmatic tip saying something had happened in apartment 1008.
The police described the scene as very cruel.
Guglielmi said it was unclear whether the incident also took place on July 27.
It's been confirmed by security cameras that Lathem and Warren were in the building.
"We believe Professor Latham and the victim had a relationship," Guglielmi said.
Grand Plaza's management team released a statement to residents: "The police are currently working on the victim's timeline and background and are exploring a variety of motives, including a possible domestic incident."
"We will complete the WADA timetable in the near future" - Russian Deputy Prime Minister Mutko
Russian Deputy Prime Minister Vitaly Mutko said it would not be long before the remaining World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) criteria for restoring the Russian Anti-Doping Agency (RUSADA) are implemented.
“At a certain stage of meeting the criteria of the roadmap, RUSADA has been given the right to plan and test in cooperation with UKAD (UK Anti-Doping).
The WADA timetable has been updated in this regard," Mutko explained on Thursday.
The PM commented on WADA's roadmap to complying with the code, which outlines RUSADA's reinstatement criteria and was recently published on the organization's website.
According to Mutko, it contains nothing that could cause concern for Russia.
“Everything has been announced, everything will be implemented.
And the results will be submitted to the International Olympic Committee (IOC) and WADA in the near future," he added.
However, one of the criteria mentioned in the list states that Russian anti-doping authorities must publicly accept the reported results of WADA-approved investigation by Canadian lawyer Richard McLaren into alleged state-sponsored doping in Russian sport.
Commenting on the matter, Mutko said: "We are conducting the investigation as our anti-doping system has admitted an error.
All measures have been implemented.
But there were no government programs, and we won’t admit something that doesn’t exist.”
Mutko's words agreed with those of Vitaly Smirnov, the head of Russia's independent anti-doping commission.
Speaking to Russia's Sport outlet at the beginning of the day, Smirnov admitted problems of the past in Russian anti-doping organisations, but then added: "We have repeatedly said that the report contains contentious positions and regulations.
No one intends to accept this report unconditionally," Smirnov added.
RUSADA was barred by WADA from conducting doping checks in November 2015 in the wake of the doping scandal.
However, she was allowed to plan and coordinate tests under the supervision of international experts and the UK Anti-Doping (UKAD).
After a visit to the organization's headquarters in Moscow in July, WADA Director General Olivier Niggli declared that RUSADA is moving "in the right direction" to be deployed again.
Aldi no longer sells eggs
This was announced by the groups of companies.
It was a mere precautionary measure, it could be further assumed of no health impairment, it said in the communication.
As of now, only eggs are likely to be delivered to Aldi, which have been proven to have tested negative for fipronil-containing anti-licenes.
Aldi wants to create clarity and transparency among its customers.
The start-ups that will help your business not to become the next Uber
Amy Errett wanted to measure happiness among her employees at her e-commerce start-up, but the surveys didn't work.
Answers have often been vague, unhelpful, or - even worse - fraudulent.
And even if you promised anonymity, some workers didn't trust the process.
"There's just never been consistency and objectivity," said Errett, who runs the 75-person San Francisco e-commerce hair care company Madison Reed.
She's been calling people from the outside for help.
A new breed of human resources start-ups is growing to help companies figure out how their employees feel.
By creating and licensing software, which has the specific purpose of measuring employee engagement, it allows companies to conduct surveys, target specific groups and demographic groups, provide anonymity and complaint hotlines to employees, and in some cases allow whistleblowers to bypass the C-suite executives and contact the board of directors directly.
"You now have tools like Strava and Fitbit for tracking their health, but where's the Fitbit for your company?" said Jim Barnett, co-founder and chief executive of Redwood City start-up Glint, whose software analytics tools are used by companies to measure employee engagement.
Errett said she gained a better insight into what your employees think and feel over the three years with Glint.
In addition to the snap surveys and surveys from certain teams, it offers a heat map of the firm that shows at a glance which units have the most complaints and which executives have low-consention outcomes - so you can re-drill why.
Organizations arre coming to realize that staying at the forefront of workplace culture so that you don't become the next Uber involved in scandals since a former employee posted a blog post describing an environment of harassment where those who have spoken have been punished.
For start-ups like Glint, this desire for an overview is a lucrative business opportunity.
The global governance, regulation and compliance industry could own more than $118.7 billion in value in 2020, according to financial engineering website Let's Talk Payments.
Denver, Colorado, start-up Convercent, which helps companies prevent and detect poor behavior, saw rising interest and activity earlier this year in disrepute amid the Uber case.
Convercent has nearly 600 clients, including Airbnb, Microsoft and Tesla.
Uber recently signed up as a client.
Like Glint, Convercent lets companies send tailored "pulse" surveys, collect confidential responses in real-time, and look at the heat maps of your problem areas.
It also provides an anonymous SMS hotline that allows employees to report bad behavior.
And when the chief executive is involved, complaints go directly to the supervisory board.
"The court of public opinion has captured regulators," said Patrick Quinlan, the founder and managing director of Convercent.
If a company is found that treats its employees poorly or behaves unethically, it can face costly consequences from consumer boycotts, employee fluctuation and lawsuits, Quinlan said.
Ruby Tuesday, the restaurant chain with more than 25,000 employees and over 500 locations, has deployed Convercent for more than a year to ensure employees are aware of the policies and procedures and have an easy way to reach their corporate headquarters.
Previously, if an employee wanted to report a problem, he or she had to find a phone number or email for the company headquarters, file a formal complaint, and hope that he was taken seriously.
It's often been an intimidation and inconvenient experience, James Vitrano said Ruby Tuesday's general counsel who said there hasn't been a good way to prosecute employees' complaints.
Now that the company deploys Convercent, issues that have previously been concealed by executives sitting in offices in cities or states - such as discrimination, harassment, or unfair practices - can be identified and addressed more quickly.
"I can get closer to this holistic, 360-degree view into the experience of employment," said Vitrano, who heads Ruby Tuesday's Risk Management Group.
“And we’re protecting our shareholders from employment litigations.”
Companies began taking ethics, values and employee engagement more seriously in 2002 after accounting firm Arthur Andersen collapsed because of ethical breaches by the Enron scandal, Quinlan said.
But it took the "social media to come to their right," that companies realized that you couldn't prevent their dirty laundry going online.
"Before the use of technology to monitor ethics, people used hope as a strategy," he said.
Both Glint and Convercent offer your software as a service, burdening companies with recurring fees for using their products.
It's a business model and an opportunity that finds approval from venture capital investors who have supported both start-ups.
Convercent received $10 million in February funding from firms like Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital, raising the entire capital to $47 million.
Glittering secured $10 million in November from Bessemer Venture Partners, bringing its entire financing to $60 million.
These investments hardly come as a surprise, given the interconnected nature of companies, culture and venture capital.
There is a growing complex of research showing that today's employees expect more from their workplaces than before.
In competitive markets like Silicon Valley, high salaries and interesting projects are just stakes.
The employees want to feel that you are accepted and valued and that you give your time to a company with a positive mission.
"If people are happy to be in a company, feel that their voices belong, and that the work you do is worthwhile, then they feel more committed to making this company successful," said Nina McQueen, vice president of global benefits and employee experience at LinkedIn, which uses both Convercent and Glint.
Investors achieve returns when their portfolio companies are doing well. Companies perform well when employees get involved and are committed.
If third-party analytics tools promise to increase engagement and employee engagement, it’s no wonder you’re finding support.
The data on employee engagement according to the experts of the workplace culture important.
But the data is useless if a company's administrators do not take action.
In fact, if a company asks employees for your feedback, it may have an expectation that change is on the way.
And if change doesn't come fast enough, or doesn't at all, it can breed disappointments and demotivate employees.
"If you're asking for 4,000 suggestions, you need to be willing to have 4,000 conversations," said Russell Raath, president of consulting at business management firm Kotter International, who has seen companies relying too much on collecting data.
“Because if you don’t follow, employees will ask yourself, ‘Did you really hear me?
You're taking care of that?
And if you don't care about it, why should I?"
Madison Reed, who is now conducting monthly employee surveys, was able to initiate action on the same day if a problem is reported about Glint, according to Errett.
And after gathering feedback from frustrated employees, she was able to reorganize several teams within the company through the speed of decision-making and the quality of communication to address the issues.
At Ruby Tuesday, the company is gaininging new insights about employees, and it hopes that in the long run, this will lead to a better retention of employees in an industry that is known for high sales.
"If you'ren't committed to creating a culture of transparency, you're going to lose people," Vitrano said.
“And if you lose people, you also lose customers.
And if you lose customers, then you're going to lose businesses.
The NCAA eliminates the two-day practices and makes a long day for the Bruins
It's about 23 o'clock, when a lot of UCLA football players converse on one side of the team's new practice fields on campus.
Some have taken off their jersey tops, showing the back full of sweat from the first day of training camp.
For a good part of the team, those idle minutes represent a lunch break.
Players enrolled in the summer classes will return to their final exams after the three-hour exercise before returning to the Wasserman Football Center the next morning at 6:30 a.m.
"That's a real treadmill for the boys the next three days," Bruin's coach Jim Mora said shortly before Monday turned into Tuesday.
The NCAA eliminates two-day exercises to ease players' burden, and it's actually extended its days - at least until Friday's final.
That's why UCLA's first three training sessions should begin at 7:15 p.m. to match the players' already packed schedules.
Compromising away from the two days is more than a day when the Bruins postponed the start of the camp almost a week after last season and carried out practical exercises for the first time during Mora's six seasons at Westwood.
They are scheduled to hold 18 exercises over the course of August. 20 prior to the start of formal game preparations for their kick-off against Texas A&M in September.
Mora said he supported the NCAA's rule change, even if it added another point to players' busy routes.
"We're putting this enormous strain on them for three or four days, and it's very challenging," Mora said.
“And they’ve just pushed through it, they keep grapping, they’re great people.
I think as soon as we come to Saturday and we have more time, it's going to be really great.'
UCLA will end its practices once the camp is finished, which, given the clear lines of sight of its fields from an adjoining parking lot and adjoining Luskin Center, an upscale hotel that has already been pelteded with footballs clearing a protective net behind a goalpost, will not be an easy task.
Bruins players are scheduled to occupy the hotel starting this weekend, but Mora conceded there could be a problem once those rooms are vacant during the season.
"We're going to deploy our security," Mora said.
“That’s a real problem, and it’s not far-fetched to think that people are renting a room there and then adjust to recording an exercise there.
If you have the videotape from an exercise, it's a pretty good deal.
But we have security forces there, and we have security forces in parking lot 8.
We'll do the best with what we have.
I don't worry too much about that.
We have a plan.”
Facebook wants to intensify fact review in the fight against fake news
Facebook wants to send more potential articles with false news reports to third-party fact-checkers and then display their results under the original post, the world's largest online social network said Thursday. It's one of its efforts to fight so-called fake news.
The company said in a statement on its site that it will begin using updated machine learning methods to detect possible false news and send them to fact-checkers who will potentially display results of the fact-check from the original article.
Facebook has been criticized as one of the main distribution points for so-called fake news, which many believe have also influenced the 2016 US presidential election.
The problem has also become a major political issue in Europe, with French voters flooded with false stories in the run-up to May's presidential elections, and Germany supports a plan for social media networks to be punished if they fail to quickly eliminate hateful postings as they did before the elections there in September.
On Thursday, Facebook said in a separate statement in German that a test of the new fact review would be launched in the U.S., France, the Netherlands and Germany.
"In addition to realizing what stories are contested by third-party fact-checkers, people want more context to make informed decisions about what they read and share," Sara Su, product manager for Facebook news, said in a blog.
She added that Facebook will continue to test its feature on the "related article" and is working on other changes to its news feed to reduce false news.
Creating a comic novel: A castle in the history of England
In its recent past, little Scotney Castle was featured in a squeeze music video and was a loophole for Margaret Thatcher in the 1970's and 1980s.
But Scotney has witnessed 700 years of its cozy seat in the Bewl River Valley in Kent and has now become the inspiration for a graphic novel by Jamie Rhodes, a mopah Yorkshireman with a penchant for historical documents.
"I find it so nice to look at the handwriting of someone who's been dead for 300 years," he says wistfully.
“What a personal relationship to keep this letter in my hand.”
'A Castle in England' spans time from the Middle Ages to the age of Edward, documenting the families living in Scotney. Each story has been illustrated by a different, emerging British artist - Isaac Lenkiewicz, Briony May Smith, William Exley, Becky Palmer and Isabel Greenberg.
It's not Rhodes's first book based on an archive: he wrote 'Dead Men's Teeth and Other Stories' in 2014, a collection inspired by documents at the British Library - a project he unexpectedly found emotional.
"I only reacted to the thumbprint on the page of a letter written by a double agent in the 1700's who worked for the Jacobins," he says.
"That's his thumbprint!
And letters always smell like smoke, because at that time a fire was lit because of the light.
For someone who lives 300 years later, the smell has an effect, as if he himself was there.
Oh, I love archives!”
For someone so beaten with old papers that he's backed by the National Trust and the Arts Council England and lived in a castle for almost four months ("To be honest: I've overvalued my welcome a little bit") all this seems too good to be true.
Rhodes spent his days exploring the Victorian "new" castle, looking through the archives and studying the many treasures and curiosities of the castle, such as the mounted hyena heads on the walls and the bottles that had spent centuries in the ditch.
He wandered through the grounds and the ruins of the ancient castle and spoke to landscapers, gardeners and sometimes with the artists who worked on the illustrations of his book.
Then, at night, he drank "whiskey and started writing."
Scotney's intimate story is relatively unknown as the National Trust didn't get full access until 2006 when the last heiress, Elizabeth Hussey, died.
When Rhodes arrived a decade later, employees were just beginning to take care of the archival, which he described as "hundreds of years when aristocrats said "Oh, throw it in the attic."
Diaries, letters from the time of the English Empire, maps depicting who owned what: "You'd think that land conflicts would be boring," Rhodes says, "but there was a birch that two families fought for.
Guys, let it all go!"
Some of the stories in the collection use the castle as a starting point to immerse yourself in English history on a wider basis, including 'The Labourer', which follows a man who leaves the newly built castle to join the peasant uprising of 1381.
"There's a record of an uproar appearing at the castle in 1380, in this book entitled 'Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex,'" Rhodes says.
“The peasant uprising began in this area and I thought an insurrection was a good start.
Maybe these two are connected?”
Other stories are firmly rooted in the history of the castle - such as 'The Priest', which tells the story of the Jesuit pater whom the Darrell family hid for seven years during the English Reformation.
Or 'The Smuggler,' in which 18th-century smuggler Arthur Darrell is portrayed who staged his own burial by filling a coffin with stones (a discovery made only years later when his coffin was unearthed).
The Darrells are Scotney's Favourite Family: "The gentlemen seemed to be quite crooks, keep making debts and spending money on things they couldn't afford.
“Other generations of Darrell men solved their debt crises by marrying rich old women – I imagine them as a bunch of good-looking guys, with a talent to advertise heiresses.”
After the end of his assignment in the castle, Rhodes is looking for other unique writing experiences
He has completed a two-week stay in the Finnish wilderness for a comics novel about Elämänmäki, the site of an early 20th-century sanatorium that has since been reclaimed by nature.
But Scotney has a special place for him.
"I was sad to leave again," he says.
It was like, "Oh, now I have to deal with fucking life again."
Campaign launched: Over 300-year-old Marienkapelle shrouded in Rhöndorf
With the concealment of the 301-year-old Marienkapelle in Rhöndorf, the city of Bad Honnef on Friday launched its campaign to introduce a new umbrella brand (Rundschau from Thursday).
Until Thursday, Mayor Otto Neuhoff and his marketing team around Nelson Artz want to keep the logo and the slogan secret.
At the end of August, there will be a celebration on the island of Grafenwerth as the highlight of the campaign.
"Too beautiful for us.
Or?" the creators of the action have been asking on the posters at the Rhöndorfer Kapelle since yesterday.
"We don't really think that (of course!)," they write on their website unlocked yesterday.
“But we’re using this measure to get your attention.
And to make it clear that some things are better than it seems.
Basically, we firmly believe that as Bad Honnefer citizens know very well about the beauty and attractiveness of our city."
The action is about more than "just" a logo and a slogan.
“It is necessary to formulate a stand-alone promise, one that gives the city an unmistakable unique selling point.”
After all, it is in competition with other municipalities.
The city of Bad Honnef can cost its new umbrella brand and the campaign a mid-five-figure amount.
Military reforms in Turkey - Erdogan's fear of the army
Every year at the beginning of August, the Supreme Military Council (YAS) advises in Ankara on retirements and promotions in the Officer Corps.
So this week, too.
At first glance, a normal process.
But nothing is routine with the Turkish armed forces a year after the coup attempt.
The meeting was no longer held in the General Staff building, as before, but in the prime minister's office.
Under the chairmanship of the head of government, the five vice-prime ministers as well as the ministers of defence, home affairs, justice and foreign affairs took part in the discussions.
Civilians in the YAS were outnumbered.
The government is pulling the reins in the military.
Hulusi Akar, chief of staff remains in office, but the commanders of the army, the air forces and the navy have been deposed.
Since the failed coup, head of state Erdogan has fired 169 of the 326 generals and admirals.
Over 10,000 soldiers have been removed from the ranks of the army - mostly because of alleged links to the movement of exiled preacher Fethullah Gulen, whom Erdogan sees as the mastermind of the coup attempt.
486 people who are said to have been directly involved in the coup attempt have been under negotiation in Ankara since this week.
Already the day after the coup, Erdogan had described the coup attempt as a "gift of God," as he gave him the opportunity to "completely purify" the armed forces.
But does Erdogan really manage the army?
At the end of February, Hürriyet newspaper published an article titled "Unease at the Military Headquarters."
In the general staff, there are concerns about an increasing "Islamization" of the army, which manifests, among other things, in the lifting of the headscarf ban for female soldiers, wrote the newspaper.
In any case, large-scale clean-up in the army and the security forces continues.
In mid-Juluy, Erdogan fired another 7395 public servants, including 2303 police officers and 546 soldiers.
It seemeed Erdogan had tamed the military.
The generals used to be regarded as the actual masters of the country.
Three times they coated, citing their role as guardians of the secular state order.
After his election victory in 2002, Erdogan pushed the power of the military back step by step, not least citing the reform requirements of the European Union.
Former officers say Erdogan is deliberately pushing an Islamization of the armed forces.
In Vocal Europe magazine, an unnamed officer who sought asylum after the coup attempt for fear of persecution abroad that the Turkish army would be "full extremists and Salafists" in a few years.
If this is true, such a development could not leave NATO cold.
In any case, there are doubts in the alliance after the purges about the readiness of the Turkish army.
The leadership structures and operational capabilities are "weakened," NATO diplomats say.
Wastans changes rules for the Green Card
There will be a new U.S. immigration plan to change the rules of the green card, President Donald Trump said, quoted by the media.
He envisages lowering legal immigrants to 50,000 per year, stricter regulations for obtaining visas for a fixed-term work permit and restrictions on family-oriented visas.
The new rules are proposed by Republicans and backed by the president.
The bill is due to be debated in the two chambers of Congress.
“This law will reduce poverty, raise wages and save billions of dollars for taxpayers, and will do so by changing the U.S. the way the U.
The law will prevent chain migration and replace the current system.
This new application procedure will benefit the candidates who speak English, who have the financial means to support themselves and show knowledge that will benefit our economy," Trump said, quoted by the American media.
Ministry wants to refurbish track for Rennsteigshuttle
In the past few months there has been arguing about the future of the Rennsteigshuttle - now it should go very quickly.
Before the end of the year, it is to be built on the ailing route on which the tourist railway runs.
In order to secure the future of the Rennsteig shuttle in the long term, construction work on the track is scheduled to begin later this year, according to the transport ministry.
"With the decision to further order and refurbish the Ilmenau-Rennsteig route, the country is committed to this connection, which is especially important for tourism," explained Transport State Secretary Klaus Sühl on Friday in Erfurt.
He estimated the cost of repairing the track at about 2.5 million euros.
Of this sum, the country takes over 75 percent.
The contract, on the basis of which the shuttle is to be ordereed by the country in the future, is currently being definitively coordinated, explained Sühl.
The ministry and the Südthüringen Bahn as operators are already in agreement on the technical details.
"With this, the timetable, the vehicles used and the quality standards are negotiated," Sühl said.
The planned contract between the ministry and the railway company will be concluded for a period of eleven years.
With this, the Rennsteig shuttle should be ordered regularly on December 2017.
The Rennsteig shuttle is a popular railway connection between Ilmenau and Rennsteig stations.
In recent months it had been tested in the course of a pilot operation whether the route is used by enough people to order it again on a regular basis in the future.
The route is also severely dilapidated in parts.
The rise of sex robots is real, and we should worry
Just when you thought it couldn't get even crazier in 2017, a new report arrives about the technological developments in the field of sex robots.
Yes, actually.
Sex robots are a big deal right now.
The Foundation for Responsible Robotics (FRFR) recently published a report on the development of sex robots over the next five to ten years.
According to Noel Sharkey, professor emeritus of robotics and artificial intelligence at Sheffield University and co-founder of FRR, we need to start taking their rise seriously.
Customers can choose the robot's eye color, its shape of the nipple, and the type of fingernail.
"People make fun of them, but the corporations deliver plenty of it, and we're going to get to see them a lot more often," Mr. Sharkey said at the release of his new report.
Modern sex dolls have evolved from their inflatable partners into robots with a silicone skin that feels warm - with deliberately human responses achieved through artificial intelligence.
The user can also "adapt" his robot according to his beliefs and choose everything, from the robot's eye color to the shape of the nipples to the type or color of his fingernails.
But it's going to be even more disturbing as many robots possess the "personalities" that a Stepford woman would look progressive.
Modern sex dolls have evolved into robots with a silicone skin.
The RealBotix robot, for example, allows its users to adapt their robots to the characteristics they find appealing, such as shyness.
And then there's the Roxxxy Gold sex robot developed by True Companion with pre-programmed personalities like "Frigid Farrah," which gives the impression of shyness and "Wild Wendy" with an "adventurous" personality.
"Roxxxy gold has a personality that's as much adapted to your personality as possible," enthuses True Companion's website.
So she likes what you like, and she doesn't like what you don't like, etc.
She also has her moods throughout the day - just like real people!
She can be tired, fixated on conversation or she can be "in the mood!"
There are many disturbing aspects in this rise of sex robots - not least because they replace real human relationships - but the submissive female traits, the allusions to rape when you take a step towards Frigid (meaning here: reluctant) Farrah worry you most.
On its site, True Companion Companion Roxxxy compared to a vibrator, but for men.
"If women (sic) can have a vibrator, why can't men have roxxxy?" the website reads.
However, these sex robots aren’t just a fetish or just another sex toy.
Their emergence and increasing sophistication point to something darker and deeper in our culture, to a retreat from the ideal of gender equality towards a desire for sex with submission as an optional encore.
Most men won't keep sex robots, of course, and while FRR notes its rising popularity, they still remain on the verge of consumer culture.
But what leaks from the marginal zones can be very instructive in relation to the spirit of the time in which we live.
And a lot of people aren't weird or offensive until the free market gives them permission.
There’s some overlap that these sophisticated sex robots are pointing at a time when women’s rights are under threatening women around the world when there’s a president in the White House who boasted about sexually assaulting women.
The most chilling aspect of the television series 'The Handmaid's Tale ('The Handmaid's Report') is not the graphic images, the intertwined bodies and the cut-out eyes, but how realistic this vision looks.
Gilead's Republic is a leap, but at the moment this doesn't feel like a big jump.
The goal behind these sex robots is to establish a physical resemblance to actual women as realistically as possible (albeit with porn star proportions).
Their builders want them to feel human, that they imitate the movement of a real body.
But, in a nutshell, when it comes to their "personalities," the robots don't represent real women.
They can't separate from their companion or just walk away.
They have no mediation or backstory, nothing suggesting independence or anything that could disturb the fantasy of total submission.
In other words, they are pleasing slaves.
Or to put it in the 2017 speaking style: maid
Trump's great achievement?
He makes Mitt Romney the epitome of common sense
It has become a truism of the Trump era that the political divisions that have polarized U.S. electorates have also divided families in small format.
Around Christmas and Thanksgiving, there have been newspapers in the U.S. with advice on how to deal with the horror of Trump-electing relatives.
We're all supposedly making an effort to burst our filter bubbles.
Less has been noticed is Trump's power to bring families together.
I'm mentioning this because my cousin, a left-leaning poet who lives in Chicago and has been dealing with the Republicans in our family for a long time, has visited me in New York this week.
She reminded me that unity against a common enemy can have a powerful effect.
With Trump in the White House, everyone she knows, including the Republican kinship she'd struggled with for decades because of politics, is on the same side.
This is certainly a good thing around the dining table, but it strikes me in a wider context that this also entails considerable risks.
The left has been strengthened by Trump, but the disfiguring effect of his presidency has the power to push us all to the right.
When we speak of "normalization" and Trump, we're referring to the scary possibility that his antics won't repel us one day.
However, there is an even more terrifying long-term scenario in which Trump "normalizes" right-wing Republicans, who at once suddenly appear as the epitome of reasonableness compared to his standards.
I'll find myself beset by an active nostalgia these days for Mitt Romney's hilarious version of the crazy -- the dog on the car roof, the 14% income tax (but at least we knew what his tax return looked like) - both of which, in fact, appear to one as very mild offences compared to Trump.
If Trump leaves, the Republican's next nominee only needs to be mentally healthy to qualify as an immeasurable improvement.
When my cousin was in the city, we brought our children to carousel in Central Park, the largest of the carousels in New York's park department, and the top three dollars you can spend in the city if you don't need caffeine.
The horses are enormous, the fiberglass designs on the central cylinder - all clowns are growling and the animals twist to look over their own shoulders full of fear - extremely sinister and the chinking music conveys this in a strange way.
So many clichés of a city disappoint: the views of tall buildings grow old, the skylines become too familiar to offer a lot of excitement, and the reality of the city doesn't live up to the dream.
thirty-five years later, and I'm still not quite throughout the day when I found out that Swiss Cottage is, in fact, not a Swiss cottage - the themed pub Ye Olde Swiss Cottage doesn't count - but a big roundabout in northern London.
The Central Park carousel, however, is still a strangely magical experience, as two-year-olds, an 11-year-old and two women can walk on it in the 40's and all have a great time here.
If I could've assessed the carousel, I would have given him the full score, but luckily none asked about it.
On the other hand, this week at a department store I was forced to evaluate my "experience" with transactions on a scale of one to five stars before I could go through my credit card.
In the coffeehouse at the corner of my street, it's the same, plus the option to match the rating with a 15% tip - this to buy something over the counter.
This not only leads to a slowing of what should actually be a neutral exchange, but also puts pressure on the server to introduce personality to a situation where none is required.
The poor woman in the department store stood apologetically grinning while I was tired of giving her my five-star credit card.
Oscar Pistorius with chest pain transferred from prison to hospital
Pistorius has left prison for the second time for a hospital visit.
Last year, he was taken to the hospital because of the treatment of cuts on his wrists, which he suffered, according to the prison authorities following a case in his cell.
Pistorius was convicted of murder following an appeal by prosecutors against an initial manslaughter verdict.
He killed Steenkamp in the early hours of Valentine's Day 2013 by shooting her several times through a door of the toilet cubicle at his home in Pretoria.
Pistorius claimed that he considered his girlfriend to be a nocturnal intruder who was hiding in the cabin.
Prosecutors have announced their intention to this time appeal against Pistoriu's six-year prison sentence, which they say is too lenient.
The national law enforcement agency said it would object to South Africa's Supreme Court, and the appeal could be heard this year.
Pistorius runs the risk that his sentence will be increased to 15 years if the prosecutors are successful.
There's no death penalty in South Africa.
When farmer Arnold finds a strange egg
As the last of the Blumberg schools, the fourth graders from Fützen and Epfenhofen were discharged on holidays and into secondary schools.
In the future, three of the dismissal students will go to high school, four to secondary school.
Headmistress Angelika Sitte greeted the parents and representatives of the public at the Bürgerhaus Biesental in Epfenhofen on Tuesday evening.
As a symbol of the school release, the 29 pupils of the four classes under the direction of Sophia Schumacher and Regine Meder chose a young eagle, which grows up in the chicken farm, but is then released to freedom.
In addition, the lively crowd performed a musical titled "The Eagle in the Chicken's Yard".
Bauer Arnold found a strange egg and had it hatched by his chickens.
Instead of a chicken chick, a small eagle came out.
But the hope of making a chicken out of him smashed, the eagle spreading its wings on a high mountain and vanished.
After that, classes three and four showed a chicken dance in Yvonne Frank's rehearsal.
The hens danced with the young gocks, who were carrying cylinders, around the bet.
The eagle stands for integration, said headmistress Angelika Sitte.
The juvenile is different from the other cubs (chicks), but belongs to it.
He'll never be like a chicken.
His destiny is to be free.
She wished every fourth-grader to feel accepted in his new school.
This was followed by words of thanks to the colleagues, the former headmaster Michael Müller, who repeatedly intervenes in case of classroom shortages, and the parents’ representatives.
The chicking season is now over, explained class teacher Sophia Schumacher.
After two years of lessons, she hates to release her students, because it was a really great time.
Finally, she relied on the fourth graders.
Finally, all the students had a song of summer time and holiday time sound.
Great joy broke out among the children.
Eight first-graders have already been registered for the new school year.
Two Bulgarians arrested for illegal camping in Greece
Two Bulgarians have been detained for illegal camping in Greece, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, cited by television, said.
On the morning of August 1, a group of illegally camping people, including the two Bulgarians, was arrested by police in Sithonia.
On the same day, they were released while an investigation is currently underway.
The State Department told bTV that the Bulgarians were later released.
The Greek authorities have tightened control of free camping and impose significant fines on anyone who is founded to stay in a place not intended for this purpose.
The checks will continue throughout the season.
The ministry said the court can impose a fine.
Software update for diesel vehicles: What those affected need to know now
How exactly does the software update take place?
Do diesel drivers have to take part in the action?
The most important questions and answers at a glance.
At the diesel summit, BMW, Daimler, Opel and the VW Group offered software updates for a total of around 5.3 million cars.
They are intended to improve the exhaust gas behavior of cars of the emission classes Euro 5 and partly Euro 6.
Euro-4 diesel or worse is exempt.
Anja Smetanin of the ACE Auto Club Europa gives assessments:
Is the new action obligatory?
No, the negotiated software updates are voluntary.
"The obligatory updates previously only apply to the vehicles that have cheating software," says Anja Smetanin.
Their retrofitting has been ordered by the Federal Motor Transport Authority (KBA).
How and when will customers be informed about updates?
The manufacturers have announced a speedy implementation in the next few months, but have not yet given an exact date.
Daimler has announced that it will write to anyone affected.
If an inspection is due anyway, this will be done within the scope.
How long will the conversions take as a whole?
"It's hard to say," Smetanin says.
This depends on the commitment of the manufacturers as well as the political pressure.
“Because we have seen that car manufacturers do not voluntarily act on the subject of the environment so far.”
How long will the update take?
The conversion itself could only take three to four hours in the workshop.
It could be more time-consuming to get an appointment.
What do motorists who don't drive a car from BMW, Daimler, Opel and the VW Group do?
The ACE advises waiting.
Presumably foreign manufacturers would follow suit, according to Smetanin.
Is there now a threat of a massive fall in the price of Euro 4 cars?
The ACE fears that.
But precisely in the regions, where driving bans threaten.
In rural areas, there are so far no losses in value for older diesels.
The ACE advises to wait with a sale.
LL Cool J Receives Kennedy Center Award
The Kennedy Awards Gala is one of the most glamorous events in Washington.
The first awards ceremony under President Trump promises to become political.
One of the award winners doesn't want to come - in protest.
LL Cool J (49) is the first rapper to receive one of the coveted awards from the prestigious Kennedy Center.
As the cultural institution announced on Thursday, singer Gloria Estefan (59), Lionel Richie (68) as well as the choreographer Carmen de Lavallade (86) and the TV producer Norman Lear (95) are also among the honorees this year.
The awards will be handed over in early December.
Traditionally, U.S. President Donald Trump will host a reception and, along with his wife Melania, attend the ceremony.
The award winner Lear, who became known with progressive TV series, has therefore already announced that he will refrain from attending the gala.
“This presidency has decided to completely neglect the arts and human rights – even depriving them of the funds – and that burdens me.”
59-year-old Estefan emphasized that she will use the conversation with Trump to make her view clear.
Named after former President John F. Kennedy, the Kennedy center is the largest cultural institution in the U.S. capital.
Former winners include Meryl Streep, Aretha Franklin, Robert De Niro, George Lucas, Tom Hanks and Sting.
We make the city worth living
Roland Wacker, Haslach's city builder, retires next year.
The position has already been advertised, but suitable applicants are scarce.
Wacker has been a city builder for 31 years.
And the work is still great fun for him, as he conveys in the press conference.
He reports on varied, but also demanding tasks waiting for his successor.
At the same time, he is surprised that so far no application meets the requirement profile.
The city is looking for an architect or civil engineer at the earliest opportunity.
Thus, in compliance with the regular notice periods, there should still be enough time for an incorporation.
Since Haslach acts for the administrative community as a lower building law authority, the applicant must have appropriate qualifications.
Because the city definitely wants to retain this function.
If they could no longer fill them out, the citizens would have to go to Offenburg for consultation.
But proximity to the citizens is important to the city and itself, says Wacker, who reports on a good working atmosphere as well as a good mood between the authorities and citizens.
In more than 30 years in office, a number of significant projects have accumulated that Wacker looks back on with pride.
"We're making the city liveable," he's sure about the construction office and its staff.
But also in the future, varied projects are on the agenda: The transformation of the former Hukla area and the Niederhofen parking lot, for example, are initiated.
The Kinzig Bridge between Bollenbach and Steinach will be renewed, the stadium renovation is still an issue.
The latter alone will cost around €1.5 million "if we do it right," Wacker estimates.
“All in all, great tasks are waiting for my successor.
The work here is fun."
In terms of design, there is great scope for a city builder.
With Haslach's own construction yard and nursery, much can be reacted quite flexibly.
"You can move a lot," says Wacker, and the design of the green areas is often praised by citizens and visitors.
Since the old town as an ensemble is under monument protection, it is important "to have an eye for what fits in", when new buildings are approved, emphasizes the outgoing city builder.
In any case, the city builder bears high responsibilities.
He's a builder, "in the end, it's all over my table here."
In terms of supervision and coordination, he can count on long-standing employees: Markus Neumaier (Hochbau) has been involved for ten years, Manfred Schöner (co-working construction) has been involved for 15 years.
Wacker also wishes that his successor stays for a long time: "We are looking for someone who really identifies and wants to stay with the position".
Continuity is not only important for a good working atmosphere, but also for the further design of the city.
Anyone wishing to inherit Roland Wacker should be able to be able to have completed studies as a graduate engineer or bachelor’s degree in architecture, according to the city of Haslach and have good specialist knowledge in the fields of building regulations and construction planning law.
The municipal building and civil engineering, site planning, land acquisition matters, administration of the municipal properties, municipal depot with construction yard and city nursery as well as subjective building authority including monument protection.
Bicycle thrown on lines: Südbahn line blocked
According to the police, the previously unknown perpetrators had thrown the blue bicycle from the railway track overpass in the area of Leobener Straße (B116) on the lines.
These were so badly damaged that the approaching freight train had to be stopped.
Investigators assume two male perpetrators and have been searching for them since the night.
Near the crime scene, a rose-colored scooter and a cyclist had been observed by witnesses.
"This goes far beyond a lousy stroke," ÖBB spokesman Christoph Posch told the APA.
The perpetrator or perpetrators would have put themselves in great danger and also jeopardized train traffic.
In addition to the damage to the locomotive and the plant as well as the delay minutes and the costs for the rail replacement traffic could also come to the perpetrators.
Overall, a "significant" sum is expected, the spokesman said.
The damage had been repaired again on Friday afternoon, the rail traffic on the Südbahn line could be managed without obstructions.
Nearly ten passenger trains had failed on Friday morning, two of which concerned long-distance traffic.
Freight trains did not fail, but they were delayed.
According to ÖBB spokesman Christoph Posch, about 200 meters of overhead lines had to be replaced.
Insulators and masts were also damaged.
Red meat, dairy products and chocolate could help relieve IBD (inflammatory bowel disease)
Red meat and chocolate can sound like a nightmare for your digestive system.
But a new study asserts exactly the opposite: a diet rich in meat-based proteins and dairy products could alleviate the symptoms of patients with inflammatory bowel diseases.
The incurable condition, which affects around 1.3 million in the U.S. and 300,000 in the UK, is causing stomach cramps and bloating, recurrent or bloody, weight loss and extreme fatigue.
While sufferers are usually advised to avoid meat and dairy products to alleviate their symptoms, researchers at Washington University found that the essential amino acid tryptophan of proteins helps develop immune cells that promote a tolerant intestine.
The lead researcher, Dr Marco Colonna, explained that immune cells in the intestines patrol to ensure that harmful microbes hiding in food do not invade the body.
Cells capable of inflammation are compensated by cells that promote tolerance and protect the body without damaging sensitive tissue.
If the balance tends too far towards inflammation, an inflammatory bowel disease can result.
In experiments, it has been found that a type of tolerance-promoting immune cell occurs in mice that carry a particular bacterium in their intestines.
And the bacterium requires tryptophan - one of the building blocks of proteins - to trigger the appearance of cells.
Professor Colonna said: "We have established a link between a species of bacteria - Lactobacillus reuteri - which is a normal part of the gut microbiome, and the development of a population of cells that promote tolerance.
The more tryptophan the mice had in their food, the more they had of these immune cells.”
He suggested that the combination of L. reuteri and tryptophan-rich diet promote more tolerant, less inflammatory intestinal milieus, if the same system also works in humans.
Postdoctoral student Dr. Luisa Cervantes-Barragan studied a kind of immune cell that promotes tolerance when she found that a group of study mice had such cells, but another group of the same strain, which was housed separately, did not.
She suspected that the difference had to do with the mice's intestinal microbiomes - the community of bacteria, viruses, and fungi that normally live in the gastrointestinal tract.
The intestines of the mice were DNA sequenced and six species of bacteria were found that were present in the mice with the immune cells, but were missing in the mice.
Mices were reared in a sterile environment, so they lacked an intestinal microbiome and they didn't develop this type of immune cells, and they obtained the L. reuteri and immune cells.
To understand how the bacteria affected the immune system, the researchers let L. reuteri grow in a liquid and then transferred small amounts of the liquid - without the bacteria - to immature immune cells isolated from the mice.
Immune cells developed into tolerance-promoting cells.
When the active component was purified from the liquid, it proved to be a by-product of tryptophan metabolism known as indole-3-mitric acid.
When the researchers doubled the amount of tryptophan in the mice's feed, the number of such cells increased by about 50 percent, but when the tryptophan levels were halved, the number of cells dropped by half.
Dr Cervantes-Barragan explained that humans have the same tolerance-promoting cells as mice, and that most of us protect L. reuteri in our gastrointestinal tracts.
It is not known whether tryptophan by-products from L. reuteri develop the cells in humans as much as in mice, but defects in genes related to tryptophan have been found in people with inflammatory bowel disease.
She said: "Developing these cells is probably something we want to promote, as these cells control inflammation on the inner surface of the intestines.
Potentially, high tryptophan levels in the presence of L. reuteri can lead to an expansion of this population."
Jose Mourinho believes Manchester United have committed a 'genius' with Nemanja Matic
At Manchester United, a "genius" has signed with Nemanja Matic, Jose Mourinho believes after the midfielder made his debut against Sampdoria on Wednesday night.
Chelsea's £40 million signature began with a 2:1 victory in Dublin, the final pre-season game before the UEFA Super Cup against Real Madrid kicks off on Tuesday, impressing in the keeper's role when people like Paul Pogba and Andreas Pereira marched forward at will.
Matic, however, had been barred from the Blues' preparatory Asia touring and managed only 45 minutes, allowing Mourinho that he had to improve before the Premier League season.
"He needs time, but his experience, intelligence, a genius in the way he thinks - he thinks football," Mourinho said.
“We’re going to help him be ready sooner than later.”
In addition to Matic, Victor Lindelof and Romelu Lukaku also started at the Aviva Stadium when Mourinho formed his starting XI for the start of the season.
The Independent revealed this week Mourinho wished three more new commitments, but he insisted that the club had already done a "fantastic job" of fulfilling three of its four senior new commitments.
"Gitting three out of four, for that I thank the club," he added.
"I'll be happier when they get four out of four for me, but they've put in a lot of effort."
Boeing "paints" Dreamliner into the sky
U.S. aircraft maker Boeing has painted the silhouette of a Dreamliner into the sky.
The pilots departed from Seattle in the U.S. state of Washington, but the drawing began in Marquette in the U.S. state of Michigan and dragged over the U.S. in the form of a Dreamliner, that's several thousand kilometers.
The action lasted for 18 hours and one minute.
On the radar screens was to be the form of a Dreamliner.
The whole thing was an endurance test for new engines.
Boeing had previously advertised like this.
Thus, the number "787" and the Boeing logo were painted into the sky.
New blow blow for Martin Schulz - Hannover, we have a problem
How the wind turns: When Sigmar Gabriel was SPD leader, Social-Democratic prime ministers always looked to Berlin before elections with a certain concern.
Merely no sturgeon fires from the party's headquarters, just no headwinds from the Confederation - that was the most important wish of the election campaigners.
Since Martin Schulz led the party, that has fundamentally changed.
Since then, the employees from the Willy-Brandt-Haus have been observing events in the SPD-ruled state capitals with increasing horror.
What Schulz got from there since the beginning of the year wouldn't even be described with headwind.
It's been low blows.
And one by one.
After the surprising end of the red-green majority in Lower Saxony, the Federal CDU sees a general signal against coalitions of the SPD and the Greens.
"This shows once again: red-green simply cannot govern reliably," CDU Secretary-General Peter Tauber told the editorial network Germany (RND).
The surprise factional exit of a Green MP has plunged the government of Prime Minister Stephan Weil (SPD) into a serious crisis.
Five months before the state election, his red-green coalition thus lost its one-vote majority to the opposition from the CDU and FDP.
Greens Federal Managing Director Michael Kellner has urged the breakaway Lower Saxon Green MP Elke Twesten to return her mandate.
The conversion to the CDU means a clear falsification of the will of the voters.
Elke Twesten must immediately return her mandate," said the Federal Managing Director and Election Director of the Greens to the editorial network Germany (RND).
"All this must not be true at all," sighs an SPD campaigner, as the new jobs message reaches the party's headquarters on Friday morning.
Stephan Weil, SPD Prime Minister of Lower Saxony, stands after the conversion of the Green MP Elke Twesten to the CDU without its own government majority.
It is the preliminary culmination of a whole series of large and small crises in SPD state associations.
And no one knows what's next.
The misery began with the unexpectedly clear defeat in the Saarland election in March.
A blue eye, nothing more, the comrades at that time calmed down.
After the state elections in Schleswig-Holstein and North Rhine-Westphalia, everything will be different.
So it became - and everything got significantly worse.
The defeat in Kiel caused the SPD tumbling, the Düsseldorf bankruptcy knocked down the party smoothly.
The comrades laboriously rallied up again in the weeks after that.
Then, when they had just gained new courage, the G-20 summit came from Hamburg.
And with him came the next low blow.
Mayor Olaf Scholz, the last strong SPD head of state, lost his nimbus that weekend because he was sitting in the Elbphilharmonie while his city burned.
And because in the crisis communication afterwards he did pretty much everything wrong that you can do wrong.
Since then, Scholz has been fighting for his political survival.
So now also Stephan Weil.
Due to the VW emissions affair and his role on the Supervisory Board, the Prime Minister of Lower Saxony has been under criticism for some time.
Miserable poll ratings were added.
Nevertheless, or perhaps precisely for this reason, Weil tried more and more in recent weeks to set accents in the Confederation.
Comrades around Martin Schulz did not remain hidden from the former mayor of Hanover’s efforts to expand his power in Berlin.
They had him granted, since they promised Weil a stabilization of the party.
Now the exact opposite could happen.
Weil is basically not to blame for the party-internal quarrel of the Lower Saxon Greens.
And Schulz doesn't.
Meanwhile, the comrades strive to keep their own ranks closed.
Their wrath is directed at the Greens, and especially at the breakaway parliamentarian Twesten.
"It's unbearable and democracy-damaging how Ms Twesten puts her own career interests above everyone else's interests," Matthias Miersch, head of the party's left in the SPD parliamentary group, tells the editorial network Germany (RND).
Ms Twesten has not been elected to parliament because of her person as a direct candidate, but via the state list of the Lower Saxon Greens, the Lower Saxony MP continued.
The logical consequence would be for her to return her mandate.
Thomas Oppermann, head of the SPD parliamentary group, laments an "undemocratic maneuver."
"The voter will four years ago was clear: the people have made Stephan Weil the prime minister," Oppermann, who leads the Lower Saxony SPD as a front-runner in the federal election, tells the RND.
"Now the personal career thinking of a future CDU MP distorts the election result," Oppermann continued.
He demanded to clarify the backgrounds.
I want to know if there have been more or there have been further commitments from the CDU Niedersachsen.
Funding for local centre III
If you want to renovate your aging house, you can soon get support from the country.
Mayor Albert Schindler and three representatives of STEG Stadtentwicklung informed about this on Wednesday in Empfingen.
At the beginning of the information event Schindler once again gave a review of what has happened so far.
There have been rehabilitation areas in Empfingen since 1991.
At that time, in remediation area I from 1991 to 2004, the state of Baden-Württemberg took over two-thirds of the funding.
In the rehabilitation area II from 2005 to 2016, the grant was still 60 percent.
For Schindler, the previous renovations are a "success project" - after all, it is also important to promote internal development, and not only to open up new construction areas.
On 4 April, the Karlsruhe Presidium received information that there will be a rehabilitation area III.
The funding amounted to 833 333 euros, of which 500 000 euros would come from the state, the rest from the municipality of Empfingen.
How does a rehabilitation area emerge?
Schindler continues to investigate where the "really old" houses are.
However, it also plays a role in whether there is any interest at all in the planned area.
If necessary, the territorial boundaries could then shift again.
The STEG represented Martin Keller, Gaby Pietzschmann and Sarah Gotzel on Wednesday night.
Keller presented the contents of the "Social City" program, within the framework of which the renovations would then be carried out.
The core of the programme is the revitalization of inner cities, the improvement of living and working conditions and the maintenance and expansion of infrastructure.
The measures would be carried out object-related, i.e. per property.
The redevelopment area would then eventually be established as a statute.
Since 1 January 2017, the approval period for the funding will run until 30 April 2026.
However, until September this year, the inventory would be carried out, within the framework of which those involved in the rehabilitation area will receive a questionnaire to fill out.
In November or December, if possible, the statute decision should take place, so that the renovations can be started in the coming year.
Pietzschmann subsequently explained that each commune had to carry out the said preliminary examinations.
It is about assessing the urbancultural grievances and deciding, when it will be refurbished and to what extent.
For the respective plot, a renovation note will also be entered in the land register.
Pietzschmann himself will come again to Empfingen and get an idea of the situation, she promised.
Gotzel emphasized that an essential success factor for the project is the participation of those involved.
Therefore, there will be questionnaires, which will be sent to the citizens in the coming weeks by post and can be completed until after the end of the summer holidays.
The yellow questionnaire is the "building and property arch", which deals, among other things, with the structural condition and renovation intentions.
In the grey questionnaire, the "household and apartment arch", information on the size, layout and residents of a living space can be provided.
The blue "operating sheet" must only be completed if business premises are operated in the building.
However, it should be noted that Gotzel pointed out that a completed questionnaire is not simultaneously an application for funding.
In addition, new construction measures are not eligible.
Schindler emphasized that the distribution of the subsidies works according to the "sighthound principle" - whoever comes first grinds first.
You can't "reserve" subsidies for later.
Finally, photos of the project "Ortskern II" were showned in order to "palatably" for those present.
British inventor sets himself a microchip to get into the house and car with a hand movement
A British inventor is the first in the country equipped with a microchip so he can open his front door, enter his office and even start his car with a hand movement.
Steven Northam, 33, had implanted the tiny chip between the thumb and index finger and now offers the service to companies and individuals who want to simplify their lives with technology through his company BioTeq.
He has partnered with Dr Geoff Watson, an advisory anaesthesiologist at Royal Hampshire County Hospital in Winchester, Hants, to make sure the implant procedure is carried out according to medical standards.
The technology resembles a cat and dogs implanted microchip and lasts only 30 seconds.
Mr Northam's company also wants to change the locks on houses and cars in such a way that they are compatible with the chip, all for a surcharge of £300 per castle.
The married father of one has now installed technology both at home and his BMW Z4, which starts when his hand is at the wheel.
White House advertising costs cost taxpayers thousands
The White House's decision to fly a naval helicopter to the South Lawn for an event highlighting American manufacturing last month costs taxpayers up to $24,40,000, according to the military's data.
The green-and-white Sikorsky VH-3D, known as Marine One, when the president is on board, was at the centerpiece of the July 17 event at the White House with American construction programs.
President Donald Trump, Vice President Mike Pence and senior White House officials took a tour of products from all 50 states.
The Connecticut-crafted helicopter was exhibited alongside a yacht from Maine, a fire truck from Wisconsin and a forklift from Mississippi.
The White House military office demanded the presence of the helicopter in a fragmentary order or FRAGO, barely 36 hours before the helicopter landed on the South Lawn, according to records of the Marine Helicopter Squadron One (HMX-1), the unit responsible for operating the helicopter.
"I just wanted you to be aware of this because it's such an unusual high-visibility event," the HMX-1 commander wrote on the eve of the event to his supervisor, the deputy naval commander for aviation.
The news indicated that the helicopter arrived on the South Lawn at around 7 a.m. on July 17 for the event at 3pm, and remained until the turf was cleared between 18:00 and 21:00 that evening.
The note adds that, if the departure from the White House were to be delayed, due to the restriction of service hours, a second crew would be needed for the return flight.
The original flight crew were at the helicopter during the event, while the unit's security personnel remained on the ground with the plane during time.
It was not clear whether a second flight crew was ultimately required.
The Hatch Act prohibits federal employees from using state property for political purposes.
Records indicate that HMX-1 asked if the event could violate the provision.
"One question we asked was whether it was a violation of the Hatch Act and we were informed that this was not the case," the commander wrote.
"Sounds like you're locked," the deputy commander replied.
The "Made in America" event has been dubbed an official event by the White House and would not have been covered by the Hatch Act.
But official events also have political overtones.
At the event, the president gave an impetus for health reforce reform that was underway in the Senate, and he promoted efforts to curb government regulations.
The use of the aircraft, known as Marine One or Air Force One, for politically beneficial purposes, is hardly a new phenomenon.
Presidents are required to use them for the trip.
But requesting their use only for photo surgery is unusual.
Trump carried out an investigation in February when the presidential plane provided taxi services against the backdrop of its first campaign rally in Melbourne, Florida, and his Twitter account is full of messages complaining abounting the use of the presidential plane by his predecessor.
"The White House is excited about the "Made In America" showing products from all 50 states made by American workers with American materials," White House deputy press secretary Lindsay Walters said.
HMX-1 crews frequently conduct training flights in the area around Washington and the South Lawn to depose the U.S. president at the White House with precise centimeters.
When Trump was at the G20 summit in Germany in early July, HMX-1 carried out 58 practice landings at the White House.
The flight for the "Made in America" event counteded on the flight crew's training needs for the month, an official said.
Helicopter units flew an additional 11 hours that day for "non-presidency support missions."
The White House said taxpayers took the burden for none of the other props presented at the event.
A spokesman for Sikorsky said the company had not been involved in exhibiting the helicopter on the South Lawn.
HMX-1 doesn't maintain budget records for the presidential flights, and it's difficult to determine the exact cost of the event.
However, according to the U.S. Department of Defense's hourly tariffs for the 2017 financial year, the DoD reimbursement rate for the VH-3D is $24,380 per flight hour.
Records of the square of the aircraft's flighting time as 30 minutes from the Marine Corps Air Facility's home base in Quantico, Virginia.
The White House argues that the actual cost of operating the helicopter is well below that rate.
"Setting the cost to taxpayers based on estimates for the reimbursement rates is highly misleading," Walters said.
“These estimates include personnel, maintenance, and many other hidden costs included in the annual funds.”
According to a RAND study from 2015, the reimbursement rates for DoD do not include personnel costs, but take into account the costs of fuel, maintenance and contracting.
At the time, former White Haus press secretary, Sean Spicer, defended the helicopter's deployment for the event.
"The idea is to present this week things that are made in America," Spicer said.
“And I know Sikorsky and Connecticut are very proud that they contribute to our national security, and I assume that hundreds, if not thousands, of people depend on it.
And I think, like most Americans, we are all proud of the helicopter and other military equipment that so many Americans have worked tirelessly for.
It's appropriate, of course, to highlight that."
It wasn't clear if Trump was interacting by the helicopter when he entered his tour of the South Lawn for the event, but he sat down in the driver's seat of a firefighting car.
At the White House, Trump spoke to Sikorsky's representatives and joked with the media about his own fleet of corporate products.
"I know Sikorsky very well," the president said, "I've got three of them."
TC BW Neuss facing tricky task
Front-runner of the Second Tennis Bundesliga will compete in the third-placement Suchsdorfer SV.
The TC Blau-Weiss Neuss competes tomorrow from 11 a.m. in Kiel at the third-tieght Suchsdorfer SV.
After five games still flawless record, the front-runner of the Second Tennis Bundesliga has retained the sense of reality.
Team boss Marius Zay certainly has respect for the opponent.
“It’s a compact team that’s always playing in pretty similar formation.
Especially in the back positions I find the team very good.”
Imports from the far north ensure class and stability at Suchsdorfer SV in particular.
“Their Danes and Finns can even point to appearances in the Davis Cup.
And they're also strong in doubles," says Zay facing Harri Heliovaara (Finland), Andre Göransson (Sweden), Sören and Esben Olesen, Martin Pedersen as well as Andreas Bjerrehus (all Denmark).
“As always, it’s going to be difficult for us.
You also need a good dose of luck.”
The Neuss team for the trip to the far north is practical.
His debut for blue-and-and-white could make Maxime Janvier.
If the Frenchman, who is currently still active at the Challenger tournament in the Italian Biella does not make it to the Bundesliga game, Zdenek Kolar (Czech Republic), who is playing at the tournament in Liberec, will make his first appearance this season.
The previously undefeated Chilean Hans Podlipnik-Castillo was yesterday with the Belarusian Andrei Vasilevski at the ATP tournament in Kitzbühel.
However, he should be in Kiel as well as Botic Van de Zandschulp, Niels Lootsma (both Netherlands) and the Belgian Julien Cagnina.
