How America can harm OMV
Donald Trump didn't seem really happy when he put his signature under Wednesday night's law to tighten sanctions against Russia.
"In its haste to enact the law," Congress had packaged some "unconstitutional regulations," he etched.
The reaction from Moscow, too, was as sharp as expected, and even Brussels frothed against the U.S. advance.
After all, the draft envisages punishing European companies to help Russians build gas pipelines into the EU.
Correspondingly, investors of the domestic OMV, which together with other European companies co-finances the construction of the Russian Baltic Sea pipeline Nord Stream 2.
The threatening backdrop is enormous.
Nevertheless, it is too early for an assessment of the consequences, according to the official statement of the oil company.
In fact, Donald Trump himself would still have to bring the sanctions to life by executive order.
Twelve weapons are up for his choice.
"The press" has looked at which the company could fend off and which could really hurt it.
Narco's Season 3 Trailer: Without Pablo Escobar, the Cali Cartel will take centre stage in Netflix's hit series
Despite Narco's second season that Pablo Escobar's killer reveals, Netflix's series is far from over.
Seasons three and four have already been confirmed by the streaming service, the first receiving its first trailer and showing the show's latest villains.
With the third season, the DEA has turned its attention to the world's richest organization of drug trafficking: the Cali cartel.
Led by four powerful godparents, they operate "like a Fortune 500 company" only with more government briberies and violent actions.
Gilberto Rodriguez Orejuela (Damian Alcazar) is the leader of the cartel, Miguel Rodriguez Orejuela (Francisco Denis) is her head, Pacho Herrera (Alberto Ammann) commands the Mexican connection, and Chepe Santacruz Londono (Pepe Rapazote) is located in New York.
In the newly released trailer, Pedro Pascal returns as Javier Peña, the guy who invites American and Colombian law enforcement agencies to support the war on drugs.
Other new actors include Matias Varela, Michael Stahl-David, Matt Whelan, Miguel Angel Silvestre, Kerry Bishe and Arturo Castro.
Narco's third season will begin on September 1.
Roll backwards: The city hires 46 kitchen aids
The city organizes the daycare lunch table itself again.
With fixed contact persons for the children and an assignment to the team.
In the city's daycare centers, the clock is turned back.
On 1 September, the city of Krefeld will take over the food supply in the municipal day-care facilities (kitas) on its own.
For Sonja Pommeranz, deputy head of youth welfare, this is good news: "We were all very happy".
Cooking continues to be outdoors: a caterer takes over this task and delivers freshly cooked.
The city runs 46 day-care centers in the Krefeld area, in 44 the children get a hot meal at lunchtime.
In the past year, 2800 children sat at the tables each, and the need is growing steadily.
"In the meantime, it's not only children who are looked after for 45 hours a week, lunch in daycare," explains Pommeranz.
"The offer is also valid in 35-hour care if children stay until 2 p.m."
From 1 September, 46 part-time workers will have kitchen service: they heat the delivered dishes, supplement them with lettuce, raw food and fruit, portion the meal individually, hand out the food, vacate, rinse and clean the kitchen.
"Employees are firmly assigned to a daycare center," says Pommeranz.
The size - there are two-grouped as well as seven-grouth facilities - decides on the scope of hours.
"The kitchen assistants are now firmly part of the daycare team," says Pommeranz with satisfaction.
"In this way, they are much more involved and it is easier for daycare management to implement pedagogical concepts."
Because that's also what it's all about: healthy diet and cooking lessons.
“The children should help prepare the food.”
The direct connection of the kitchen power to the team makes sense.
for the children as well.
“They have a well-known force in daycare, a person who is familiar to them, and not constantly changing people.”
This continuity is important for children.
In the future, the cleaning of the day-care rooms – which has also been the responsibility of the kitchen staff – will be carried out by special cleaning staff in the future.
In addition to the quality improvement of the kitchen performance, this variant - the separation of cleaning and kitchen service - also has the charm that it has proven to be the most economical in the cost comparison between self-direction and external allocation for the city, according to the press office.
And that's even though 46 part-time workers are being rehired.
"But these hours don't matter so much with around 750 people in the field of education," says Sonja Pommeranz.
Afghan democracy depends on women's political role, the UN says
Elections can only be representative if all members of the society, including women, play a role, the head of the U.N. mission in Afghanistan said on Wednesday.
Nicholas Haysom, acting head of the United Nations mission, said hundreds of women will run in council elections in the provinces. Three women are running for the role of vice-president and more than 30 percent of newly registered voters are women.
"The role of women cannot be overestimated," he said in a statement on Wednesday.
Elections can only be truly representative and credible if women fully participate and involved in all parts of the electoral process.
Afghan elections for the provincial council and presidential elections will take place on April 5.
The Taliban have issued threats against an electoral process that coincides with the transition from international combat operations to an advisory role.
This year, for the first time, elections will peacefully hand over power from one democratically elected government to the next.
Haysom said a strong role for women will reinforce Afghanistan's democratic success.
The Afghan Independent Electoral Commission reports that 1.2 million women were registered by a total registered 3.6 million voters.
Hike to the TV anniversary
On the occasion of its 110-year anniversary, the TV Kaiseringen invites you for a short hike followed by coffee and cake.
The hike starts on Sunday, July 9, at 1 p.m. in Frohnstetten at the water reservoir in Amerikastraße.
From there it goes to the airfield, to Natter and over the Waldhof back to the water reservoir.
The hike lasts about an hour and a half.
Afterwards there is coffee, cakes, cool drinks, string sausages and cheese wakes in Kaiseringen in the schoolhouse.
If you need a ride from Kaiseringen to Frohnstetten: Meeting point is in Kaiseringen at the school.
Men try to rob 13-year-old girl
Two young men, aged 19 and 23, are said to have attempted depriving a 13-year-old girl on Thursday.
According to information from the police, the men approached the 13-year-old, who was staying with friends on Kolping Street, at around 2:55 p.m. because they wanted to call the girl's mobile phone.
The 13-year-old, however, declined the request and went on with her friends.
As a result, one of the perpetrators tried to grab the girl's mobile phone and necklace, but failed.
Without prey, the duo fled.
The two young men could later be arrested by police.
Investigations are ongoing.
GMB concern over investments at McVitie factory in Glasgow
A union wants to make sure with managers of biscuit maker McVitie about the long-term commitment to production at its plant in Glasgow.
GMB Scotland said the company had remained silent about the future of investments at the Tollcross site.
More than 500 employees work at the plant, which manufactures Hobnobs and Rich Tea biscuits alongside other products.
McVitie's owner Pladis said he has invested more than £5 million in recent years.
He added that employees and union representatives would "be the first to know" if their activities were to change.
In a letter to the company's store manager, GMB called on McVitie's "to raise concerns that funding will not go forward for the modernisation of production lines."
GMB senior organizer Drew Duffy, said: "The fact that we can't gain clarity from the general-site manager about the business needs and investment plans for the future of Tollcross deeply affects our members.
Generations of families have worked here and the workers are heavily focused on the local community and the Greater Glasgow area, an important pillar for decent employment in an area with high unemployment and social disadvantage.
Therefore, under the perspective of jobs, wages and prosperity, and against the backdrop of Brexit, the rising cost of living and the chronic decline in production, the eastern end of Glasgow urgently needs a thriving production presence at McVitie."
A spokeswoman for Pladis said: "The branch manager at Tollcross only received a letter from GMB Scotland yesterday, and the company will respond directly to it as we appreciate the continued commitment among employees and union representatives.
We had a regular meeting two months ago with staff and GMB representatives at Tollcross, where some questions were discussed openly.
We discussed with the employees that, like all companies operating in an extremely competitive and uncertain economic climate, we are constantly reviewing our businesses - and this applies to all our businesses and locations - to remain competitive.
She added: "Over the past few years, we've invested more than £5 million in our Tollcross location, particularly in production lines and operational improvements.
We fully recognize Tollcross’ heritage as a manufacturing facility. Should our operations at the site change, employees and their union representatives will be the first to experience this.”
PSG warned Barcelona against Neymar three years ago?
La Liga's side may regret their interest in Marco Verratti
Paris Saint-Germain won't slump in their chase from Neymar after making an offer of £189 million to trigger his release clause, but did they warn Barcelona about the world record robbery three years ago?
The sensational approach has fuelled the transfer window in the summer as the Ligue 1 club is poised to break the world record by more than twice the previous mark, with Paul Pogba returning to Manchester United for £89m last season.
After cessation, Neymar becomes the world's most expensive player and the world's highest-paid professional athlete. PSG offers wages of more than £500,000 per week.
Barcelona are furious at the way Neymar forced his way out, although a source near the Catalan board insists the club has always done its best to take care of him since its arrival from Santos in 2013.
But was the football world warned of this prospect by the PSG president three years ago?
When Manchester City expressed interest in signing Blaise Matuidi in 2014, Nasser Al-Khelaifi had a stern warning for any club who wanted to sign one of PSG's best players.
"I'm not worried because they know that if they touch one of our players, then we're going to be in opposition to them, we're going to contact their players," Al-Khelaifi said.
"We respect all clubs.
But if they are trying to have one of our players signed with you, this will have consequences.”
Barcelona appear to have crossed that line, with the Spanish club expressing interest in PSG midfielder Marco Verratti earlier this summer.
PSG made it clear that they would not allow the international from Italy to leave under any circumstances, and Barcelona could feel the consequences of that interest by losing one of its price offers for Neymar.
Scooter overturned and damaged
In the night from Friday to Saturday, an unknown perpetrator intentionally knocked over a scooter parked in Königsfelder Straße in front of estate number 30 and thus caused property damage to the disguise of the vehicle of over 200 euros.
Schramberg police, phone 07422/ 27 01-0, asks for clues.
The police are looking for witnesses to an accident on the B 215 between Stolzenau and Leese.
Because a car met him in his lane, the driver of a VW Touran had to dodge and touched the guardrail.
On Thursday evening, the 34-year-old from Rehburg-Loccum with his VW at around 19.55 on the B 215 from Stolzenau in the direction of Leese.
In the area of Stolzenau’s bypass, a car approached him in his lane, so that he had to dodge to the right.
In doing so, the Touran rider touched the guardrail.
Property damage was caused to the passenger car.
The driver of the oncoming passenger car, a Mercedes Benz, had overtaken a truck and continued its journey without worrying about the traffic accident.
According to the accident victim, it may well be that the Mercedes driver had not even noticed the accident.
The police are now asking witnesses of the incident as well as the driver of the polluting vehicle to get in touch with the commissariat in Stolzenau on Tel. 05761 / 92060.
"Domspatzen report relieves Pope Brother Georg Ratzinger"
The Regensburg Domspatzen report on the abuse cases relieves Pope brother Georg Ratzinger.
This is what author and historian Michael Hesemann means.
The final report on the abuse scandal at the Regensburger Domspatzen continues to cause discussion.
Christian Weisnder, spokesperson of the reform movement "We are a Church", explained after publication to the German Press Agency that he expects an apology from the former Regensburg bishop and today's Cardinal Gerhard Ludwig Müller as well as the cathedral chapel master Georg Ratzinger, the 93-year-old brother of Emeritus Benedict XVI.
It would greatly serve the reputation of the Catholic Church if Müller and Ratzinger would express their deep regret over their own omissions or at that time then misjudgments of the events.
On the other hand, the Vatican newspaper "Osservatore Romano" criticized that in the coverage of violence at the cathedral sparrows, "two measures".
With regard to the attacks in Regensburg, no one doubts that these were "disgraceful and shameful acts" that must be punished and prevented in the future; however, the "degree of media manipulation" is striking, according to the author Lucetta Scaraffia, a historian and member of the national Italian bioethics committee.
"The habit of calling the Catholic Church the source of all evils is already part of everyday experience and prepares public opinion to consider it normal," Scaraffia writes.
Princess Gloria von Thurn und Taxis also said in an interview with Bayerischer Rundfunk: "You like to go to the church and that's a found food".
She also pointed out that in her youth "beatings had been a completely normal educational means" "to cope with naughty children as I was one."
She would find it unfair to apply today's standards to previous decades, because the world has changed.
In connection with the reports about the abuse cases, Pope brother Georg Ratzinger often appeared.
Quite unjustly, says author and historian Michael Hesemann, who wrought with Ratzinger the commemorative volume "My Brother the Pope" (translated into 12 languages).
"I have to protect Georg Ratzinger," says Hesemann, "because all this had nothing to do with him," he evaluates the results of the final report.
Exactly what does the 440-page report presented by Ulrich Weber, the diocese-in-chief of Regensburg.
A total of 547 Regensburg cathedral sparrows were sexually abused between 1945 and the beginning of the 1990s "with high plausibility" victims of attacks, 67 cathedral sparrows.
Georg Ratzinger, who worked in Regensburg from 1964 to 1994, said: "In summary, it can therefore be noted that in many cases forms of physical and psychological violence were used by Domkapellmeister R.
Especially with high emotionality, the punishment for the students could also be more severe.
Nevertheless, in contrast to numerous other defendants, it is noticeable that many victims appreciated the general humanity of R. and therefore in numerous cases even associate positive memories with him despite violence.
In an interview, Hesemann explains why, in his opinion, the final report on the abuse scandal of the Regensburg cathedral sparrow Georg Ratzinger relieves.
Mr. Hesemann, the 440-page report on the "Incidents of Violence on Personnel Orders at the Regensburger Domspatzen" gives a contradictory picture of Domkapellmeister Georg Ratzinger.
For one thing, many pupils highlight his "humanity."
On the other hand, he also appears as a choleric and perfectionist, which often became tangible when a Domspatz did not achieve a top performance musically.
What do you, as his biographer, evaluate this final report?
Michael Hesemann: If you make the effort and read the 440-page report from front to back, you have to ask yourself why the name Georg Ratzinger even appears in it.
Only eight pages are dedicated to him and he is clearly acquitted of any suspicion of accomplicity or co-knowledge in the horrific cases of sexual abuse.
Violent excesses at the pre-school also occurred outside its sphere of impact.
At best, he can be accused of learning about it too late or of not taking initial insinuations seriously enough.
The Domspatzengymnasium, on which he acted, was perceived by virtually all respondents as - so literally - "redemption", "heaven" and "better world".
Thus, the vast majority of his pupils Georg Ratzinger also issues a quite excellent testimony, describing him as "warm-hearted," "popular," "sincere," "competent" and "understanding," as an extremely good-natured teacher who regularly provided the boys with cakes, biscuits and candies and was "always surrounded by clusters of children."
Only a minority complains about his perfectionism and its high emotionality, which has sometimes led to outbursts of anger.
The fact that he not only popped with chairs and threw the keychain on the floor, but also handed out slaps and pulled too naughty boys by the hair or ears, he himself admitted years ago, deeply regretted and apologized for it.
However, in his rescue, it must be said that such methods of education were widespread in the period in question, the 1960s and 1970s, among parents as well as with teachers.
Do you also know such chastisement methods?
Of course, I think each of us that was born before 1968.
I, too, experienced this in a secular school, a state high school.
Compared with what this report is actually about, namely the appalling sexual abuse and the excesses of violence in the preschool, these are certainly trivials.
One wonders whether it has to do with his notoriety and his role as the brother of Benedict XVI that many mediates have just fallen on him.
Many who have never understood him are still eagerly working on the Bavarian pope.
The truth, however, is that the report exonerates Georg Ratzinger in every respect.
In connection with the report, there has been a great deal report in the media about the subject of abuse at the Regensburg Cathedral Spatzen.
Is Georg Ratzinger also accused of any involvement or sympathy with regard to sexual abuse in the final report?
Michael Hesemann: Clearly no.
None of the 67 victims of sexual assault claims to have confided themselves to the Domkapellmeister.
Not even the parents were inaugurated.
"I never dared to talk to my father or my mother about it," said one sufferer on behalf of the others, "I was very embarrassed."
The real problem in elucidating sexual abuse was and still is that most victims are silent out of shame.
If they didn’t even confide in parents and siblings, it’s certainly not a choirmaster who would have been a colleague of perpetrators.
How do the Domspatzen students rate Ratzinger's role with regard to the cases of abuse?
Michael Hesemann: A surveyed student put it this way: "With regard to sexual abuse ... I don't see ... any case in which he (Georg Ratzinger) would have had timely knowledge and could have stopped the respective perpetrator.
I also consider it really absurd to assume to assume that, of all places, boys have more hauntingly or more clearly or rather opened up than towards their parents, boarding school leaders, trust teachers, etc.
That you talk to him about sex ... unthinkable."
Thus, the report comes to the very clear conclusion: "The fact that pupils and/or their surroundings concerned entrusted themselves to him in advance could not be confirmed within the framework of the investigation".
As far as sexual abuse is concerned, Georg Ratzinger is to be acquitted by any sympathy.
Thus, he cannot be accused of not having prevented him or reported him.
Therefore, there is also no reason to associate its good name with these obnoxious crimes, as unfortunately happened in some cases.
In the media there was much to be read about "Violence excesses" at the Regensburger Domspatzen.
Former Domkapellmeister Georg Ratzinger is accused in the report of "looking away".
Does he have to live with this accusation?
Michael Hesemann: That’s the big problem that everything was mixed and blended into a huge melange of sexual abuse and excesses of violence and somewhere, in the middle, Georg Ratzinger.
Yet that, in turn, is an abuse of the report.
It separates neatly: between the preschool and the gymnasium of the cathedral sparrows, between sexual abuse and excesses of violence and, let’s say, other allegations.
In addition, the report deals with incidents since 1945, i.e. from four decades, in which Georg Ratzinger was not choir director of the Domspatzen.
All the terrible beating pedagogy, the cases in which boys of sadistic teachers were literally harassed and maltreated and suffered horribly, all occurred at the preschool in Etterzhausen and Pielenhofen, where really scandalous conditions must have prevailed.
But Domkapellmeister Georg Ratzinger had nothing to do with them, because he only saw the boys when they were already at the Domspatzengymnasium.
On which, as the vast majority of witnesses point out, the topic "pre-school" was taboo.
One was only happy to have escaped her and enjoyed the so much better treatment and, for many, even "paradisiacal" conditions.
What was different at Domspatzengymnasium?
Michael Hesemann: There was already discipline, but no sadistic violence.
And this also applies to Georg Ratzinger, in which the report concludes that "many victims appreciated the general humanity of R. and therefore in numerous cases even associate positive memories with him despite violence".
At most, the accusation of having looked away remains.
However, despite all efforts, the report was only able to define two periods in which it was told of the conditions in pre-school.
The first time in 1970/71, when he was just beginning to establish himself and did not want to hear anything about the events at a school where he did not work and was responsible, (so the witness), and then again around 1993.
Georg Ratzinger consistently closed his ears in front of the conditions?
Michael Hesemann: No, because already in 1998 he wrote a letter to the director of the Domspatzen Gymnasium, which the report even reproduces.
In it, he points out to this "that in the pre-school the beating penalty will continue to be practiced".
Even in view of the danger of negative press releases, he recommended urgent intervention.
This was, of course, at a time when legal clarity existed, when beating penalties had long been banned in Bavaria.
Before that, they occurred in a legal gray area, as each of us still knows.
It was also difficult for him to estimate where those affected might have exaggerated or dramatized and where the permissible limit had really been crossed.
But you can't say he'd always looked away.
That's just not true.
It was not until 1980 that the beating penalty at schools was officially abolished in Bavaria.
Princess Gloria von Thurn und Taxis claimed that it was unfair to apply today's educational standards to the time.
How do you see that?
Michael Hesemann: We historians are talking about the "trike of anachronism", i.e. the danger of assessing the past according to today's moral standards.
The princess is right.
But, of course, this does not apply to sexual abuse, which at all times was abhorrent at all times and also not for what has been vomited in pre-schools in pre-schools of innocent children's souls, this goes far beyond what was customary at the time.
This is all quite terrible and I'm glad it's finally being worked up.
But at the same time I have to protect Georg Ratzinger, because all this had nothing to do with him.
His means of disciplination and punishment, i.e. slapping and pulling by the hair and ears, were widespread in the 1970s.
When corporal punishment was then banned, he also strictly adhered to it.
What picture of his time in Regensburg did Georg Ratzinger reproduce in the conversations with you for the book "My Brother the Pope"?
Was the subject of beating punishment also discussed?
Michael Hesemann: Well, the book is about the life of his brother Joseph, i.e. Pope Benedict XVI, told from his perspective that the cathedral sparrows only played a supporting role.
Nevertheless, I do not hide the fact that he led a strict regiment there.
But this must also be said: Without this perfectionism, without the demanding absolute discipline, it would hardly have been possible for him to form a world-class institution out of the hitherto rather nationally well-known Regensburg cathedral sparrows, true cultural ambassadors of Europe and its musical tradition, which went on tour twice in the USA (1983 and 1987) and Japan (1988 and 1991).
With biscuits and sweets alone, you don’t turn unruly boys into no great singers and are faced with every success, in whatever field, but always discipline, passion and self-overcoming.
As what kind of person have you experienced Georg Ratzinger during the conversations about her book?
Michael Hesemann: By working on the book and beyond, I got to know Georg Ratzinger really well and experienced again and again as a wonderful, humorous, rational, deeply devout and good-hearted person, of whom you can not imagine that he could hurt even a fly.
I regularly notice how many former cathedral sparrows today, 23 years after his retirementation, maintain regular contact with him, visit him, read to him - he is almost blind - and treat him like a member of their family.
Therefore, those who do not forgive him to this date that he was once strict to them are clearly in the minority.
Most Domspatzen today are grateful to him for enriching their lives and bringing them the world of music.
And that he was always a good, paternal teacher - a stern father, of sure, that may be - but was also a lovable respective person, to whom they still look up today and whom they downright adore.
And please, once again very clearly: all he is accused of are methods of education, which were commonplace at that time.
We can be glad that we have long since overcome this time.
But it's supremely unrighteous to make a man just because he's prominent and has an even more prominent brother, a scapegoat.
That's where the evil intention behind it is all too obvious.
Because the report, which has just been published, relieves him.
Now the decency would require a 93-year-old man, who has earned great merits and without which outside of Bavaria and perhaps Germany probably no one would know the Regensburg cathedral sparrows, finally in peace.
